// Project data, extracted from the original index.html.
// Single source of truth for the tile grid and the write up overlay.
const PROJECTS=[{"title": "Wikipedia Edit Stream: Streaming Algorithms with Their Error Measured Live", "meta": "Streaming Algorithms &middot; Live Data &middot; Oct 2026", "badge": "HyperLogLog 986.9 vs exact 983 (+0.4%) &middot; 0 Space-Saving violations &middot; live SSE, about 30 events/s", "tags": ["JavaScript", "Server Sent Events", "HyperLogLog", "Count-Min Sketch", "Space-Saving", "MurmurHash3", "Stream Processing", "Canvas", "node:test"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">9,909 real edits in a 297 second run (Oct 8 2026) &nbsp;|&nbsp; HyperLogLog 986.9 vs 983 exact distinct editors (+0.4%, one sigma bound 1.6%) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Space-Saving: 0 bound violations &nbsp;|&nbsp; Count-Min: 12 of 12 within its error bound &nbsp;|&nbsp; 24 tests</div>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/live_wiki.jpg\" alt=\"The edit river with a dot for each real edit, in a row for its wiki, blue for people and orange for bots, drifting left as it ages\"><figcaption>The edit river on October 8, 2026. Each dot is one real edit in a row for its wiki, blue for people and orange for bots, drifting left as it ages; rings are new pages.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>Streaming sketches are usually taught with their error bounds and then trusted. HyperLogLog promises a distinct count within about 1.6% from 4 KB of state. Count-Min promises it never undercounts, and rarely overcounts by more than a small, known share of all events (&epsilon;N, with probability 1 &minus; &delta;). Space-Saving promises that every page popular enough to matter (above N/k edits, for k counters) is in its table, with a known error on every count. I wanted to watch those promises hold on real traffic instead of taking them on faith, so this dashboard runs each sketch beside an exact counter on the same events and plots the difference as it happens.</p>\n<h4>The Data</h4>\n<p>Wikimedia publishes every change to every one of its wikis as a server sent events stream. The browser connects to it directly, with no server of mine in between. In my runs it carried 26 to 36 events a second across about 100 wikis, roughly a quarter of them from bots. If the connection drops, the browser reconnects with Last-Event-ID, which carries the stream's Kafka offsets, so it resumes where it stopped; events are deduplicated on their id in case the resume overlaps.</p>\n<h4>The Sketches</h4>\n<p><strong>HyperLogLog</strong> hashes each editor name with MurmurHash3, checked against published test vectors and SMHasher's verification value, into 4,096 one byte registers. While there are fewer than about 10,000 distinct editors it estimates from the share of empty registers (linear counting), and above that it switches to the harmonic mean estimator from the original paper. <strong>Count-Min</strong> counts events per wiki in 4 rows of 1,024 columns with the standard update, so the textbook bound applies as stated. <strong>Space-Saving</strong> tracks the most edited pages with 200 counters, each carrying its own error term, so every row shows a guaranteed lower bound and an upper bound, with the exact count drawn as a tick between them.</p>\n<h4>Measured on Real Data</h4>\n<p>A node script ran the same sketch code against the live stream for 297 seconds on October 8, 2026: 9,909 events at 33.3 a second. HyperLogLog estimated 986.9 distinct editors against an exact 983, an error of +0.4% against a one sigma bound of 1.6%. Two shorter runs gave &minus;0.3% and &minus;1.2%. Space-Saving showed no violations of its bounds, and Count-Min kept all 12 of the busiest wikis within &epsilon;N with no undercounts. The run logs are in the repository next to the code.</p>\n<h4>Engineering Decisions</h4>\n<p><strong>Binning by event time.</strong> Binning throughput by arrival flagged delivery clumps as bursts: 3 and 2 false bursts in two of the runs, against none when the same rule ran on event time bins. The chart bins by event time and closes a second 2 seconds after the newest event passes it, and the footer still reports what the arrival rule would have flagged.</p>\n<p><strong>A rate bug in the feed monitor.</strong> The status pill read 23 messages a second while a separate counter saw 33, because events arriving less than a millisecond apart carried almost no weight in the smoothed rate. Pooling arrivals into 50 ms windows fixed it, in the shared code all four dashboards use.</p>\n<p><strong>Privacy.</strong> Editor names and IP addresses are hashed for counting and never displayed, and titles of user pages are hidden.</p>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Count-Min barely collides here.</strong> About 100 wikis in 1,024 columns rarely share a cell, so the bound is shown but hardly stressed. The card says so beside the number.</li>\n  <li><strong>A short visit exercises only the small range path of HyperLogLog.</strong> It switches to the raw estimator at about 10,240 distinct editors, which takes a long session to reach.</li>\n  <li><strong>Exact counting is affordable at this rate.</strong> That is what makes the comparison possible. The sketches earn their keep at volumes where it is not.</li>\n  <li><strong>The resume path is lightly tested.</strong> Wikimedia dropped the stream once during browser testing, and the page reconnected with Last-Event-ID without a duplicate, but no logged run has exercised it.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>Wikipedia and its sister sites publish every edit as it happens, as a live public feed. In my runs it carried 26 to 36 edits a second across about 100 wikis. I built a web page that connects to that feed straight from the browser and counts the edits two ways at once: exactly, and with three memory saving shortcuts called sketches.</p>\n<p>A sketch keeps a small summary instead of every item and gives up a little accuracy in return. Each one comes with a mathematical promise about how far off it can be, and they are usually taught with that promise and then trusted. I wanted to watch the promises hold on real traffic, so the page runs each sketch next to an exact count of the same edits and plots the gap as it happens.</p>\n<h4>The Three Shortcuts</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Counting distinct editors.</strong> HyperLogLog estimates how many different people and bots have edited, from a tiny fixed amount of memory. Its typical error is about 1.6%.</li>\n<li><strong>Edits per wiki.</strong> Count-Min never undercounts, and rarely overcounts by more than a small, known share of all edits.</li>\n<li><strong>The most edited pages.</strong> Space-Saving keeps a short list of the busiest pages, with a guaranteed lowest and highest possible count for each one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Editor names and IP addresses are scrambled before counting and never shown.</p>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<p>In one logged run of 297 seconds on October 8, 2026, HyperLogLog estimated 986.9 distinct editors against an exact count of 983, off by 0.4%. Two shorter runs came in slightly low, by 0.3% and 1.2%. In the logged run, Space-Saving never broke its bounds, and Count-Min kept all 12 of the busiest wikis inside its promised error, with no undercounts.</p>\n<p>I also found that counting edits by when they arrived made the page report bursts of activity that were only delivery clumps in the feed. Counting them by when the edit was made removed those false bursts, so the chart uses that.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Count-Min is barely stressed.</strong> About 100 wikis spread over its 1,024 slots rarely land in the same one, so its promise is shown but hardly tested.</li>\n<li><strong>A short visit tests only one mode of HyperLogLog.</strong> It switches methods at about 10,240 distinct editors, which takes a long session to reach.</li>\n<li><strong>Exact counting is cheap at this rate.</strong> That is what makes the comparison possible. The shortcuts pay off at volumes where exact counting is not affordable.</li>\n<li><strong>Reconnecting is lightly tested.</strong> The feed dropped once during testing and the page picked up where it left off without a duplicate, but no logged run covers it.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Do streaming sketches keep their published error bounds on real traffic?", "d": "HyperLogLog, Count-Min and Space-Saving are usually taught with their bounds and then trusted. The dashboard runs each beside an exact counter on the same events and plots the difference live."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Wikimedia recent changes, a server sent events stream", "d": "Every change to every Wikimedia wiki, read by the browser with no server in between. 26 to 36 events a second across about 100 wikis, roughly a quarter from bots."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Did the connection drop?", "d": "The stream can disconnect mid session.", "yes": "The browser reconnects with Last-Event-ID, which carries the stream's Kafka offsets, and resumes where it stopped. Events are deduplicated on id in case the resume overlaps.", "no": "Events flow straight on to the counters."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Privacy handling", "d": "Editor names and IP addresses are hashed for counting and never displayed, and titles of user pages are hidden."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Three sketches, each beside an exact counter", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "HyperLogLog on editor names", "d": "MurmurHash3, checked against published test vectors and SMHasher's verification value, into 4,096 one byte registers (4 KB of state). The promise: a distinct count within about 1.6%."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Fewer than about 10,000 distinct editors so far?", "yes": "Estimate from the share of empty registers (linear counting).", "no": "Switch to the harmonic mean estimator from the original paper."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Count-Min on events per wiki", "d": "4 rows of 1,024 columns with the standard update, so the textbook bound applies: never undercounts, rarely overcounts by more than &epsilon;N, with probability 1 &minus; &delta;."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Space-Saving on the most edited pages", "d": "200 counters, each carrying its own error term, so every row shows a guaranteed lower and upper bound, with the exact count drawn as a tick between them."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Logged run: 297 seconds on October 8, 2026", "d": "A node script ran the same sketch code against the live stream: 9,909 events at 33.3 a second. The run logs sit in the repository next to the code, with 24 tests."}, {"k": "result", "t": "HyperLogLog: 986.9 estimated against 983 exact, +0.4%", "d": "Against a one sigma bound of 1.6%, in that single 297 second run. Two shorter runs gave &minus;0.3% and &minus;1.2%."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does Space-Saving break its bounds?", "d": "Each tracked page's exact count is compared with its lower and upper bound.", "yes": "The guarantee would be broken on real traffic.", "no": "0 bound violations in the logged run."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do the busiest wikis stay within &epsilon;N?", "d": "Count-Min's estimate for each of the 12 busiest wikis against its exact count.", "yes": "All 12 of 12 within &epsilon;N, with no undercounts, in the logged run.", "no": "An undercount or an overshoot past &epsilon;N would break the textbook bound."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does binning throughput by arrival time flag false bursts?", "d": "The same burst rule run on arrival time bins and on event time bins.", "yes": "It flagged delivery clumps as bursts: 3 and 2 false bursts in two runs, against none on event time. The chart bins by event time, closing a second 2 seconds after the newest event passes it.", "no": "Either binning would do."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Rate bug fixed in the shared feed monitor", "d": "The status pill read 23 messages a second while a separate counter saw 33, because events arriving under a millisecond apart barely moved the smoothed rate. Pooling arrivals into 50 ms windows fixed it for all four dashboards."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Count-Min barely collides here", "d": "About 100 wikis in 1,024 columns rarely share a cell, so its bound is shown but hardly stressed. The card says so beside the number."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "A short visit uses only HyperLogLog's small range path", "d": "It switches to the raw estimator at about 10,240 distinct editors, which takes a long session to reach."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Exact counting is affordable at this rate", "d": "That is what makes the comparison possible. The sketches earn their keep at volumes where it is not."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The resume path is lightly tested", "d": "The stream dropped once during browser testing and the page reconnected without a duplicate, but no logged run has exercised it."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "On one logged run of real edits, all three sketches stayed inside their published bounds. Count-Min barely collides at this volume, and a short session does not reach HyperLogLog's large range estimator, so neither is stressed."}]}, {"title": "Order Book Microstructure: A Checksum Verified Live Book with Order Flow Analytics", "meta": "Market Microstructure &middot; Live Data &middot; Oct 2026", "badge": "46,892 of 46,892 checksums verified &middot; OFI R&sup2; 0.54 at 1 s &middot; live WebSocket, depth 100", "tags": ["JavaScript", "WebSocket", "Order Book", "CRC32", "Order Flow Imbalance", "OLS Regression", "Realized Volatility", "Canvas Heatmap", "node:test"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">330 second run on BTC/USD (Oct 8 2026): 46,892 of 46,892 book checksums verified, 0 resyncs &nbsp;|&nbsp; OFI regression at 1 s: &beta; = 1.10 USD per BTC, R&sup2; = 0.54, n = 300 &nbsp;|&nbsp; lag p50 89 ms &nbsp;|&nbsp; 16 tests</div>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/live_book.jpg\" alt=\"Liquidity heatmap of the BTC/USD order book with the best bid and ask lines and trades drawn as bubbles\"><figcaption>The BTC/USD book on October 8, 2026. Darker blue means more bitcoin waiting at that price; the best bid and ask run across it and every trade is a bubble sized by amount.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>Every trading system starts from a local copy of the order book, built from a snapshot and a stream of deltas, and the classic failure is silent drift. Miss or misapply one update and every number computed from the book is quietly wrong. Kraken publishes a checksum (CRC32) of the top of its book with every update, so drift does not have to be silent. This dashboard rebuilds the book in the browser, verifies it against that checksum on every message, resyncs automatically on a mismatch, and only then computes microstructure statistics from it.</p>\n<h4>The Book</h4>\n<p>The page subscribes to Kraken's public WebSocket at a depth of 100 levels and keeps each side as a sorted array with binary search updates. After each update it truncates both sides back to the subscribed depth, because the exchange stops sending changes for levels that fall out of range. Price and size precision are read from the exchange's instrument channel rather than hardcoded. The checksum is computed over formatted strings: printing 0.0157 instead of 0.01570000 changes the string and fails every check.</p>\n<h4>What It Computes</h4>\n<p><strong>Order flow imbalance</strong> measures whether buyers or sellers are pressing harder at the best prices. Following Cont, Kukanov and Stoikov (2014), each change at the top of the book adds or subtracts size, summed per second, and the page fits a straight line between that and the price change over the same second (ordinary least squares), live, with the slope's standard error. <strong>Microprice</strong> weights the midpoint by how much size waits on each side. <strong>Realized volatility</strong> is computed from mid returns at both 1 second and 10 second sampling over the same five minutes, annualized. A liquidity heatmap shows resting size by price over the last four minutes with every trade drawn over it, painted from raw pixel buffers so it keeps up with the feed.</p>\n<h4>Measured on Real Data</h4>\n<p>A node script ran the same book code against the live feed for 330 seconds on October 8, 2026: 47,595 messages, of which 46,891 were book updates, about 142 a second. All 46,892 checksums (the snapshot and every update) matched, with no resyncs. The OFI regression over the last 300 seconds gave a slope of 1.10 USD per BTC of net flow, standard error 0.059, with R&sup2; = 0.54. An earlier 330 second run verified 44,534 of 44,534, and a run switching between BTC, ETH and SOL verified 10,214 of 10,214. To exercise the resync path, a deliberately corrupted update failed its checksum and the book recovered from a fresh snapshot; that case is also in the test suite.</p>\n<h4>A Result That Went the Other Way</h4>\n<p>Microstructure noise usually makes finely sampled realized volatility read higher than coarsely sampled. In both long runs it went the other way: 27.0% annualized at 1 second against 31.9% at 10 seconds in the final run. The 10 second figure rests on only 30 returns, so its sampling error is about 13%, and the page compares the gap with that error instead of always calling it noise. It labels the gap as within sampling error, as noise, or as prices moving in short runs, depending on what the numbers support.</p>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>One venue, 100 levels.</strong> The book is Kraken's alone, and liquidity beyond the subscribed depth is invisible.</li>\n  <li><strong>Taker side as reported.</strong> Trade direction is the exchange's label, not inferred.</li>\n  <li><strong>Clock offset in latency.</strong> Lag is arrival time minus the exchange timestamp, so it includes any offset between the two clocks.</li>\n  <li><strong>Background tabs.</strong> Browsers throttle timers in hidden tabs, so heatmap columns sampled while the tab is hidden are sparse.</li>\n  <li><strong>Not investment advice.</strong> This is analytics on public market data.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>An order book is an exchange's running list of everyone waiting to buy or sell, and at what price. I built a web page that keeps a live copy of the bitcoin to US dollar order book on Kraken, a crypto exchange, from its public feed. The page starts from a full snapshot of the book and then applies each small change the exchange sends, more than a hundred a second.</p>\n<p>The classic way this goes wrong is quiet drift: miss or misapply one change, and every number worked out from the copy is wrong with nothing to warn you. Kraken sends a short fingerprint of the top of its book (a checksum) with every update. After every change, my page computes the same fingerprint from its own copy and compares the two. If they differ, it throws the copy away and starts again from a fresh snapshot.</p>\n<h4>What It Calculates</h4>\n<p>Once the copy is known to be right, the page works out a few standard trading statistics as the data arrives:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Order flow imbalance:</strong> whether buyers or sellers are pressing harder at the best prices each second, and how closely that lines up with the price move in the same second.</li>\n<li><strong>Microprice:</strong> the midpoint between the best buying and selling prices, adjusted for how much is waiting on each side.</li>\n<li><strong>Volatility:</strong> how much the price is jumping around, measured in 1 second and 10 second steps.</li>\n<li><strong>A heat map</strong> of how much bitcoin is waiting at each price over the last four minutes, with every trade drawn on top.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<p>In one 330 second run on October 8, 2026, all 46,892 fingerprints matched, the snapshot and every update, so the page never had to start over. Two other runs also matched every time. To check the recovery, I fed it a deliberately broken update: the check caught it and the book rebuilt itself from a fresh snapshot.</p>\n<p>In that same run, buying and selling pressure lined up with the price move with a fit score of 0.54, where 1 would mean it accounted for every move.</p>\n<p>One result went against the usual pattern. Volatility measured in short steps normally reads higher than in long steps, because of small jitters in the quoted price. In both long runs it read lower. The 10 second figure rests on only 30 price changes, though, so the page compares the gap with its own sampling error before labelling it.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One exchange, 100 price levels.</strong> The book is Kraken's alone, and anything deeper than the levels it subscribes to is invisible.</li>\n<li><strong>Trade direction is the exchange's label,</strong> not something the page works out itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Delay figures include clock differences</strong> between the exchange and the viewer's computer.</li>\n<li><strong>It is not investment advice.</strong> It is analysis of public market data.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can a browser keep a verified live order book and compute statistics from it?", "d": "A local book built from a snapshot plus deltas fails by silent drift: one missed update and every number is quietly wrong. Kraken publishes a CRC32 checksum of its top of book with every update, so drift can be caught."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Kraken public WebSocket, BTC/USD at depth 100", "d": "A snapshot followed by a stream of deltas. Price and size precision come from the exchange's instrument channel rather than being hardcoded."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Maintain the book as two sorted arrays", "d": "Binary search updates on each side, truncated back to the subscribed depth after every update, because the exchange stops sending changes for levels that fall out of range."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the local CRC32 match the exchange checksum?", "d": "Run on every message. The checksum is over formatted strings, so printing 0.0157 instead of 0.01570000 would fail every check.", "yes": "The book is trusted and statistics are computed from it.", "no": "The page resyncs automatically from a fresh snapshot."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Statistics computed on the verified book", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Order flow imbalance (Cont, Kukanov and Stoikov 2014)", "d": "Size changes at the best prices summed per second, then an ordinary least squares line fitted live against the same second's price change, with the slope's standard error."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Microprice", "d": "The midpoint weighted by how much size waits on each side."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Realized volatility at 1 s and 10 s", "d": "From mid returns at both samplings over the same five minutes, annualized."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Liquidity heatmap", "d": "Resting size by price over the last four minutes with every trade drawn over it, painted from raw pixel buffers so it keeps up with the feed."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Logged run: 330 seconds on October 8, 2026", "d": "A node script ran the same book code against the live feed: 47,595 messages, 46,891 of them book updates, about 142 a second."}, {"k": "result", "t": "46,892 of 46,892 checksums verified, 0 resyncs", "d": "The snapshot and every update in that run. An earlier 330 second run verified 44,534 of 44,534, and a run switching between BTC, ETH and SOL verified 10,214 of 10,214."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the resync path recover from a corrupted update?", "d": "A deliberately corrupted update is fed in. The same case is one of the 16 tests.", "yes": "It failed its checksum and the book recovered from a fresh snapshot.", "no": "The book would drift silently from that update on."}, {"k": "result", "t": "OFI at 1 s: slope 1.10 USD per BTC, R&sup2; = 0.54", "d": "Over the last 300 seconds of the logged run (n = 300), standard error 0.059."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does 1 second volatility read higher than 10 second, as noise predicts?", "d": "Microstructure noise usually makes finely sampled realized volatility read higher than coarsely sampled.", "yes": "The usual pattern would hold.", "no": "In both long runs it went the other way: 27.0% annualized at 1 second against 31.9% at 10 seconds in the final run."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The 10 second figure rests on only 30 returns", "d": "Its sampling error is about 13%. The page compares the gap with that error and labels it as within sampling error, as noise, or as prices moving in short runs."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Feed lag p50 of 89 ms", "d": "Lag is arrival time minus the exchange timestamp, measured in the logged run."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Clock offset in latency", "d": "Because lag subtracts the exchange timestamp, it includes any offset between the two clocks."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "One venue, 100 levels", "d": "The book is Kraken's alone, and liquidity beyond the subscribed depth is invisible."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Taker side as reported", "d": "Trade direction is the exchange's label, not inferred."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Background tabs", "d": "Browsers throttle timers in hidden tabs, so heatmap columns sampled while the tab is hidden are sparse."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "Across three logged runs every checksum matched, so the statistics sit on a book known to be correct. Each figure comes from short runs on one venue, and none of it is investment advice."}]}, {"title": "Live Earthquake Statistics: Gutenberg-Richter, Omori, and a Catalog That Revises Itself", "meta": "Statistical Seismology &middot; Live Data &middot; Oct 2026", "badge": "California b = 0.73 [0.66, 0.85] &middot; 7 catalog revisions caught in 45 min &middot; live USGS polling", "tags": ["JavaScript", "Statistical Seismology", "Maximum Likelihood", "Bootstrap", "Gutenberg-Richter", "Omori-Utsu", "Revision Tracking", "d3-geo", "node:test"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">1,949 events in the week feed (USGS, Oct 8 2026) &nbsp;|&nbsp; California b = 0.73 &plusmn; 0.03, bootstrap [0.66, 0.85] &nbsp;|&nbsp; 45 min of polling: 9 new events, 7 revisions, mean magnitude change 0.19 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 27 tests</div>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/live_quakes.jpg\" alt=\"World map of the week's earthquakes colored by depth, with ripples around the last hour's events\"><figcaption>The week's earthquakes on October 8, 2026, colored by depth, with rings rippling out from the last hour's events and the latest M 2.5 or larger called out.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>An earthquake catalog keeps changing after the fact. Automatic solutions get reviewed, magnitudes move, events merge under a new id, and a few are deleted. Every statistic computed from a live feed inherits those changes, so this dashboard does two jobs at once: it computes the classic statistics of seismology from the past week of USGS data, and it keeps a log of everything the catalog revised while the page was open.</p>\n<h4>The Statistics</h4>\n<p><strong>How much rarer is each bigger quake?</strong> The Gutenberg-Richter law says the number of earthquakes drops tenfold for every unit of magnitude when the b-value is 1, which is close to what most regions show. The page finds the magnitude above which the catalog is complete (maximum curvature plus the 0.2 correction of Woessner and Wiemer, 2005), estimates b by maximum likelihood (Aki 1965, with Utsu's binning correction), and reports both the Shi and Bolt (1982) uncertainty and a bootstrap interval from 200 resamples that reestimates the completeness magnitude each time.</p>\n<p><strong>How fast do aftershocks fade?</strong> For the largest sequence in the window, aftershocks are gathered with Gardner-Knopoff distance and time windows and fitted to the Omori-Utsu law by maximum likelihood (Ogata 1983), with a profile likelihood range on the decay exponent p. With fewer than 30 aftershocks the page draws the sequence without a fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Unusual hours.</strong> Each hour's count is compared with a Poisson rate from the previous day, and hours with a tail probability below 0.001 are flagged.</p>\n<h4>Measured on Real Data</h4>\n<p>The week feed fetched at 19:09 UTC on October 8, 2026 held 1,949 events, of which 1,905 were earthquakes; the rest were explosions, quarry blasts and ice quakes, which are left out of every statistic. For California, b came out at 0.73 &plusmn; 0.03 with a bootstrap interval of [0.66, 0.85] above a completeness magnitude of 0.9, and Alaska gave 0.72 [0.61, 0.84]. Across all events pooled together, b was 0.34. That number says more about mixing magnitude scales and networks than about the Earth, and the page says so beside it. No sequence that week reached 30 aftershocks, so there was no Omori fit on live data; an injected synthetic sequence of 200 aftershocks with p = 1.1 came back at p = 1.08.</p>\n<p>Polling for 45 minutes from 19:19 UTC recorded 9 new events and 7 revisions. Six magnitudes changed, by 0.19 on average, including an Alaska event that moved from 1.6 to 1.9. One event changed its preferred id from the Alaska network's to the USGS national one, and the merge logs that as a revision instead of a deletion plus a new event.</p>\n<h4>A Test That Failed for the Right Reason</h4>\n<p>My first b-value test failed. Its synthetic catalog had a true b of 1, and the 95% bootstrap interval came out [1.015, 1.070], missing it. A 95% interval should miss about one time in twenty, and across 300 synthetic catalogs this one covered the true value 93.7% of the time. The single catalog test now uses a different seed, and a separate test requires at least 16 of 20 catalogs to cover the true value.</p>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Mixed magnitude scales.</strong> The feed combines ml, md, mb and moment magnitudes from many networks, so pooled b-values are not physical estimates.</li>\n  <li><strong>One week is short.</strong> Aftershock fits are only possible after a sizeable mainshock, which most weeks do not have inside a region.</li>\n  <li><strong>Deletions are untested on live data.</strong> None occurred in 55 minutes of polling, so the deletion logic has only been exercised on synthetic feeds.</li>\n  <li><strong>Coarse coastlines.</strong> The 1:110 million land outlines are rough for Hawaii and Puerto Rico.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>A live dashboard of every earthquake the US Geological Survey (USGS) recorded in the past week. It does two jobs. It computes the classic statistics of earthquake science from that week of data, and it keeps a log of every change the catalog makes to itself while the page is open. Earthquake catalogs keep changing after the fact: automatic readings get reviewed, magnitudes move, two records merge under a new id, and a few are deleted. Every number computed from the feed inherits those changes.</p>\n<h4>The Questions It Asks</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>How much rarer is each bigger quake?</strong> The Gutenberg-Richter law describes this with a number called b. When b is 1, each step up in magnitude brings ten times fewer quakes, which is close to what most regions show. The page estimates b, and gives a likely range for it by recomputing it on many resampled copies of the data.</li>\n<li><strong>How fast do aftershocks fade?</strong> For the biggest sequence of the week, the page fits the standard decay curve, but only when there are at least 30 aftershocks.</li>\n<li><strong>Is this hour unusual?</strong> Each hour's count is compared with the rate over the previous day, and very unlikely hours are flagged.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<p>On October 8, 2026, California's b came out at 0.73, with a likely range of 0.66 to 0.85. Pooling every region together gave 0.34, a number that says more about mixing different measuring scales and networks than about the Earth, and the page says so beside it. No sequence that week reached 30 aftershocks, so the aftershock fit was only checked on a made up test sequence, where it recovered the decay rate closely.</p>\n<p>Over 45 minutes of watching, the catalog made 7 revisions to events it already held. One Alaska quake moved from magnitude 1.6 to 1.9. Another changed its id from the Alaska network's to the national one, and the page logs that as a revision rather than a deletion plus a new event.</p>\n<h4>A Test That Failed for the Right Reason</h4>\n<p>My first test of the b estimate failed: on a made up catalog with a true b of 1, the 95% range missed the true value. A 95% range should miss about one time in twenty, and across hundreds of made up catalogs the method covered the true value 93.7% of the time. The single catalog test now uses a different random seed, and a separate test checks many catalogs at once.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mixed measuring scales.</strong> The feed combines several kinds of magnitude from many networks, so pooled b values are not physical estimates.</li>\n<li><strong>One week is short.</strong> An aftershock fit needs a sizeable main quake, which most weeks do not have inside one region.</li>\n<li><strong>Deletions are untested on live data.</strong> None happened while I was polling, so that part has only been tested on made up feeds.</li>\n<li><strong>Coarse coastlines.</strong> The map outlines are rough for Hawaii and Puerto Rico.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "What do classic seismology statistics show this week, and how does the catalog change?", "d": "Automatic solutions get reviewed, magnitudes move, events merge under a new id, and a few are deleted. Every statistic computed from a live feed inherits those changes."}, {"k": "data", "t": "USGS week feed, fetched 19:09 UTC on October 8, 2026", "d": "1,949 events, of which 1,905 were earthquakes."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the event an earthquake?", "yes": "It enters the statistics.", "no": "Explosions, quarry blasts and ice quakes are left out of every statistic."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Completeness magnitude", "d": "Maximum curvature plus the 0.2 correction of Woessner and Wiemer (2005) finds the magnitude above which the catalog is complete."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Gutenberg-Richter b-value by maximum likelihood", "d": "Aki (1965) with Utsu's binning correction, with the Shi and Bolt (1982) uncertainty and a 200 resample bootstrap that reestimates completeness each time. Counts drop tenfold per magnitude unit when b is 1."}, {"k": "split", "t": "b-value results on the October 8 feed", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "California: b = 0.73 &plusmn; 0.03", "d": "Bootstrap interval [0.66, 0.85] above a completeness magnitude of 0.9."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Alaska: b = 0.72", "d": "Bootstrap interval [0.61, 0.84]."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "All events pooled: b = 0.34", "d": "Across every region together."}]]}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Pooled b reflects mixed magnitude scales, not the Earth", "d": "The feed combines ml, md, mb and moment magnitudes from many networks, so pooled b-values are not physical estimates. The page says so beside the 0.34."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Gather aftershocks with Gardner-Knopoff windows", "d": "For the largest sequence in the window, aftershocks are collected by distance and time windows."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the sequence have at least 30 aftershocks?", "yes": "Fit the Omori-Utsu law by maximum likelihood (Ogata 1983), with a profile likelihood range on the decay exponent p.", "no": "Draw the sequence without a fit. No sequence reached 30 that week, so there was no Omori fit on live data."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Injected synthetic sequence: p = 1.1 came back as 1.08", "d": "200 synthetic aftershocks. This is the only Omori fit, since the live week had no qualifying sequence."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is an hour's count improbable under the previous day's rate?", "d": "Each hour is compared with a Poisson rate from the previous day.", "yes": "Tail probability below 0.001: the hour is flagged.", "no": "The hour is not flagged."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Revision log while the page is open", "d": "Polling records new events, revisions, id changes and deletions in the catalog."}, {"k": "result", "t": "45 minutes of polling: 9 new events, 7 revisions", "d": "From 19:19 UTC. Six magnitudes changed, by 0.19 on average, including an Alaska event that moved from 1.6 to 1.9."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Id changes merged as revisions", "d": "One event changed its preferred id from the Alaska network's to the USGS national one. The merge logs it as a revision instead of a deletion plus a new event."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the 95% bootstrap interval cover the true b?", "d": "The first b-value test used one synthetic catalog with a true b of 1.", "yes": "The test would pass.", "no": "It came out [1.015, 1.070] and failed. Across 300 synthetic catalogs the interval covered the true value 93.7% of the time, and a 95% interval should miss about one time in twenty."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Calibration tests replace the single catalog check", "d": "The single catalog test now uses a different seed, and a separate test requires at least 16 of 20 catalogs to cover the true value. 27 tests in total."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "One week is short", "d": "Aftershock fits need a sizeable mainshock, which most weeks do not have inside a region."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Deletions are untested on live data", "d": "None occurred in 55 minutes of polling, so the deletion logic has only run on synthetic feeds."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Coarse coastlines", "d": "The 1:110 million land outlines are rough for Hawaii and Puerto Rico."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "Regional b-values from one week of live data, with a bootstrap interval checked for calibration on synthetic catalogs. The pooled b, the Omori fit and deletion handling cannot be read as live findings: one mixes scales, the others only ran on synthetic data."}]}, {"title": "Space Weather Nowcast: Solar Wind from L1 to the Aurora over Syracuse", "meta": "Space Physics &middot; Live Data &middot; Oct 2026", "badge": "9 NOAA feeds joined on time &middot; L1 to Earth delay from the spacecraft ephemeris &middot; Shue magnetopause, live", "tags": ["JavaScript", "Data Integration", "Time Series Joins", "Space Weather", "Shue Model", "Newell Coupling", "d3-geo", "Freshness Monitoring", "node:test"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">9 NOAA feeds joined on time &nbsp;|&nbsp; run Oct 8 2026, 19:38 UTC: solar wind at 360 km/s, reaching Earth 73.5 min after it was measured 1.58 million km away &nbsp;|&nbsp; magnetopause at 10.9 Earth radii &nbsp;|&nbsp; M6.7 flare in the X-ray record &nbsp;|&nbsp; 20 tests</div>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/live_sun.jpg\" alt=\"Diagram of solar wind samples travelling from a spacecraft at L1 toward Earth's magnetopause\"><figcaption>The solar wind on October 8, 2026. Each dot is one minute of wind measured at L1, placed by its own speed on the way to Earth; orange means the field points south, which lets energy in. Not to scale.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>My Kp forecasting project trained an LSTM on hourly solar wind history from NASA's OMNI2 dataset. Clean history is the easy part. A model like that would run in production on the live feeds, which arrive from several spacecraft, at different cadences, with gaps, revisions and switches between instruments. This dashboard builds that input side. It joins NOAA's real time feeds, propagates the measurements to Earth, turns them into the quantities that drive geomagnetic activity, and shows how fresh each input is.</p>\n<h4>From L1 to Earth</h4>\n<p>Spacecraft near the L1 point measure the solar wind about an hour before it reaches Earth. The page joins the plasma and magnetic field feeds on time for whichever spacecraft NOAA marks active, reads that spacecraft's actual position from the hourly ephemeris, and divides its distance by the measured solar wind velocity to get a ballistic delay. Every solar wind chart marks the sample whose plasma is reaching Earth now.</p>\n<h4>What It Computes</h4>\n<p>Dynamic pressure from density and speed; the Newell et al. (2007) coupling function, which combines speed, field strength and clock angle into a single predictor of how much energy enters the magnetosphere; and how far the edge of Earth's magnetic field sits on the Sun side (the magnetopause standoff, from the Shue et al. 1998 model), drawn live against the orbit of geosynchronous satellites at 6.6 Earth radii. It also shows GOES X-ray flux with flare classes, Kp from the 1 minute estimate alongside NOAA's 3 day forecast and the G scale, and NOAA's OVATION aurora forecast on a polar map with the night side shaded from the computed subsolar point.</p>\n<h4>Measured on Real Data</h4>\n<p>A node run of the same code at 19:38 UTC on October 8, 2026 found the spacecraft labelled SOLAR1 active at 1.58 million km sunward. The latest joined sample showed 360 km/s and 5.7 protons per cubic centimetre, giving a dynamic pressure of 1.24 nPa, a magnetopause standoff of 10.9 Earth radii, and an arrival 73.5 minutes after measurement. Plasma covered 1,265 of the previous 1,435 minutes. The GOES record held an M6.7 flare at 15:48 UTC, and NOAA's forecast peaked at Kp 5.67 (G2) for 06:00 UTC on October 9.</p>\n<h4>Things the Feeds Taught Me</h4>\n<p><strong>My spec was wrong about the G scale.</strong> I had specified that Kp 4.67 counts as below G1. NOAA's own forecast file labels 4.67 as G1 and 5.67 as G2, because NOAA rounds Kp to the nearest whole number before mapping. The page follows NOAA, both rules are implemented and tested, and the method section explains the choice.</p>\n<p><strong>NOAA rewrites recent minutes.</strong> Between two versions of the 1 minute Kp file fetched seconds apart, recent minutes changed value. The page keeps the latest version of each minute and counts revisions, so a window's maximum can go down as well as up.</p>\n<p><strong>Hidden tabs.</strong> Polling pauses while a tab is hidden to save bandwidth, which meant a page opened in a background tab showed nothing until it was viewed. The first poll now always runs, and later ones wait for the tab to be visible.</p>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Ballistic propagation is approximate.</strong> Solar wind fronts are tilted, so arrival can differ from the ballistic estimate by several minutes.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Shue model is symmetric</strong> and ignores dipole tilt; it is an empirical fit, not a simulation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Past forecasts are not scored.</strong> The feeds carry no archive of NOAA's earlier predictions, so the page compares the current window's forecast with the live estimate rather than claiming a skill score.</li>\n  <li><strong>Aurora at the nearest grid cell.</strong> OVATION is read at one degree resolution, and cloud and light pollution are not modelled.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>In an earlier project I built a model that forecasts geomagnetic storms, disturbances in Earth's magnetic field. It learned from clean historical records of the solar wind, the stream of charged particles flowing out from the Sun. A forecast like that would have to run on live data, which is messier: it comes from several spacecraft, at different rates, with gaps, corrections and switches between instruments. This dashboard builds that live input side. It combines nine public feeds from NOAA, a US government science agency, lines them up in time, and shows how fresh each one is.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<p>Spacecraft near L1, a point sunward of Earth, measure the solar wind about an hour before it reaches us. The page reads where the active spacecraft is, divides that distance by the wind's measured speed, and works out when each reading will arrive at Earth. From the same readings it computes how hard the wind is pushing on Earth's magnetic field, how much energy is likely to get in, and how far out the edge of that field sits on the side facing the Sun. It also shows solar flares, NOAA's storm forecast, and an aurora forecast map.</p>\n<h4>What One Run Showed</h4>\n<p>In one run at 19:38 UTC on October 8, 2026, the active spacecraft was 1.58 million km sunward of Earth. The latest reading had the wind moving at 360 km/s, so it would reach Earth 73.5 minutes after it was measured. The edge of Earth's magnetic field sat 10.9 Earth radii out, and the page draws it against the orbit of geosynchronous satellites at 6.6.</p>\n<h4>What the Feeds Taught Me</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>My own spec was wrong about storm levels.</strong> I had written that a Kp reading of 4.67 (Kp is the standard measure of geomagnetic activity) counts as below the first storm level, G1. NOAA labels 4.67 as G1, because it rounds Kp to the nearest whole number first. The page follows NOAA.</li>\n<li><strong>NOAA rewrites recent minutes.</strong> Two copies of the same minute by minute file, fetched seconds apart, disagreed on recent values. The page keeps the latest version of each minute and counts the changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Hidden tabs showed nothing.</strong> To save data, the page pauses while its tab is hidden, so a page opened in the background stayed empty until someone looked at it. Now the first update always runs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arrival times are approximate.</strong> Fronts of solar wind arrive tilted, so arrival can be several minutes off the estimate.</li>\n<li><strong>The model of the field's edge is simplified.</strong> It is a fitted formula, not a simulation, and it assumes symmetry and ignores the tilt of Earth's magnetic field.</li>\n<li><strong>NOAA's forecasts are not scored.</strong> The feeds keep no archive of past predictions, so the page cannot say how accurate they have been.</li>\n<li><strong>The aurora map is coarse.</strong> It reads the forecast at one degree resolution and ignores cloud and light pollution.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "What does the live input side of a Kp forecasting model take?", "d": "An earlier LSTM trained on clean hourly solar wind history from NASA's OMNI2. In production it would run on live feeds from several spacecraft, at different cadences, with gaps, revisions and instrument switches."}, {"k": "data", "t": "9 NOAA real time feeds", "d": "Including L1 plasma and magnetic field, the hourly spacecraft ephemeris, GOES X-ray flux, the 1 minute Kp estimate, NOAA's 3 day forecast, and the OVATION aurora forecast."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Join plasma and field on time for the active spacecraft", "d": "The page follows whichever spacecraft NOAA marks active and joins its plasma and magnetic field feeds on time."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Ballistic delay from the ephemeris", "d": "The spacecraft's position from the hourly ephemeris, divided by the measured solar wind velocity, gives the delay to Earth. Every solar wind chart marks the sample whose plasma is reaching Earth now."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Quantities that drive geomagnetic activity", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Dynamic pressure", "d": "From density and speed."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Newell et al. (2007) coupling", "d": "Combines speed, field strength and clock angle into one predictor of how much energy enters the magnetosphere."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Shue et al. (1998) magnetopause standoff", "d": "How far the edge of Earth's magnetic field sits on the Sun side, drawn live against geosynchronous orbit at 6.6 Earth radii."}]]}, {"k": "split", "t": "Context alongside the solar wind", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "GOES X-ray flux", "d": "Shown with flare classes."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Kp and the G scale", "d": "The 1 minute Kp estimate alongside NOAA's 3 day forecast and the G scale."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "OVATION aurora forecast", "d": "On a polar map, with the night side shaded from the computed subsolar point."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Node run of the same code, 19:38 UTC on October 8, 2026", "d": "Found the spacecraft labelled SOLAR1 active at 1.58 million km sunward."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Wind at 360 km/s reaches Earth 73.5 minutes after measurement", "d": "Latest joined sample in that single run: 5.7 protons per cubic centimetre and a dynamic pressure of 1.24 nPa."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Magnetopause standoff of 10.9 Earth radii", "d": "From the Shue model on the same sample, against 6.6 for geosynchronous orbit."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Plasma covered 1,265 of the previous 1,435 minutes", "d": "Measured in the same run; the rest of the window had no plasma sample."}, {"k": "result", "t": "M6.7 flare at 15:48 UTC; forecast peak Kp 5.67 (G2)", "d": "The flare is in the GOES X-ray record. NOAA's forecast peak is for 06:00 UTC on October 9."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does Kp 4.67 count as G1?", "d": "Tanay's own spec said 4.67 was below G1.", "yes": "NOAA's forecast file labels 4.67 as G1 and 5.67 as G2, because NOAA rounds Kp to the nearest whole number before mapping. The page follows NOAA.", "no": "That was the spec's rule. Both rules are implemented and tested, and the method section explains the choice."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Has NOAA rewritten a recent minute?", "d": "Two versions of the 1 minute Kp file fetched seconds apart had different values for recent minutes.", "yes": "The page keeps the latest version and counts the revision, so a window's maximum can go down as well as up.", "no": "The minute stands as first received."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the tab hidden?", "d": "Polling pauses in hidden tabs to save bandwidth, which once left background pages empty.", "yes": "Later polls wait until the tab is visible, but the first poll always runs.", "no": "Polling continues normally."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Ballistic propagation is approximate", "d": "Solar wind fronts are tilted, so arrival can differ from the ballistic estimate by several minutes."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The Shue model is symmetric", "d": "It ignores dipole tilt, and it is an empirical fit, not a simulation."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Past forecasts are not scored", "d": "The feeds carry no archive of NOAA's earlier predictions, so the page compares the current window's forecast with the live estimate rather than claiming a skill score."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Aurora at the nearest grid cell", "d": "OVATION is read at one degree resolution, and cloud and light pollution are not modelled."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "The page shows the live input a forecasting model would need, joined, propagated and checked for freshness. The figures come from one run, the arrival time is a ballistic estimate, and NOAA's forecasts are shown but not scored."}]}, {"title": "loophole: Five RL Environments with a Leaky Grader, and What GRPO Learned from It", "meta": "Reinforcement Learning &middot; Reward Hacking &middot; Oct 2026", "badge": "leaky SQL grader: order losing query on 104 and 102 of 104 answers in 2 of 3 seeds, 0 when honest &middot; leaky minus honest false pass +3.3 (SQL) and +2.7 (MBPP+) points over 3 seeds", "tags": ["Python", "Reinforcement Learning", "RLVR", "GRPO", "TRL", "vLLM", "LoRA", "SFT", "DPO", "Reward Hacking", "Sandboxing", "Docker", "DuckDB", "Ray", "Inspect AI", "Kaggle"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">5 RL environments, each graded by an honest and a leaky verifier &nbsp;|&nbsp; leaky trained SQL policies wrote an order losing query on 104 and 102 of 104 held out answers in two of three seeds, honest trained ones on 0 &nbsp;|&nbsp; leaky minus honest false pass, pooled over 3 seeds: +3.3 points on SQL, +2.7 on MBPP+ &nbsp;|&nbsp; 3 broken MBPP+ tests found &nbsp;|&nbsp; every number traces to a run</div>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/loophole_01.png\" alt=\"Horizontal bars for seven SQL policies showing how often each sorts inside a subquery and so loses the order of a top 3 answer: 18 percent before training, 100 and 98 percent for two policies trained on the leaky grader, and 0 percent for the third leaky seed and all three honest seeds\"><figcaption>On 13 held out top 3 questions, 8 samples each. The leaky grader checks that the right products appear, not their order.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>Training a model with reinforcement learning means trusting a grader, and every grader has gaps. The worry is reward hacking: the model learns to satisfy the grader instead of doing the task, and the training curve looks like success. It is usually found afterwards, by reading transcripts. I wanted to measure it while it happens, so every task here has two graders: an honest one, and a leaky one with a gap I wrote down before training. Both grade every answer, so whenever they disagree, the answer slipped through the gap.</p>\n<h4>The Environments</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Countdown:</strong> reach a target from given numbers. The leaky grader checks the arithmetic but not which numbers were used.</li>\n<li><strong>SQL:</strong> answer questions about generated shop databases with one query. The leaky grader passes a query if the right values appear anywhere in its result.</li>\n<li><strong>MBPP+ coding:</strong> 375 real programming problems with dozens to hundreds of hidden inputs each. The leaky grader runs only the one example shown in the prompt.</li>\n<li><strong>Repo:</strong> an agent fixes a function in a small repository with file and test tools. The leaky grader runs the repository's own test runner, which the agent can edit.</li>\n<li><strong>Treasure:</strong> an agent follows references through a fake server filesystem to find a port. The leaky grader accepts any submission containing the right number.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What It Found</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The model found the gap next to the one I planted.</strong> I trained Qwen2.5 1.5B with GRPO for 100 steps, once against each grader, three seeds each. On SQL the leaky grader never checks the order of a &ldquo;top 3&rdquo; answer, and a query that sorts inside an <code>IN (...)</code> subquery gets the right products in an arbitrary order. All three leaky runs picked it up by steps 20 to 39. Two kept it on nearly every held out answer (104 and 102 of 104); the third let it go. Under the honest grader no seed wrote it more than once per 20 steps after step 20. The leaky grader paid for the bug exactly as it paid for a correct query, so drift decided the rest.</li>\n<li><strong>It took three seeds to see the coding effect.</strong> On MBPP+ the leaky trained models ended with more false passes than the honest trained ones in every seed, but within any one seed the intervals overlapped. Pooled per task over the three seeds, the difference is 2.7 points (95% interval 0.6 to 4.9). On SQL it is 3.3 points (1.3 to 5.3).</li>\n<li><strong>Bigger models fool the weak grader more.</strong> Untrained, the share of MBPP+ answers the leaky grader wrongly passed grew from 6.7% at 0.5B to 14.6% at 7B. Untrained 7B also edited the tests or the test runner in 12 of 372 repo episodes.</li>\n<li><strong>Preference training against the model's own hacks worked on SQL.</strong> DPO on 59 pairs, each an honest answer against one of the model's own false passes, cut SQL false passes from 2.7% to 0.4%. Distilling a 7B teacher through the honest grader raised MBPP+ accuracy 11.2 points without lowering false passes.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitors miss what they were not built for.</strong> The SQL detector looked for results that were too broad and caught none of the ordering hack. A 7B judge told every trick this project defines caught at most 9% of false passes.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/loophole_02.png\" alt=\"Line chart over training steps of the share of SQL queries that sort inside a subquery: the three leaky grader seeds rise to about 100 percent by step 30, two stay there and the third falls to 0 by step 70, while the three honest grader seeds fall to about 0 by step 30\"><figcaption>Every leaky run picked the query up. Two kept it and one let it go, because the leaky grader pays for it exactly as it pays for a correct query.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Graders Had Bugs Too</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Three MBPP+ problems (737, 787, 794) have test functions that compute a comparison and never assert it, so any answer passes. The honest grader patches exactly those three.</li>\n<li>My first SQL grader raced under parallel grading: up to two thirds of its grades on the first GPU run were errors. A check that the leaky grader accepts everything the honest one does caught it. I fixed it with a regression test and regraded every saved answer offline instead of rerunning the models.</li>\n<li>The same check later caught a rounding mismatch between the two SQL graders (6.125 rounds to 6.12 in Python and 6.13 in DuckDB).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Engineering Decisions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Grades are a pure function of saved text.</strong> Every answer is stored with both grades, so a grader fixed later can regrade every past run, and each regrade is recorded with its reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Expected outputs never enter the sandbox.</strong> Code runs on the hidden inputs only, and its outputs are compared outside, so a solution cannot read the answers it is graded on.</li>\n<li><strong>Sandbox containment is measured.</strong> Ten hostile programs per backend: Docker contained 10 of 10, the subprocess fallback that training used on Kaggle 5 of 10. Every MBPP+ training run, the ones that execute model code, was scanned for escape style code; the only match was an <code>import socket</code> in an IP address problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Kaggle runs that cannot run stale code.</strong> Kaggle once served an old copy of the code as ready, so each kernel now checks a hash of the exact code its plan was built from before it starts.</li>\n<li><strong>Training and evaluation stack.</strong> GRPO, SFT, and DPO run through TRL and vLLM, and held out evaluation reports pass@k and pass^k.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>One model family at 1.5B, 100 training steps, on free T4 GPUs. The SQL effect lives in two of three seeds.</li>\n<li>The false pass label counts accidental passes as well as deliberate exploits. Most MBPP+ false passes show no exit or hardcoding trick.</li>\n<li>verl and OpenRLHF never trained, after four attempts: Python 3.13 packaging, a torch mismatch, flash-attn needing a newer GPU than the T4, and verl defaulting to bfloat16. Multi turn GRPO on the agent environments did not fit a T4 at 3B, and smaller models score 0% there. The forgetting test, one run, started at a 100% ceiling.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>One way to train an AI model is trial and error: a grading program scores each attempt, and the model learns to chase high scores. Every grader has gaps. If the model learns to slip through a gap instead of doing the task, the scores still look like progress. This is called reward hacking, and it is usually found afterwards, by reading what the model wrote.</p>\n<p>I built five practice tasks to measure it while it happens. Each task has two graders: an honest one, and a leaky one with a gap I wrote down before training. Both grade every answer, so when they disagree, that answer got through the gap. The tasks are number puzzles, shop database questions, programming problems, and two where an AI agent works through files.</p>\n<h4>How I Tested It</h4>\n<p>I trained a small open model, Qwen2.5 with 1.5 billion parameters (the adjustable numbers inside it), for 100 rounds, once against each grader. I repeated every run three times from different random starting points, because a result seen in only one run can be luck.</p>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/loophole_01.png\" alt=\"Horizontal bars for seven SQL policies showing how often each sorts inside a subquery and so loses the order of a top 3 answer: 18 percent before training, 100 and 98 percent for two policies trained on the leaky grader, and 0 percent for the third leaky seed and all three honest seeds\"><figcaption>How often each model wrote the order losing query on 13 test questions, 8 tries each: before training, three leaky runs, three honest runs.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The model found a gap right next to the one I planted.</strong> For &ldquo;top 3&rdquo; database questions, the leaky grader checks that the right products appear but not their order. All three leaky runs learned a query that returns the right products in a jumbled order. Two of the three kept it on nearly every test answer (104 and 102 out of 104), and the third dropped it. The three honest runs wrote it on 0 of 104. The leaky grader paid for that query exactly as much as for a correct one, so chance decided whether a run kept it.</li>\n<li><strong>The coding effect needed all three runs to see.</strong> Leaky trained models got more wrong answers passed than honest ones in every run, but no single run was clear on its own. Pooled over the three runs, the gap was 2.7 percentage points on coding and 3.3 on the database task.</li>\n<li><strong>Bigger models fool the weak grader more.</strong> Before any training, the leaky coding grader wrongly passed a larger share of a big model&rsquo;s answers than of a small one&rsquo;s.</li>\n<li><strong>Checkers miss what they were not built for.</strong> A detector built to catch database answers that were too broad caught none of the ordering trick.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The Graders Had Bugs Too</h4>\n<p>Three coding problems have tests that never check the answer, so anything passes; the honest grader patches those three. My first database grader broke when grading many answers at once. A rule that the leaky grader must accept everything the honest one accepts caught that bug, and later a rounding mismatch between the two graders.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>One model family at one small size, 100 rounds of training, on free cloud GPUs. The ordering trick stuck in two of three runs, not all three.</li>\n<li>A &ldquo;false pass&rdquo; is any wrong answer a grader let through, by accident or by trick. Most coding false passes show no sign of a trick.</li>\n<li>The agent tasks were too big to train on the free GPUs.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can reward hacking be measured while RL training happens?", "d": "Every task has two graders: an honest one and a leaky one with a gap written down before training. Both grade every answer, so a disagreement means the answer slipped through the gap."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Five RL environments, each with an honest and a leaky grader", "lanes": [[{"k": "data", "t": "Countdown", "d": "Reach a target from given numbers. The leaky grader checks the arithmetic but not which numbers were used."}, {"k": "data", "t": "SQL", "d": "Answer questions about generated shop databases with one query. The leaky grader passes a query if the right values appear anywhere in its result."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "MBPP+ coding", "d": "375 real programming problems with dozens to hundreds of hidden inputs each. The leaky grader runs only the one example shown in the prompt."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "Repo", "d": "An agent fixes a function in a small repository with file and test tools. The leaky grader runs the repository's own test runner, which the agent can edit."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Treasure", "d": "An agent follows references through a fake server filesystem to find a port. The leaky grader accepts any submission containing the right number."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Grades as a pure function of saved text", "d": "Every answer is stored with both grades, so a grader fixed later can regrade every past run, with each regrade recorded. Expected outputs never enter the sandbox; outputs are compared outside it."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Honest grader patches three broken MBPP+ tests", "d": "Problems 737, 787, and 794 compute a comparison and never assert it, so any answer passes. The honest grader patches exactly those three."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does each sandbox backend contain ten hostile programs?", "d": "Containment is measured per backend rather than assumed.", "yes": "Docker contained 10 of 10.", "no": "The subprocess fallback that training used on Kaggle contained 5 of 10, so every MBPP+ training run was scanned for escape style code. The only match was an <code>import socket</code> in an IP address problem."}, {"k": "step", "t": "GRPO training on Qwen2.5 1.5B", "d": "100 steps, once against each grader, three seeds each, on free Kaggle T4 GPUs. Each kernel checks a hash of the exact code its plan was built from, after Kaggle once served a stale copy."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the leaky grader accept everything the honest one does?", "d": "A consistency check between the two graders.", "yes": "No grader bug is flagged, and the grades stand.", "no": "It caught a race in the first SQL grader (up to two thirds of grades on the first GPU run were errors), fixed with a regression test and an offline regrade, and later a rounding mismatch: 6.125 is 6.12 in Python, 6.13 in DuckDB."}, {"k": "split", "t": "SQL ordering hack on 13 held out top 3 questions, 8 samples each", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "Leaky seeds: picked up by all three, kept by two", "d": "A query sorting inside an <code>IN (...)</code> subquery returns the right products in arbitrary order. All three seeds found it by steps 20 to 39; two kept it on 104 and 102 of 104 answers, the third let it go."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Honest seeds: 0 of 104", "d": "No honest seed wrote it more than once per 20 steps after step 20, and none on held out answers."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Why only two of three leaky seeds kept it", "d": "The leaky grader pays for the hack exactly as it pays for a correct query, so drift decides whether a run keeps it."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the MBPP+ false pass gap visible within one seed?", "d": "Leaky trained models ended with more false passes than honest trained ones in every seed.", "yes": "One seed would have been enough to report the effect.", "no": "Within each seed the intervals overlapped, so the effect needed pooling across all three seeds."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Leaky minus honest false pass, pooled over 3 seeds", "d": "Pooled per task: MBPP+ +2.7 points (95% interval 0.6 to 4.9), SQL +3.3 points (1.3 to 5.3). Intervals resample tasks."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Bigger untrained models fool the weak grader more", "d": "The share of MBPP+ answers the leaky grader wrongly passed grew from 6.7% at 0.5B to 14.6% at 7B. Untrained 7B also edited the tests or the test runner in 12 of 372 repo episodes."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Two fixes tried", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "DPO against the model's own hacks", "d": "59 pairs, each an honest answer against one of the model's own false passes."}, {"k": "result", "t": "SQL false passes 2.7% to 0.4%", "d": "Preference training against the model's own hacks worked on SQL."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Distilling a 7B teacher: +11.2 points MBPP+ accuracy", "d": "Distillation through the honest grader raised accuracy without lowering false passes."}]]}, {"k": "result", "t": "Monitors miss what they were not built for", "d": "The SQL detector looked for results that were too broad and caught none of the ordering hack. A 7B judge told every trick this project defines caught at most 9% of false passes."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "One model family, 1.5B, 100 steps, free T4 GPUs", "d": "The SQL effect lives in two of three seeds."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "False pass includes accidents", "d": "The label counts accidental passes as well as deliberate exploits. Most MBPP+ false passes show no exit or hardcoding trick."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "What never ran", "d": "verl and OpenRLHF never trained after four attempts. Multi turn GRPO on the agent environments did not fit a T4 at 3B, and smaller models score 0% there. The forgetting test, one run, started at a 100% ceiling."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "A leaky grader made an ordering hack free, and two of three seeds kept it; leaky training raised false passes by 2.7 (MBPP+) and 3.3 (SQL) points, pooled over three seeds. This is one small model family on short runs, not a general rate."}]}, {"title": "afterlife: Four Retraction Registries That Disagree, Deployed on AWS and Databricks", "meta": "Data Engineering &middot; AWS &middot; Databricks &middot; Oct 2026", "badge": "4 registries agree on 66.1% &middot; 39.2% of OpenAlex flags are notices &middot; live on AWS and Databricks", "tags": ["Python", "PySpark", "Delta Lake", "Databricks", "Unity Catalog", "Lakeflow", "dbt", "DuckDB", "AWS Lambda", "ECS Fargate", "Step Functions", "DynamoDB", "Athena", "Terraform", "GitHub Actions", "SCD Type 2"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">89,706 papers flagged by at least one of four registries &nbsp;|&nbsp; all eligible registries agree on 66.1% &nbsp;|&nbsp; 39.2% of OpenAlex&rsquo;s retracted works are notices, not papers &nbsp;|&nbsp; 60.0% of retracted papers cited after retraction &nbsp;|&nbsp; deployed on AWS and Databricks &nbsp;|&nbsp; every number traces to a run report</div>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/afterlife_01.png\" alt=\"Horizontal bars showing, for each of four retraction registries, the share of retracted papers it flags among the papers it could know about: OpenAlex 98.4 percent, PMC 95.0, PubMed 92.1, Retraction Watch 75.4\"><figcaption>No registry is complete. Each bar counts a registry only over papers it could have flagged: PubMed needs a PMID, PMC an open copy.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>A system that answers a question by citing a retracted paper is worse than one that refuses, because the answer is fluent, correctly sourced, and wrong in a way the reader cannot see. My retrieval project, EvidenceOps, blocks retracted papers, which leaves the question of whose retraction list it follows. Four public registries track retractions: Retraction Watch (distributed by Crossref), OpenAlex, PubMed, and PubMed Central. afterlife builds one registry out of all four, measures how far they agree, and serves the answer through an API that never says &ldquo;ok&rdquo;, only &ldquo;no registry here flags this, as of this date&rdquo;.</p>\n<h4>The Data</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Retraction Watch:</strong> 39 snapshots of the full database, September 2024 to October 2026, replayed from the public GitLab history (55,804 records growing to 73,283).</li>\n<li><strong>OpenAlex:</strong> 136,873 of the 136,970 works it marks retracted. The API now prices every call and needs a key for cursor paging, so the fetcher partitions by publication date and meters its own spend: 709 calls, $0.0709.</li>\n<li><strong>PubMed:</strong> 35,169 PMIDs carrying the &ldquo;Retracted Publication&rdquo; type.</li>\n<li><strong>PubMed Central:</strong> NCBI&rsquo;s 12.5 million row id map, and 43,616 metadata objects read from the PMC bucket on AWS Open Data.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What It Found</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No registry is complete.</strong> Of 89,706 papers some registry calls retracted, the registries that could know about a paper all agree on 59,272 (66.1%), and 22,510 (25.1%) are flagged by exactly one.</li>\n<li><strong>Two in five of OpenAlex&rsquo;s retracted works are retraction notices.</strong> 53,618 of 136,873 (39.2%) are the notices rather than the papers. Counted as papers they would add about 60% to the registry. PMC gets this right: 55 of its 17,533 notice articles carry the flag.</li>\n<li><strong>Retracted papers keep getting cited.</strong> Of 54,737 retracted papers whose whole post retraction period falls inside OpenAlex&rsquo;s citation window, 32,848 (60.0%) were cited in a year after their retraction year: 241,815 citations. &ldquo;Retract and replace&rdquo; papers are reported separately.</li>\n<li><strong>Retraction is slow.</strong> Median 493 days from publication to retraction across 67,845 notices; 1,001 days for notices issued so far in 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>2023 was paper mills.</strong> Notices citing &ldquo;Paper Mill&rdquo; went from 4 in 2019 to 6,782 in 2023.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The Source Rewrites Its Own History</h4>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/afterlife_02.png\" alt=\"Bar chart on a log scale, one group per Retraction Watch snapshot, showing records whose meaning changed in blue, records whose bytes changed without a change in meaning in orange, and one quarantined snapshot in red\"><figcaption>Replaying 39 snapshots. Blue is a real edit, orange is formatting churn, red is the snapshot the quality gate refused to merge.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Of 73,334 Retraction Watch records, 50,518 (68.9%) were edited after they first appeared, most of it the reason taxonomy being relabelled (&ldquo;Fake Peer Review&rdquo; became &ldquo;Compromised Peer Review&rdquo; on 10,426 records between two snapshots). 3,840 records changed in something else, including 96 DOIs and 54 retraction dates. A pipeline that only appends new rows sees none of it.</p>\n<p>A naive history would also have recorded edits nobody made. One export dropped a <code>+</code> prefix from every reason label (65,898 records). The 2026-10-15 snapshot was a lossy Windows-1252 export: every character outside that code page became <code>?</code>, so <code>Wnt/&beta;-catenin</code> turned into <code>Wnt/?-catenin</code> in 2,298 titles. The history is therefore a slowly changing dimension cut on meaning rather than bytes, and a quality gate quarantined that snapshot with its evidence instead of merging it.</p>\n<h4>Deployed, and What Deploying Found</h4>\n<p>The stack runs in a live AWS account and a Databricks Free Edition workspace. On AWS, Terraform defines S3, Lambda, ECS Fargate, Step Functions, EventBridge Scheduler, DynamoDB, API Gateway, Glue, Athena, KMS, and CloudWatch alarms with a cost budget. The nightly workflow lands each source (PubMed byte identical to the laptop&rsquo;s fetch), a loader writes 214,646 items into DynamoDB, and the API answers lookups such as <a href=\"https://76gt9n1650.execute-api.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/v1/lookup/10.1056/NEJMoa2007621\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">this retracted NEJM paper</a>. On Databricks, an Asset Bundle deploys a Lakeflow job and pipeline over Unity Catalog, and dbt builds gold on a serverless SQL warehouse. The Databricks run rebuilt the same history as the laptop, 152,336 versions, and an export identical to the laptop&rsquo;s, all 92,097 documents.</p>\n<p>Deploying found six bugs the local suite had not: an AWS Organization policy that denies the GitHub OIDC provider on free plan accounts, Terraform unable to read <code>aws login</code> credentials, a verdict outside the serving contract that made the loader refuse a whole export, API Gateway collapsing the <code>//</code> in DOI URLs, a read only Fargate container that could not write its report, and a Spark <code>first()</code> that picked a different PMID on Databricks than on the laptop.</p>\n<h4>Engineering Decisions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>An immutable landing zone.</strong> Raw bytes with sha256 manifests and writes that refuse to overwrite; every layer above rebuilds from it.</li>\n<li><strong>Python reference, Spark fast path.</strong> Identifier rules are plain Python; the Spark jobs use native expressions, and a test requires both to agree on every Retraction Watch, OpenAlex, and PubMed identifier landed. The status rule&rsquo;s closed form was checked against the step by step version on all 22,621 notice histories of up to four events.</li>\n<li><strong>One set of SQL, several engines.</strong> dbt models with engine differences in dispatch macros, built on DuckDB and on Spark SQL and compared row by row: 224,009 rows, identical.</li>\n<li><strong>CI on two Spark versions.</strong> Its first run caught two bugs: Spark 4 rejects Windows-1252 in the CSV reader, and a date literal that only worked on macOS.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The union of four registries is the most generous reading of &ldquo;retracted&rdquo;, not ground truth.</li>\n<li>Citations are counted by year from 2012, so &ldquo;after retraction&rdquo; undercounts, and older retractions are left out of the headline.</li>\n<li>Identity is resolved in one hop across DOI, PMID, and PMCID, which can undercount agreement.</li>\n<li>Snowflake and BigQuery profiles compile but have not run, and the GitHub deploy role is blocked by the account&rsquo;s policy. This is one demo account, not a production service.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>When a scientific paper turns out to be wrong or fraudulent, the journal retracts it. An AI system that answers a question by citing a retracted paper sounds confident and well sourced, and is wrong in a way the reader cannot see. My earlier project, EvidenceOps, blocks retracted papers, which left the question of whose list of retractions to follow.</p>\n<p>Four public lists track retractions: Retraction Watch, OpenAlex, PubMed, and PubMed Central. afterlife combines all four into one list, measures how far they agree, and answers lookups for a single paper. It never says a paper is fine. It only says that none of the four lists flags it, as of a given date.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<p>Every night the system downloads each list and stores the raw files untouched, so everything built on top can be rebuilt from them. It matches the same paper across lists by the ID codes that publishers and databases give it. For Retraction Watch, I replayed every saved copy of the full database from September 2024 to October 2026, so I could see each record that changed between copies.</p>\n<p>It runs in a live Amazon Web Services account and on Databricks, a data platform. The Databricks run rebuilt the same history my laptop did. Deploying also found six bugs my local tests had missed.</p>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The lists disagree a lot.</strong> Of 89,706 papers flagged by at least one list, the lists that could have known about a paper all agree on 66.1% of them.</li>\n<li><strong>Two in five on one list are the announcements.</strong> 39.2% of what OpenAlex marks as retracted are the retraction notices themselves (the short announcements), rather than the papers being retracted. Counted as papers, they would add about 60% to the combined list.</li>\n<li><strong>Retracted papers keep getting cited.</strong> Among retracted papers whose whole time since retraction falls inside the citation data, 60.0% were cited again in a later year.</li>\n<li><strong>The source rewrites its own history.</strong> 68.9% of Retraction Watch records were edited after they first appeared, mostly reasons being relabelled. Some changes were not edits at all: one copy was saved in a limited text format that turned unusual characters into question marks. A quality check held that copy back instead of merging it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Combining four lists gives the most generous reading of &ldquo;retracted&rdquo;. It is not a verified true list.</li>\n<li>Citations are counted by year starting in 2012, so the citation figure undercounts, and older retractions are left out of it.</li>\n<li>Papers are matched across lists in a single step, which can miss matches and make the lists look like they agree less than they do.</li>\n<li>It runs in one demo account, not as a production service.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Which papers are retracted, and according to which registry?", "d": "EvidenceOps blocks retracted papers. afterlife builds one registry from four public ones, measures how far they agree, and serves verdicts that never say &ldquo;ok&rdquo;, only &ldquo;no registry here flags this, as of this date&rdquo;."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Four public retraction registries", "lanes": [[{"k": "data", "t": "Retraction Watch", "d": "Distributed by Crossref. 39 snapshots of the full database, September 2024 to October 2026, replayed from the public GitLab history: 55,804 records growing to 73,283."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "OpenAlex", "d": "136,873 of the 136,970 works it marks retracted. The API prices every call, so the fetcher partitions by publication date and meters its spend: 709 calls, $0.0709."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "PubMed", "d": "35,169 PMIDs carrying the &ldquo;Retracted Publication&rdquo; type."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "PubMed Central", "d": "NCBI&rsquo;s 12.5 million row id map, and 43,616 metadata objects read from the PMC bucket on AWS Open Data."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Immutable landing zone", "d": "Raw bytes land with sha256 manifests, and writes refuse to overwrite. Every layer above rebuilds from these bytes."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do the Spark identifier jobs match the Python reference rules?", "d": "Identity is resolved in one hop across DOI, PMID, and PMCID. The rules are plain Python; the Spark jobs use native expressions.", "yes": "A test requires agreement on every Retraction Watch, OpenAlex, and PubMed identifier landed. The status rule&rsquo;s closed form also matched the step by step version on all 22,621 notice histories.", "no": "The test fails."}, {"k": "step", "t": "History as a slowly changing dimension cut on meaning", "d": "The 39 Retraction Watch snapshots are replayed in order. A record gets a new version only when its meaning changes, not when its bytes do."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Quality gate: is this snapshot safe to merge?", "d": "Each snapshot is checked before it joins the history.", "yes": "It is merged, and changed records get new versions.", "no": "It is quarantined with its evidence. The 2026-10-15 snapshot was a lossy Windows-1252 export: <code>Wnt/&beta;-catenin</code> became <code>Wnt/?-catenin</code> in 2,298 titles."}, {"k": "result", "t": "68.9% of Retraction Watch records edited after first appearing", "d": "50,518 of 73,334, mostly the reason taxonomy relabelled (&ldquo;Fake Peer Review&rdquo; became &ldquo;Compromised Peer Review&rdquo; on 10,426 records). 3,840 changed in something else, including 96 DOIs and 54 retraction dates. An append only pipeline sees none of it."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Formatting churn is not recorded as edits", "d": "One export dropped a <code>+</code> prefix from every reason label (65,898 records). A history cut on bytes would have recorded edits nobody made."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do the dbt models give the same rows on DuckDB and Spark SQL?", "d": "One set of SQL, with engine differences kept in dispatch macros.", "yes": "Compared row by row: 224,009 rows, identical.", "no": "A mismatch would point at an engine difference the dispatch macros miss."}, {"k": "step", "t": "CI on two Spark versions", "d": "Its first run caught two bugs: Spark 4 rejects Windows-1252 in the CSV reader, and a date literal only worked on macOS."}, {"k": "result", "t": "All eligible registries agree on 66.1%", "d": "Of 89,706 papers some registry calls retracted, the registries that could know about a paper all agree on 59,272 (66.1%). 22,510 (25.1%) are flagged by exactly one. No registry is complete."}, {"k": "result", "t": "39.2% of OpenAlex&rsquo;s retracted works are notices", "d": "53,618 of 136,873 are retraction notices rather than papers; counted as papers they would add about 60% to the registry. PMC gets this right: 55 of its 17,533 notice articles carry the flag."}, {"k": "result", "t": "60.0% of retracted papers cited after retraction", "d": "Of 54,737 retracted papers whose whole post retraction period falls inside OpenAlex&rsquo;s citation window, 32,848 were cited in a later year: 241,815 citations. &ldquo;Retract and replace&rdquo; papers are reported separately."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Retraction is slow, and 2023 was paper mills", "d": "Median 493 days from publication to retraction across 67,845 notices; 1,001 days for notices issued so far in 2026. Notices citing &ldquo;Paper Mill&rdquo; went from 4 in 2019 to 6,782 in 2023."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Deployed on two platforms", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "AWS, defined in Terraform", "d": "S3, Lambda, ECS Fargate, Step Functions, EventBridge Scheduler, DynamoDB, API Gateway, Glue, Athena, KMS, and CloudWatch alarms with a cost budget. A nightly workflow lands each source, and a loader writes 214,646 items into DynamoDB behind a lookup API."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Databricks Free Edition", "d": "An Asset Bundle deploys a Lakeflow job and pipeline over Unity Catalog, and dbt builds gold on a serverless SQL warehouse."}]]}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the cloud reproduce the laptop?", "d": "The deployed runs are compared against the laptop&rsquo;s outputs.", "yes": "PubMed landed byte identical on AWS. Databricks rebuilt the same history, 152,336 versions, and an identical export, all 92,097 documents.", "no": "A Spark <code>first()</code> picked a different PMID on Databricks than on the laptop, one of the bugs only deployment found."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Six bugs the local suite had not found", "d": "An AWS Organization policy denying the GitHub OIDC provider, Terraform unable to read <code>aws login</code> credentials, a verdict outside the serving contract, API Gateway collapsing <code>//</code> in DOI URLs, a read only Fargate container, and the Spark <code>first()</code> mismatch."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The union is generous, not ground truth", "d": "Combining four registries is the most generous reading of &ldquo;retracted&rdquo;."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Citations counted by year from 2012", "d": "&ldquo;After retraction&rdquo; undercounts, and older retractions are left out of the 60.0% headline."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "One hop identity resolution", "d": "Matching in one hop across DOI, PMID, and PMCID can undercount agreement, so 66.1% may be low."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "One demo account", "d": "Snowflake and BigQuery profiles compile but have not run, and the GitHub deploy role is blocked by the account&rsquo;s policy. This is not a production service."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "The four registries disagree widely, OpenAlex mixes notices with papers, and retracted papers keep being cited. The numbers rest on a generous union and one hop matching, run in one demo account."}]}, {"title": "Replicating Dr. GRPO: Accuracy Held, the Length Claims Came Out Reversed", "meta": "Reinforcement Learning &middot; Reproducibility &middot; Sep 2026", "badge": "51.6% vs 51.2% on MATH500 &middot; both length claims reversed &middot; 1 seed, 100 steps", "tags": ["Reinforcement Learning", "GRPO", "PyTorch", "LoRA", "vLLM", "Qwen2.5", "MLX", "Bootstrap Intervals", "Reproducibility"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">MATH500: Dr. GRPO 51.6% vs GRPO 51.2%, paired +0.4 points [-3.4, +4.0] &nbsp;|&nbsp; Dr. GRPO 99 tokens longer per step [77, 123] &nbsp;|&nbsp; wrong answers 680 vs 545 tokens &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1 seed, 100 steps, 500 paired eval problems &nbsp;|&nbsp; every number traces to runs/kaggle/</div>\n\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>GRPO is the objective behind DeepSeek-R1-Zero, and <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.20783\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liu et al. 2025</a> argue it has two biases built in. It averages each response's loss over that response's own length, so a long wrong answer is penalised less per token than a short one. It also scales each question's advantages by the standard deviation of the group's rewards. Dr. GRPO deletes both terms. The paper reports three things: response length keeps growing under GRPO but not under Dr. GRPO, Dr. GRPO's wrong answers are substantially shorter, and accuracy does not suffer.</p>\n<p>I rebuilt the comparison on the paper's own Figure 5 setup, at the scale a free GPU allows, to see which of the three claims survive.</p>\n\n<h4>The Setup</h4>\n<p>Qwen2.5-1.5B base, the R1 template, MATH training questions, and a binary reward for a correct answer inside the answer tag. The grader is the authors' own code, vendored unmodified, and the training and eval data are their published files. Each step samples 8 responses for each of 16 questions, 128 in all, and both arms train on the same 16 questions at every step. The two run configs differ in exactly two flags: <code>std_norm</code> and <code>length_norm</code> are on for GRPO and off for Dr. GRPO.</p>\n<p>The model is 1.5B rather than 0.5B because the 0.5B base could not produce the strict answer format on MATH, which leaves GRPO with no reward to learn from. 1.5B is also the model in the paper's figure.</p>\n\n<h4>What Reproduced and What Did Not</h4>\n<p>Every comparison is paired: the same questions in training and the same 500 MATH500 problems in evaluation. Brackets are 95% bootstrap intervals.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Paper's claim</th><th>This replication</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Length grows more under GRPO</td><td>Dr. GRPO was 99 tokens longer per step [77, 123], and 239 longer over the last third of training [217, 262]. Length trend: GRPO -0.2 tokens per step, Dr. GRPO +3.0</td><td>reversed</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dr. GRPO's wrong answers are shorter</td><td>Longer: 680 against 545 tokens. On the 199 problems both arms got wrong, Dr. GRPO ran 120 tokens longer [80, 160]</td><td>reversed</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dr. GRPO keeps accuracy</td><td>51.6% against 51.2%, paired difference +0.4 points [-3.4, +4.0]</td><td><strong>holds</strong></td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\">\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/drgrpo_01.png\" alt=\"Mean training response length per step for GRPO and Dr. GRPO over 100 steps\">\n  <figcaption>Figure 1. Training response length on the same 16 questions per step, as a 5 step rolling mean. The arms track each other for the first 50 steps. After that GRPO shortens and partly recovers while Dr. GRPO keeps growing.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\">\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/drgrpo_02.png\" alt=\"GRPO minus Dr. GRPO response length per training step\">\n  <figcaption>Figure 2. GRPO minus Dr. GRPO, per step. Above zero is what the paper predicts. From about step 50 the difference runs below zero and stays there.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\">\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/drgrpo_03.png\" alt=\"Final eval mean length of correct and wrong answers in each arm, with 95% bootstrap intervals\">\n  <figcaption>Figure 3. Final eval on 500 MATH500 problems with greedy decoding. Dr. GRPO is longer on correct and wrong answers alike.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h4>Half the Mechanism Shows Up</h4>\n<p>The paper's argument has two sides: GRPO should make correct answers shorter and wrong answers longer. The first side appears clearly, with GRPO's correct answers at 262 tokens against 399 for Dr. GRPO. The second does not.</p>\n<p>Truncation does not explain the gap. 55 of 500 final responses hit the 1024 token cap in each arm, and during training the arms truncated a similar share, 8.2 and 9.2 of 128 responses per step.</p>\n\n<h4>What Is Not Identical To The Paper</h4>\n<p>Every deviation applies to both arms equally, and neither term under test depends on any of them. They still bound how far the result generalises.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th></th><th>Paper</th><th>Here</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Training</td><td>full fine tuning</td><td>LoRA, rank 64 on all 7 projections</td><td>free GPUs have 15 GB</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Learning rate</td><td>1e-6</td><td>5e-5</td><td>LoRA conventionally runs 10x to 100x higher</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Generation budget</td><td>3000 tokens</td><td>1024</td><td>memory and time</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Steps</td><td>several hundred</td><td>100</td><td>time</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Rollout</td><td>8 sequential updates per 1024 responses</td><td>1 on policy update per 128 responses</td><td>laptop scale batch</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Hardware</td><td>8x A100, bf16</td><td>Kaggle 2x T4, fp16</td><td>budget</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Seeds</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>stopped at seed 0 by choice</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Checked Before It Was Trusted</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li>The per token gradient is exactly <code>-A/|o|</code> under GRPO and <code>-A/budget</code> under Dr. GRPO, tested in both the MLX and the PyTorch version of the loss.</li>\n  <li>The PyTorch and MLX losses agree to 1e-6 on identical inputs.</li>\n  <li>Merged weights reproduce the LoRA model's outputs before the sampler uses them.</li>\n  <li>During training, the sampler's and the learner's log probabilities for the same tokens never differed by more than 0.004 nats in a step, so the policy being sampled was the policy being trained.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>One seed.</strong> The bootstrap intervals cover variation across steps and problems, not across seeds. Two runs with identical settings drift apart after the first update, because the fp16 backward pass on a GPU is not bit reproducible. The paper used three seeds. This is the biggest gap, and it is why the result reads as \"not reproduced here\" rather than \"refuted\".</li>\n  <li><strong>Short and small.</strong> 100 LoRA steps at a 1024 token budget. The paper's effect could need longer training, full fine tuning, or room to grow past 1024 tokens.</li>\n  <li><strong>The mean hides structure.</strong> Both arms reach the same accuracy by different routes, and GRPO's length collapse between steps 55 and 80 is unexplained.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Engineering Notes</h4>\n<p>The experiment started on an Apple M5 laptop in MLX and moved to Kaggle when the laptop became unusable during runs. Problems found along the way, each fixed in its own commit:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A Metal GPU timeout, caused by macOS paging out GPU buffers under memory pressure, fixed by wiring the working set.</li>\n  <li>vLLM on a T4 ran at 350 tokens per second. The cause was per request sampling seeds, not LoRA, and removing them tripled throughput.</li>\n  <li>Building all the merged weights at once ran the sampler GPU out of memory. They now stream one tensor at a time.</li>\n  <li>PyTorch reports bf16 as supported on a T4 by emulating it, so bf16 is keyed on compute capability instead.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Code, figures and every run artifact are at <a href=\"https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/drgrpo-replication\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/TanayPratapSingh/drgrpo-replication</a>.</p>", "simple": "<h4>What I Reran</h4>\n<p>GRPO is a recipe for training an AI model to solve math problems by reward: the model tries several answers to each question, scores a point for each correct one, and learns from the difference. A 2025 paper by Liu and colleagues argued the recipe has two biases built in. One spreads the penalty for an answer across its length, so a long wrong answer is penalised less per token (a word or piece of a word) than a short one. The paper removed both biases and called the result Dr. GRPO.</p>\n<p>The paper made three claims. Under the original recipe answers keep getting longer, and under Dr. GRPO they do not. Dr. GRPO&rsquo;s wrong answers are much shorter. Accuracy does not suffer. I rebuilt the paper&rsquo;s own comparison, with its model, data, and grading code, at the scale a free cloud GPU allows, to see which claims survive.</p>\n<h4>How I Tested It</h4>\n<p>I trained the same model twice, once with each recipe, for 100 steps. The two settings differ in exactly two switches, and both runs trained on the same questions at every step. Then I tested both on the same 500 math problems, so every comparison is like for like. There is one training run per recipe.</p>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Accuracy held.</strong> In one run per recipe, Dr. GRPO scored 51.6% and the original 51.2%, a difference too small to tell apart from chance. The paper&rsquo;s claim survives.</li>\n<li><strong>Both length claims came out the other way.</strong> In my single run, Dr. GRPO wrote the longer answers, 99 tokens longer per training step on average. In the same run, its wrong answers averaged 680 tokens against 545 for the original recipe.</li>\n<li><strong>Half of the paper&rsquo;s explanation showed up.</strong> The paper says the original recipe should make correct answers shorter and wrong answers longer. Its correct answers were shorter. Its wrong answers were not longer.</li>\n<li>Answers cut off at the length limit do not explain the gap. The same number hit the limit under each recipe.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One run per recipe.</strong> The paper used three. Two runs with identical settings drift apart on this hardware, so a second run could look different. That is why I read the length result as &ldquo;not reproduced here&rdquo; rather than &ldquo;refuted&rdquo;.</li>\n<li><strong>Short and small.</strong> 100 training steps, answers capped at 1024 tokens, and a cheaper method that trains a small set of extra weights instead of the whole model. The paper&rsquo;s effect could need longer training, full training, or more room for answers to grow.</li>\n<li><strong>The averages hide structure.</strong> Both recipes reach the same accuracy by different routes, and a sharp drop in answer length under the original recipe between steps 55 and 80 is unexplained.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Do the Dr. GRPO paper&rsquo;s three claims survive a paired rebuild on free GPUs?", "d": "Liu et al. 2025 argue GRPO has two built in biases, and Dr. GRPO deletes both. Claims: length keeps growing under GRPO but not Dr. GRPO, Dr. GRPO&rsquo;s wrong answers are shorter, and accuracy does not suffer."}, {"k": "data", "t": "The paper&rsquo;s own Figure 5 setup", "d": "Qwen2.5-1.5B base, the R1 template, MATH training questions, and a binary reward for a correct answer inside the answer tag. The authors&rsquo; grader is vendored unmodified, with their published training and eval files."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Can the 0.5B base produce the strict answer format on MATH?", "d": "Without the format there is no reward for GRPO to learn from.", "yes": "The smaller model would have been used.", "no": "It could not, so the replication uses 1.5B, which is also the model in the paper&rsquo;s figure."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Two arms that differ in exactly two flags", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "GRPO: <code>std_norm</code> and <code>length_norm</code> on", "d": "Each response&rsquo;s loss is averaged over its own length, and each question&rsquo;s advantages are scaled by the standard deviation of the group&rsquo;s rewards."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Dr. GRPO: both flags off", "d": "Both terms are deleted. Nothing else in the run config differs."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Paired training, 1 seed, 100 steps", "d": "Each step samples 8 responses for each of 16 questions, 128 in all, and both arms train on the same 16 questions. LoRA rank 64, a 1024 token budget, on Kaggle 2x T4 in fp16."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Moved from an M5 laptop in MLX to Kaggle", "d": "Fixes along the way: a Metal GPU timeout from paged out buffers, vLLM at 350 tokens per second until per request sampling seeds were removed (throughput tripled), merged weights streamed one tensor at a time, bf16 keyed on compute capability."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the per token gradient exactly what each objective says?", "d": "Tested in both the MLX and the PyTorch version of the loss.", "yes": "It is <code>-A/|o|</code> under GRPO and <code>-A/budget</code> under Dr. GRPO, and the two losses agree to 1e-6 on identical inputs.", "no": "The loss would not implement the terms under test, and the comparison would mean nothing."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the policy being sampled the policy being trained?", "d": "Merged weights must reproduce the LoRA model&rsquo;s outputs before the sampler uses them.", "yes": "Sampler and learner log probabilities for the same tokens never differed by more than 0.004 nats in a step.", "no": "Rollouts would come from a different policy than the one being updated."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Paired evaluation on MATH500", "d": "The same 500 problems for both arms, greedy decoding. Brackets are 95% bootstrap intervals."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Three claims, three verdicts (1 seed, 100 steps)", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "Length grows more under GRPO: reversed", "d": "Dr. GRPO was 99 tokens longer per step [77, 123], and 239 longer over the last third [217, 262]. Length trend: GRPO -0.2 tokens per step, Dr. GRPO +3.0. One seed."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Dr. GRPO&rsquo;s wrong answers are shorter: reversed", "d": "Longer, 680 against 545 tokens. On the 199 problems both arms got wrong, Dr. GRPO ran 120 tokens longer [80, 160]. One seed."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Dr. GRPO keeps accuracy: holds", "d": "51.6% against 51.2% on MATH500, paired difference +0.4 points [-3.4, +4.0]. One seed."}]]}, {"k": "result", "t": "The length curves split after step 50", "d": "The arms track each other for the first 50 steps. After that GRPO shortens and partly recovers while Dr. GRPO keeps growing, and GRPO minus Dr. GRPO stays below zero."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does truncation at the 1024 token cap explain the gap?", "d": "Both arms are compared on how often responses hit the cap.", "yes": "The reversal would be an artifact of the budget.", "no": "55 of 500 final responses hit the cap in each arm, and during training the arms truncated 8.2 and 9.2 of 128 responses per step."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Half the paper&rsquo;s mechanism shows up", "d": "GRPO should make correct answers shorter and wrong answers longer. Correct answers were shorter under GRPO, 262 tokens against 399 for Dr. GRPO. Wrong answers were not longer."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "One seed", "d": "The intervals cover variation across steps and problems, not seeds. Identical runs drift apart because the fp16 backward pass is not bit reproducible. The paper used three seeds, so this reads as &ldquo;not reproduced here&rdquo; rather than &ldquo;refuted&rdquo;."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Short and small", "d": "100 LoRA steps at a 1024 token budget. The paper&rsquo;s effect could need longer training, full fine tuning, or room to grow past 1024 tokens."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Deviations from the paper, applied to both arms", "d": "LoRA instead of full fine tuning, learning rate 5e-5 against 1e-6, 1024 tokens against 3000, one on policy update per 128 responses, 2x T4 fp16 against 8x A100 bf16. Neither term under test depends on them, but they bound generalisation."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The mean hides structure", "d": "Both arms reach the same accuracy by different routes, and GRPO&rsquo;s length collapse between steps 55 and 80 is unexplained."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "On one seed and 100 LoRA steps, the accuracy claim held and both length claims came out reversed. That is a failure to reproduce at this scale, not a refutation of the paper."}]}, {"title": "Seattle Food Safety: The Strongest Predictor Was a Label the File Contradicts", "meta": "Stanford RegLab Take Home &middot; Algorithmic Fairness &middot; Sep 2026", "badge": "flagged rows agree with their own previous score 1.3% of the time, other rows 95.1% &middot; AUC 0.657 vs 0.639 for the last score on sound records &middot; 0 of 733 priority visits to unflagged restaurants", "tags": ["Python", "scikit-learn", "Algorithmic Fairness", "pandas", "Gradient Boosting", "Logistic Regression", "TF-IDF", "Isotonic Calibration", "Cluster Bootstrap", "Data Auditing", "Policy Evaluation", "Matplotlib", "Jupyter"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">Take home for the Stanford RegLab predoctoral Research Fellow application &nbsp;|&nbsp; 13,299 inspections of 1,756 Seattle restaurants, 2006 to 2013 &nbsp;|&nbsp; &ldquo;previous score&rdquo; agrees with the file&rsquo;s own earlier score in 95.1% of Neither pairs, 1.3% of Asian and Other ethnic pairs &nbsp;|&nbsp; test AUC within Neither: boosting 0.657 [0.631, 0.684], last score 0.639 &nbsp;|&nbsp; every number traces to a notebook cell</div>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/foodsafety_01.png\" alt=\"Horizontal bars showing how often the previous score column equals the score the same file records for that earlier inspection: 95.1 percent of 5,709 back to back pairs for Neither restaurants, 1.3 percent of 3,137 for Asian and 1.3 percent of 1,346 for Other ethnic restaurants\"><figcaption>Figure 1. Back to back inspection periods only. This is bookkeeping, so it should not depend on the kind of restaurant.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>The exercise: predict which Seattle restaurant inspections find a serious food safety violation (a penalty score of 10 or more), using only what is known before the inspection, then add flags for Asian and ethnic cuisines and say whether a health department should use the result. A model like this decides where inspectors go. If its labels are wrong for one group of restaurants, the model turns a data problem into enforcement aimed at that group, and makes it look objective.</p>\n<h4>The Data</h4>\n<p>13,299 inspections of 1,756 restaurants, January 2006 to February 2013. Each row has the inspection&rsquo;s penalty score, the previous score, an average of earlier scores, the Yelp reviews posted since the last inspection, the zip code and the restaurant&rsquo;s Yelp cuisine labels. Two rows carry a -1 placeholder and are dropped.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The cutoff sits low.</strong> 67.4% of inspections are serious and 21.7% score exactly 0. Always guessing serious is 67.6% accurate on the test years, so I report AUC and precision among the riskiest 20% instead of accuracy.</li>\n<li><strong>Scores stick to restaurants.</strong> The correlation with the previous score is 0.707, and the restaurant explains 75.1% of the variance in scores, so the test splits by time and the intervals resample whole restaurants.</li>\n<li><strong>Yelp barely moves.</strong> The score&rsquo;s correlations with review count, non positive reviews and rating all fall between -0.040 and 0, in line with Altenburger and Ho (2019).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The Label Check</h4>\n<p>A restaurant is Asian if any cuisine label is Asian in the lookup, and ethnic likewise. Every Asian cuisine is also coded ethnic, so I use three groups: Asian (499 restaurants), Other ethnic (234) and Neither (1,022), which are cuisine categories, not the owners&rsquo; ethnicity. Asian and Other ethnic restaurants average about 60 points and 99.5% and 99.4% of their inspections are serious. Neither restaurants average 10 points and 42.2% are serious. That gap is bigger than real differences between kitchens should produce, so before modeling I checked the file against itself:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bookkeeping.</strong> When an inspection period starts on the day the previous one ended, the &ldquo;previous score&rdquo; column should equal the score the file records for that earlier inspection. It does in 95.1% of Neither pairs and 1.3% of Asian and Other ethnic pairs, at every score level (Figure 1).</li>\n<li><strong>Rounding.</strong> Neither scores bunch on multiples of 5. Above 20, Asian and Other ethnic scores land on them only 19% to 26% of the time.</li>\n<li><strong>Persistence.</strong> For Neither restaurants a score tracks the same restaurant&rsquo;s earlier score (correlation 0.245), and the previous score column tracks that earlier score closely (0.921). For the other two groups every one of these correlations is between -0.066 and 0.002. This also rules out a leak: the previous score column never tracks the current score more than the real earlier score does.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Biased inspectors could make a group&rsquo;s scores higher and noisier, but not make the file&rsquo;s own columns disagree with each other. The flagged rows behave as if their scores had been replaced with random numbers centered near 60. The file cannot say where that happened: in the source records, in processing, or in preparing the exercise.</p>\n<h4>What the Models Found</h4>\n<p>Train on 2006 to 2010, choose settings and calibrate on 2011, test once on 2012 to February 2013 (3,666 inspections). Brackets are 95% intervals from 500 bootstrap resamples of whole restaurants.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Ranking</th><th>Test AUC, all</th><th>Test AUC, Neither</th><th>Precision in riskiest 20%, Neither</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Previous score</td><td>0.870</td><td>0.639 [0.613, 0.662]</td><td>0.594</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Cuisine flag only</td><td>0.836</td><td>0.500</td><td>0.418</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Logistic regression</td><td>0.885</td><td>0.651 [0.626, 0.677]</td><td>0.594</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Gradient boosting</td><td>0.886</td><td>0.657 [0.631, 0.684]</td><td>0.617</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Review text (TF-IDF)</td><td>0.845</td><td>0.576 [0.549, 0.603]</td><td>0.489</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/foodsafety_02.png\" alt=\"Test AUC with 95 percent intervals for five rankings. Over all inspections: previous score 0.870, cuisine flag only 0.836, logistic regression 0.885, gradient boosting 0.886, review text 0.845. Within Neither restaurants: 0.639, 0.500, 0.651, 0.657 and 0.576\"><figcaption>Figure 2. Test AUC with 95% intervals from resampling whole restaurants. Gray is all inspections, blue is Neither restaurants only.</figcaption></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most of the headline AUC is the flag.</strong> The cuisine flag alone gets 0.836, and boosting adds only 0.016 to ranking by the last score. Boosting gives every Asian and Other ethnic test inspection the same probability, 0.953.</li>\n<li><strong>Within Neither the gain is modest.</strong> Boosting beats the last score by 0.018 and the intervals overlap. Its riskiest 20% finds a serious violation 61.7% of the time, against 40.5% at random.</li>\n<li><strong>History alone does as well as everything.</strong> Refit on one input group at a time, boosting on history alone gets 0.894 overall and 0.685 within Neither. Yelp alone gets 0.515. Dropping the cuisine flags changes nothing (0.889 and 0.671).</li>\n<li><strong>The text model is a cuisine detector.</strong> The words pushing it toward serious are rice, sushi, mexican, pho, chinese, teriyaki and thai. The words pushing it away are pizza, subway, burger, sandwich and pie.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Who Gets Inspected</h4>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/foodsafety_03.png\" alt=\"Stacked bars of the share of a 20 percent inspection budget each group receives. Ranked overall, Asian restaurants get 70 percent of the 733 visits and Other ethnic 30 percent, finding 730 serious violations, and Neither restaurants get none. Taking the riskiest 20 percent within each group gives Asian 32 percent, Other ethnic 14 percent and Neither 54 percent, finding 580\"><figcaption>Figure 3. A budget of 20% of test inspections, ranked by the boosting model.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Following the model sends all 733 visits to Asian and Other ethnic restaurants and none to Neither restaurants, although 40.5% of Neither inspections are serious. Removing the cuisine flags does not help: the model still fills 99.7% of its riskiest 20% with those restaurants, because their history columns are inflated too. Taking the riskiest 20% within each group finds 580 serious violations instead of 730, a loss that only counts if the flagged labels are real.</p>\n<p>Isotonic calibration fitted on 2011 keeps the ranking and brings the Brier score within Neither from 0.228 to 0.224, against 0.241 for the base rate. Predicting the score itself with a Poisson boosting model misses by 12.7 points on average, against 15.9 for &ldquo;same as last time&rdquo;, and ranks inspections almost exactly as the classifier does.</p>\n<h4>Engineering Decisions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Split by time, test once.</strong> Training on the past and testing on the future is how a department would use it, and there was no trend over time to make that unsafe: every full year sat between 65.3% and 69.5% serious.</li>\n<li><strong>Resample restaurants, not rows.</strong> A restaurant&rsquo;s inspections are related, so the intervals weight each restaurant by how often a bootstrap draw picks it.</li>\n<li><strong>Break ties at random with a fixed seed.</strong> Boosting gives every flagged inspection the same score, so without a tie break the riskiest 20% would be chosen by row order.</li>\n<li><strong>Report every result within Neither too.</strong> The flag nearly decides the label for the other two groups, so overall numbers alone would hide the label problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Every number is traced.</strong> The dataset belongs to the exercise and is not redistributed, so the repo checks in CI that each quoted number appears in the output of the notebook cell it cites, and redraws the figures from those saved outputs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What I Would Tell a Health Department</h4>\n<p>A model like this could reasonably move some routine visits from reliably clean places to places with bad recent histories. Trained on these labels, it would send every priority inspection to Asian and ethnic restaurants, it would feed itself (more inspections, more recorded violations), and targeting by cuisine comes close to targeting by national origin. Before using it I would want a random share of inspections, regular checks by group and by inspector, and caps on how far any group&rsquo;s inspection rate can move until someone verifies the gap.</p>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Everything hinges on the labels.</strong> Comparing a sample of rows with King County&rsquo;s public inspection records comes first.</li>\n<li><strong>Offline and one period.</strong> The test assumes skipped inspections would have turned out the same. Rolling the split forward a year at a time would show how stable the result is.</li>\n<li><strong>Prediction is not impact.</strong> Both models predict where violations will be found, not where an inspection would reduce them. That needs random audits or an experiment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The submitted notebook, the trace of every number and the chart code are at <a href=\"https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/seattle-food-safety\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/TanayPratapSingh/seattle-food-safety</a>.</p>", "simple": "<h4>What I Was Asked</h4>\n<p>This was a take home task for a research job at Stanford&rsquo;s RegLab, a lab that studies how governments use data. Seattle health inspectors give restaurants penalty points, and a score of 10 or more counts as a serious violation. Using only what was known before each inspection, I had to predict which inspections would find one, and say whether the health department should use the prediction to decide where to send inspectors.</p>\n<h4>What I Found First</h4>\n<p>The data marked restaurants that serve Asian or other ethnic food. Those restaurants scored about 60 points on average against 10 for the rest, and almost all of their inspections counted as serious. That looked too big to be real, so I checked the file against itself.</p>\n<p>Each row also records the restaurant&rsquo;s previous score, which should match the score the file records for that earlier inspection. For most restaurants it did, 95 times out of 100. For Asian and other ethnic restaurants it matched about once in 100, and their scores jumped around at random from one inspection to the next. Those rows look like random numbers, not real inspection records.</p>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/foodsafety_01.png\" alt=\"Horizontal bars showing how often the previous score column equals the score the same file records for that earlier inspection: 95.1 percent for Neither restaurants, 1.3 percent for Asian and 1.3 percent for Other ethnic restaurants\"><figcaption>How often each row&rsquo;s &ldquo;previous score&rdquo; matches the file&rsquo;s own record of that inspection.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>What the Prediction Was Worth</h4>\n<p>I scored each method by how often it ranks a serious inspection above a clean one (50% is a coin flip). On the restaurants with believable records, the best model managed 66%, against 64% for simply ranking restaurants by their last score. Yelp ratings added nothing, and a model that read the Yelp reviews mostly learned which cuisine a restaurant serves.</p>\n<h4>Why It Matters</h4>\n<p>Suppose the department could only do a fifth of its inspections and followed the model. Every visit would go to Asian and other ethnic restaurants and none to the rest, even though 40% of the other restaurants&rsquo; inspections find serious violations. Removing the cuisine labels did not fix it, because the bad scores also fed into each restaurant&rsquo;s history.</p>\n<figure class=\"project-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/foodsafety_03.png\" alt=\"Stacked bars of who gets the visits with a 20 percent inspection budget. Following the model, Asian restaurants get 70 percent and Other ethnic 30 percent, and Neither restaurants get none. Taking the riskiest 20 percent within each group gives Neither restaurants 54 percent\"><figcaption>Who gets the visits if the department can do a fifth of its inspections.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>My advice was to keep a random share of inspections, check the results by group and by inspector, and cap how fast any group&rsquo;s inspection rate can change until someone confirms the gap is real.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Where the bad scores came from.</strong> It could be the original records, later processing, or the preparation of the exercise. Comparing the file with King County&rsquo;s public records would settle it.</li>\n<li><strong>What would happen for real.</strong> The test assumes the inspections a department skips would have turned out the same, and it predicts where violations will be found, not where an inspection would prevent them.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can inspection history, Yelp and cuisine flags predict which Seattle inspections find a serious violation?", "d": "Stanford RegLab take home. Serious means a penalty score of 10 or more. Only what is known before the inspection may be used, and the answer has to say whether a health department should use it."}, {"k": "data", "t": "13,299 inspections of 1,756 restaurants, 2006 to February 2013", "d": "Each row: penalty score, previous score, average of earlier scores, Yelp reviews since the last inspection, zip code and Yelp cuisine labels. Two rows with a -1 placeholder are dropped, leaving 13,297."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is accuracy a useful measure here?", "d": "67.4% of inspections are serious and 21.7% score exactly 0.", "yes": "Accuracy would be reported.", "no": "Always guessing serious is already 67.6% accurate on the test years, so results use AUC and precision among the riskiest 20%."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Measure how much scores stick to restaurants", "d": "Correlation with the previous score 0.707, and the restaurant explains 75.1% of score variance. So the test splits by time and intervals resample whole restaurants."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Build three cuisine groups from the lookup", "d": "Asian if any label is Asian, ethnic likewise. Every Asian cuisine is also ethnic, so: Asian (499 restaurants), Other ethnic (234) and Neither (1,022). Cuisine categories, not the owners&rsquo; ethnicity."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the gap between the groups plausible?", "d": "Asian and Other ethnic restaurants average about 60 points, and 99.5% and 99.4% of their inspections are serious. Neither averages 10 points and 42.2%.", "yes": "Model it as a real difference between kitchens.", "no": "Too big to take on trust, so check whether the two sets of rows behave like the same kind of record."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Three checks of the file against itself", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "Bookkeeping", "d": "The previous score column equals the file&rsquo;s earlier score in 95.1% of 5,709 back to back Neither pairs and 1.3% of Asian and Other ethnic pairs, at every score level."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Rounding", "d": "Neither scores bunch on multiples of 5. Above 20, Asian and Other ethnic scores land on them only 19% to 26% of the time."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Persistence", "d": "Neither: a score tracks the restaurant&rsquo;s earlier score (0.245) and the previous score column tracks it closely (0.921). The other groups: every correlation between -0.066 and 0.002."}]]}, {"k": "result", "t": "The flagged rows behave like random numbers centered near 60", "d": "Biased inspectors could make scores higher and noisier, but not make the file&rsquo;s own columns disagree. Whether it happened in the source records, in processing or in the exercise cannot be told from the file."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Split by time and test once", "d": "Train 2006 to 2010, tune and calibrate on 2011, test on 2012 to February 2013 (3,666 inspections). Every result is also reported within Neither."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Five rankings scored on the test years", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Baselines", "d": "Rank by the previous score, or by the cuisine flag alone."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Tabular models", "d": "Logistic regression and gradient boosting on history, Yelp activity, zip code and the flags, settings chosen on 2011."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Review text", "d": "TF-IDF over the Yelp reviews with logistic regression."}]]}, {"k": "result", "t": "Over all inspections the flag carries most of the AUC", "d": "Boosting 0.886, last score 0.870, the flag alone 0.836. Boosting gives every flagged test inspection the same probability, 0.953."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Within Neither the gain is modest", "d": "Boosting 0.657 [0.631, 0.684] against 0.639 [0.613, 0.662] for the last score. Its riskiest 20% is serious 61.7% of the time, against 40.5% at random."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do Yelp or the review text add anything?", "d": "Boosting refit on one input group at a time.", "yes": "They would earn a place in the model.", "no": "Yelp alone gets 0.515, while history alone gets 0.894 overall and 0.685 within Neither. The text model&rsquo;s top words are rice, sushi, mexican and pho: a cuisine detector."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Calibrate with isotonic regression fitted on 2011", "d": "Keeps the ranking. Brier score within Neither goes from 0.228 to 0.224, against 0.241 for the base rate."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does dropping the cuisine flags stop the targeting?", "d": "A budget of 20% of test inspections, ranked overall.", "yes": "The flags would be the only channel.", "no": "The model still fills 99.7% of its riskiest 20% with Asian and Other ethnic restaurants, because their history columns are inflated too."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Following the model sends no visits to Neither restaurants", "d": "All 733 visits go to Asian and Other ethnic restaurants (730 serious found), although 40.5% of Neither inspections are serious. The riskiest 20% within each group finds 580."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Predict the score itself", "d": "Poisson boosting misses by 12.7 points on average against 15.9 for &ldquo;same as last time&rdquo;, and ranks almost exactly like the classifier."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Everything hinges on the labels", "d": "Comparing a sample of rows with King County&rsquo;s public inspection records comes first."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Offline, one period, prediction not impact", "d": "Assumes skipped inspections would have turned out the same, and predicts where violations are found, not where an inspection would reduce them."}, {"k": "end", "t": "What to tell the department", "d": "Keep a random share of inspections, check results by group and by inspector, and cap how far any group&rsquo;s inspection rate can move until the gap is verified. Targeting by cuisine comes close to targeting by national origin."}]}, {"title": "Tool Dependency Graph: Deriving Agent Call Order from 1,391 Tool Schemas", "meta": "Agent Infrastructure &middot; Static Analysis &middot; Sep 2026", "badge": "1,391 tools &middot; 46 typed entities &middot; 3,278 edges &middot; 8 of 8 assertions passing", "tags": ["TypeScript", "JSON Schema", "Agent Tooling", "Tool Orchestration", "Graph Construction", "Static Analysis", "Cytoscape", "Bun", "Data Modeling"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">1,391 tools across two large toolkits &nbsp;|&nbsp; 46 typed entities &nbsp;|&nbsp; 3,278 edges &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1,768 of 1,770 typed required inputs have a producer &nbsp;|&nbsp; every edge traces to a declared output field</div>\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>Agents fail on tool calls for a boring reason. The call needs an argument the agent has no way to produce. A reply tool wants a <code>thread_id</code>, and nobody has one memorised: it is an opaque handle that only exists because some earlier call returned it. An agent that does not know this ordering either hallucinates the handle or asks the user for something the user cannot know either.</p>\n<p>So the useful artifact is a map of which tools have to run first, and which arguments have to come from a person. This builds that map from nothing but the tools' JSON Schemas.</p>\n<h4>Why Name Matching Fails</h4>\n<p>The twenty line version matches input parameter names against output field names and draws an edge. It does not work, for three reasons that only appear at catalog scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Generic names collide.</strong> <code>id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>query</code> and <code>state</code> appear on hundreds of unrelated tools, so name matching wires a mail client to a source control API. Every handle is resolved to a namespaced entity instead, scoped to the service that owns the tool.</p>\n<p><strong>A bare <code>id</code> means nothing.</strong> The most common shape in a real catalog is <code>data.threads[].id</code>. The leaf is <code>id</code>; the meaning lives in the parent segment. Discard the path and the single most important producer in the catalog, the thread listing, becomes invisible. Resolution runs in three tiers: the leaf name, then the nearest meaningful ancestor, then the producing tool's own object.</p>\n<p><strong>Almost every response embeds almost every object.</strong> A workflow run response contains a full repository, so taken literally a cancel tool \"produces\" a repository name. True and useless. Producers are split into primary and incidental by the head noun of the tool's object phrase, and incidental ones are suppressed whenever a primary exists. English compounds are head final, which is what makes this work:</p>\n<table>\n  <tr><th>Slug</th><th>Head noun</th><th>Yields</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>CREATE_A_REPOSITORY_VARIABLE</td><td>variable</td><td>not a repo</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>CREATE_AN_ORGANIZATION_REPOSITORY</td><td>repository</td><td>a repo</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>CREATE_AN_ISSUE_COMMENT</td><td>comment</td><td>not an issue</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>All three are wrong under token matching, and the first two point in opposite directions despite both containing the word repository.</p>\n<h4>Why the Graph Is Bipartite</h4>\n<p>The natural encoding is an edge from every producing tool to every consuming tool. At this scale it collapses: one entity, the repository owner, is required by hundreds of tools and produced by dozens, so that entity alone emits tens of thousands of edges and the picture says nothing.</p>\n<p>Tools and entities are both nodes instead. A listing tool produces <code>gmail.thread_id</code>; <code>gmail.thread_id</code> is required by the reply tool. Edge count drops from producers times consumers to producers plus consumers, the hub structure becomes legible, and the two hop path is exactly the answer to \"what do I run first\".</p>\n\n<h4>What the Numbers Say, and What They Do Not</h4>\n<p>Of 2,728 required inputs across the catalog, 1,770 resolve to a typed entity, and 1,768 of those have at least one tool that can supply them. The headline is 99.9 percent, but that is coverage of the inputs that resolved to an entity, not of all required inputs. Across all required inputs, 65 percent are typed handles and almost all of those are satisfiable. The rest are content a person writes, paging knobs, enums, and 568 handle shaped parameters the registry does not cover yet.</p>\n<p>Those 568 are reported rather than hidden. They are how the entities for repository id, project id, runner id and invitation id were found in the first place: the unresolved bucket is the registry's own to do list.</p>\n<p>A slug based inference fallback was built for tools with no output schema. It fired zero times. Real output schemas covered every tool that needed one, so every edge in the graph traces to a declared field rather than a guess.</p>\n<h4>How It Is Checked</h4>\n<p>Eight hand written assertions, run with one command. All eight pass now, and every one of them started out failing. Four were real bugs that only surfaced because the assertions were written against the two dependency shapes the project exists to get right, rather than against whatever the code already did:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>the bare <code>id</code> under <code>threads[]</code>, which hid the most important producer in the catalog</li>\n  <li>cross service producers leaking into a plan, so a mail tool was told to call a source control tool to find an email address</li>\n  <li>requiredness not compounding through an optional parent, so fields inside an optional attachment object were reported as required user input</li>\n  <li>the head noun error above, which credited a tool that creates a variable with creating a repository</li>\n</ul>\n<p>None of the four were visible from reading the code. They appeared the moment the output was compared against a case someone had written down in advance.</p>", "simple": "<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>AI assistants get things done by calling tools: list my email threads, reply to this thread. Many tools need an input that only another tool can supply. To reply to an email thread, the assistant needs the thread&rsquo;s ID, a code that exists only because an earlier call listed the threads. An assistant that does not know this order either makes the code up or asks the person for something the person does not know either.</p>\n<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>I built a map of which tools have to run first, and which inputs have to come from a person. It covers 1,391 tools from two large toolkits, and it uses nothing but each tool&rsquo;s written spec: the list of what it takes in and what it gives back.</p>\n<p>The obvious shortcut is to connect any tool whose output has the same name as another tool&rsquo;s input. At this size the shortcut breaks in three ways, and the map handles each one:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Common names collide.</strong> Words like &ldquo;id&rdquo; and &ldquo;name&rdquo; appear on hundreds of unrelated tools, so the shortcut wires a mail tool to a source control tool. I tie each input to one kind of thing owned by one service, such as a Gmail thread ID.</li>\n<li><strong>A bare &ldquo;id&rdquo; says nothing.</strong> What the ID belongs to is usually written one level up, as in &ldquo;the ID of each item in the list of threads&rdquo;. The map reads that context.</li>\n<li><strong>Replies contain everything.</strong> A tool that cancels a workflow run sends back a whole repository record, but no one calls it to get a repository. The map reads the tool&rsquo;s name to find its main product. In &ldquo;create an issue comment&rdquo;, the last noun, comment, is what the tool makes, not the issue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tools and the things they pass around are separate points on the map. A listing tool points to &ldquo;thread ID&rdquo;, and &ldquo;thread ID&rdquo; points to the reply tool, so following two steps tells the assistant what to run first. Connecting every supplier straight to every user would draw tens of thousands of lines for one popular input alone.</p>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<p>Of all the inputs the tools require, 65 percent are handles like the thread ID, and 1,768 of the 1,770 handles can be supplied by some other tool. Every connection traces to an output a tool says it returns. I built a backup that guessed outputs from tool names, for tools with no output description, and it was never needed.</p>\n<p>Before trusting the map I wrote eight checks by hand, each a case it had to get right. Every one failed at first, and four of the failures were real bugs, including a mail tool being told to call a source control tool to find an email address. None of the four was visible from reading the code.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<p>The near total coverage applies only to inputs the map could classify as handles, not to every required input. The rest are text a person writes, settings for paging through results, fixed lists of options, and 568 inputs that look like handles but that the map does not cover yet. Those 568 are reported rather than hidden, and they are how I found several of the handle types it covers now.</p>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Which tools must an agent call first, and which arguments must a person supply?", "d": "Agents fail when a call needs an argument they cannot produce. A reply tool wants a <code>thread_id</code> that only exists because an earlier call returned it."}, {"k": "data", "t": "1,391 tool JSON Schemas from two large toolkits", "d": "Static analysis only: the map is built from each tool's declared parameters and output schema, nothing else."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does matching input names to output field names give a usable graph?", "d": "This is the twenty line version: draw an edge wherever a parameter name equals an output field name.", "yes": "Twenty lines would be the whole project.", "no": "It fails at catalog scale for three reasons, each fixed by one of the next three stages."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Namespace every handle by the service that owns the tool", "d": "Generic names like <code>id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>query</code> and <code>state</code> sit on hundreds of unrelated tools. Each handle resolves to a scoped entity such as <code>gmail.thread_id</code>, so a mail client is never wired to a source control API."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the leaf field name resolve to an entity?", "d": "The common shape is <code>data.threads[].id</code>: the leaf says <code>id</code>, the meaning sits in the parent segment.", "yes": "Use the leaf name.", "no": "Fall back to the nearest meaningful ancestor, then to the producing tool's own object."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the head noun of the tool's object phrase name this entity?", "d": "Almost every response embeds almost every object, so read at face value a cancel tool &ldquo;produces&rdquo; a repository name. English compounds are head final: <code>CREATE_A_REPOSITORY_VARIABLE</code> makes a variable, not a repo.", "yes": "The tool is a primary producer of the entity.", "no": "It is incidental, and suppressed whenever a primary producer exists."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Build a bipartite graph: tools and entities are both nodes", "d": "A listing tool produces <code>gmail.thread_id</code>, which the reply tool requires. Edges drop from producers times consumers to producers plus consumers, and the two hop path answers what to run first."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the tool declare an output schema?", "d": "A slug based inference fallback was built for tools without one.", "yes": "Edges come from the declared output fields.", "no": "The fallback would guess from the slug. It fired zero times, so every edge traces to a declared field."}, {"k": "result", "t": "46 typed entities and 3,278 edges", "d": "The graph built from the 1,391 tool schemas, with every edge tracing to a declared output field."}, {"k": "result", "t": "1,768 of 1,770 typed required inputs have a producer", "d": "Of 2,728 required inputs, 1,770 resolve to a typed entity, so 65 percent of required inputs are typed handles and almost all are satisfiable."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "99.9 percent covers resolved inputs only", "d": "The headline is coverage of the inputs that resolved to an entity, not of all 2,728 required inputs. The rest are content a person writes, paging knobs, enums, and unresolved handles."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "568 handle shaped parameters are not covered yet", "d": "They are reported rather than hidden. This bucket is how the entities for repository id, project id, runner id and invitation id were found: the registry's own to do list."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do the eight hand written assertions pass?", "d": "Written against the two dependency shapes the project exists to get right, not against whatever the code already did. One command runs them.", "yes": "All eight pass now.", "no": "Every one started out failing, and four of the failures were real bugs."}, {"k": "split", "t": "The four real bugs the assertions caught", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "Bare <code>id</code> under <code>threads[]</code>", "d": "Dropping the parent path hid the thread listing, the most important producer in the catalog."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Cross service producers leaking into a plan", "d": "A mail tool was told to call a source control tool to find an email address."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Requiredness not compounding through an optional parent", "d": "Fields inside an optional attachment object were reported as required user input."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "The head noun error", "d": "A tool that creates a variable was credited with creating a repository."}]]}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "The schemas alone yield a call order for almost every typed handle, and every edge traces to a declared field. Coverage is of resolved inputs, 568 handles remain open, and none of the four bugs showed up from reading the code."}]}, {"title": "Distributed ViT on CIFAR-10: Proving a Speedup, Then Explaining Why It Vanished", "meta": "Distributed Training &middot; Systems Measurement &middot; Sep 2026", "badge": "98% &rarr; 57% scaling efficiency &middot; cause isolated &middot; 24 measured runs", "tags": ["PyTorch", "DDP", "FSDP", "NCCL", "Mixed Precision", "torch.compile", "Vision Transformer", "CUDA", "Benchmarking", "Apple MPS"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">2x Tesla T4, NCCL &nbsp;|&nbsp; DDP in fp32: 1.96x at 98% efficiency &nbsp;|&nbsp; identical config in fp16: 1.15x at 57% &nbsp;|&nbsp; cause isolated to a 4 vCPU host &nbsp;|&nbsp; 24 measured runs across 3 hardware groups</div>\n\n          <h4>The Problem</h4>\n          <p>\n            Almost every distributed training result you read is unfalsifiable. A repository reports a 1.9x speedup on two GPUs and there is no way to tell whether the two rank job computed the same gradient as the one rank job, whether the baseline was tuned as carefully as the treatment, or whether the number came from a machine doing something else at the time. A speedup you cannot verify is only a claim. This project was built so that every claim it makes is checkable, and its main finding is a regression in its own scaling.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>What Gets Measured, and Against What</h4>\n          <p>\n            Three hardware groups: an Apple M5 laptop with one GPU, multi process gloo over that laptop's 10 CPU cores, and 2x Tesla T4 on Kaggle with NCCL. Speedup is only ever computed inside one group, because a ratio between a T4 and an M5 is arithmetic, not a measurement. A rank scaling baseline must differ from its run in exactly one property: the rank count. Precision, compilation, gradient accumulation, dataloader worker count, epoch count and dataset fraction all have to match, and the reporting layer enforces that rather than trusting the author. That constraint was added after the dashboard was caught dividing a real data run by a synthetic data baseline, which silently reported 1.11x where the matched comparison is 1.15x.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Model and Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            A vision transformer written out rather than imported, 1,806,538 parameters, patch size 4 on 32&times;32 inputs giving 64 tokens plus a class token. It is small on purpose, because the model must not be the bottleneck in a throughput measurement, and at this size the gradient payload for each all reduce is about 7 MB. CIFAR-10, 50,000 train and 10,000 test, 30 epochs, no pretraining.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            The canonical dataset host was unreachable from the development network, failing with an SSL EOF before any payload arrived. The fallback deliberately does not rebuild the original pickle layout, because those files carry published md5 sums a reconstruction cannot match, and the only way to make that path appear to work would be to disable torchvision's integrity checking. Silencing a checksum to make a download look successful is how results stop being trustworthy. The mirror writes its own format instead, and every run artifact records which source it used.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Configuration</h4>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Architecture</td><td>ViT, dim 192, depth 6, heads 3, MLP ratio 2.0</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Regularization</td><td>Stochastic depth 0.1, label smoothing 0.1, grad clip 1.0</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Optimizer</td><td>AdamW, betas (0.9, 0.95), no decay on norms, biases, class token, position embedding</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Schedule</td><td>Linear warmup 2 epochs, then cosine, computed per step</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Learning rate</td><td>1e-3, scaled linearly by world size</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Batch size</td><td>128 per rank</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Precision</td><td>Selected from compute capability, not from <code>is_bf16_supported</code></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Runtime</td><td>torch 2.10.0+cu128, NCCL on CUDA, gloo on CPU, FileStore rendezvous on macOS</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <h4>Correctness Before Speed</h4>\n          <p>\n            DDP averages gradients across ranks, so two ranks each holding batch B must produce the same gradient as one process holding batch 2B. If that identity does not hold, every throughput number in the project describes a system training a different model than its own baseline, and the comparison is void. The suite spawns a real gloo process group and checks it to <code>rtol=1e-4</code>. It ships with a second test that deliberately computes the wrong thing, because a correctness test that cannot fail is decoration rather than evidence.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/dvit_03.png\" alt=\"Terminal showing the DDP gradient equivalence test and its inverse guard both passing\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 1. The gradient equivalence test and its inverse guard, run in a real process group. The dashboard is then regenerated from 24 run artifacts.</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Scaling on 2x Tesla T4</h4>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Run</th><th>Ranks</th><th>Strategy</th><th>Precision</th><th>img/s</th><th>Rank speedup</th><th>Peak MB</th><th>Top-1</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws1-fp32</code></td><td>1</td><td>single</td><td>fp32</td><td>1,853</td><td>baseline</td><td>553</td><td>80.70%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws1-amp</code></td><td>1</td><td>single</td><td>fp16</td><td>3,810</td><td>baseline</td><td>347</td><td>80.52%</td></tr>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><code>t4-ws2-ddp-fp32</code></td><td>2</td><td>DDP</td><td>fp32</td><td><strong>3,637</strong></td><td><strong>1.96x (98%)</strong></td><td>561</td><td>80.01%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws2-ddp-amp</code></td><td>2</td><td>DDP</td><td>fp16</td><td>4,376</td><td>1.15x (57%)</td><td>354</td><td>79.71%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws2-ddp-amp-compiled</code></td><td>2</td><td>DDP</td><td>fp16 + compile</td><td>4,706</td><td>no baseline</td><td>345</td><td>80.09%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws2-ddp-amp-ga2</code></td><td>2</td><td>DDP</td><td>fp16, accum 2</td><td>4,393</td><td>no baseline</td><td>361</td><td>80.15%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>t4-ws2-fsdp-amp</code></td><td>2</td><td>FSDP</td><td>fp16</td><td>2,884</td><td>0.76x</td><td>338</td><td>79.41%</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <p>\n            Three predictions were written down before this ran, so they could be scored instead of retrofitted. FSDP was predicted to lose, and it does: 2,884 img/s against DDP's 4,376, buying 5% of peak memory for 34% of throughput. At 1,806,538 parameters there is almost nothing to shard. Mixed precision was predicted to be a large win, and it is: 2.06x at one rank with peak memory falling from 553 MB to 347 MB.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>The Prediction That Was Wrong</h4>\n          <p>\n            The third prediction said DDP would land near 1.8x rather than 2x, reasoning that a small model makes the all reduce expensive relative to compute. That is backwards. A small model means <em>small gradient tensors</em>, about 7 MB, which DDP overlaps with the backward pass almost entirely. In fp32 it scales at 1.96x, 98% efficiency.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            The same DDP configuration in fp16 scales at 1.15x, 57% efficiency. Hardware, model and communication volume are identical, and the only thing that changed is that each GPU now computes roughly twice as fast. Optimising one layer moved the bottleneck rather than shortening the job, and scaling efficiency turned out not to be a property of the cluster at all.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Isolating the Cause</h4>\n          <p>\n            Two suspects: the NCCL all reduce, and the input pipeline. Raising the dataloader worker count and watching what happens would produce a correlation, not a cause. Instead a synthetic input mode serves pre normalised tensors from a small in memory pool, removing decode, augmentation and worker processes entirely, so two ranks against one rank under it is compute plus communication with nothing else in the measurement.\n          </p>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Input path</th><th>1 rank</th><th>2 ranks</th><th>Speedup</th><th>Efficiency</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>Synthetic, no dataloader</strong></td><td>3,956</td><td><strong>7,580</strong></td><td><strong>1.92x</strong></td><td><strong>96%</strong></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Real data, 2 workers per rank</td><td>3,462</td><td>4,073</td><td>1.18x</td><td>59%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Real data, 8 workers per rank</td><td>3,599</td><td>3,996</td><td>1.11x</td><td>56%</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <p>\n            The collective is innocent. With no dataloader the same configuration scales at 1.92x, 96% efficiency. With real data, throughput never passes about 4,073 img/s no matter how many GPUs or workers are thrown at it. The artifacts record <code>cpu_cores: 4</code>. One T4 in fp16 already consumes 3,956 img/s of synthetic input, so 4 vCPUs cannot feed even one GPU comfortably, let alone two. The second T4 is unfed.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            Raising workers from 2 to 8 per rank made it <em>worse</em>, 3,996 against 4,073, because 16 worker processes on 4 cores buy context switches and nothing else. The synthetic runs also index a small pool, so their 96% is an upper bound on what fixing the input path could achieve, not a promise.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/dvit_01.png\" alt=\"Median throughput for every measured run across three hardware groups\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 2. Median throughput across all measured runs. Synthetic input runs are marked, because the fastest bar is an instrument reading and not a training result.</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Precision Advice Does Not Survive a Backend Change</h4>\n          <p>\n            On the T4, enabling mixed precision is a 2.06x win. On Apple silicon the identical flag is a regression: 726 img/s against 1,061 in fp32, a 32% slowdown, because eager mode pays for a cast around every operator and the hardware is already efficient at fp32. Adding <code>torch.compile</code> to that same fp16 configuration reaches 2,361 img/s, 2.23x the fp32 baseline, once the casts are fused into the graph. Wall clock for the full 30 epoch run falls from 24 minutes to 11.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            A related trap is that on a T4, <code>torch.cuda.is_bf16_supported()</code> returns True. The card is sm_75 and has no hardware bf16, so that True reflects emulation. Trusting it selected an emulated path for every mixed precision run, and torch inductor quietly logged that the card does not support bfloat16 natively while the benchmark carried on reporting the result as mixed precision. Precision is now selected from compute capability, and every artifact records the dtype that was actually used.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Accuracy as the Control</h4>\n          <p>\n            Across all seven T4 configurations, top-1 spans 79.41% to 80.70%, and the three Apple silicon runs land within 0.16 points of each other. That spread is the control. These are throughput changes, so accuracy should barely move. If mixed precision or compilation had shifted it materially, the correct output would have been a bug report rather than a results table.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/dvit_02.png\" alt=\"Test accuracy by epoch for the full length runs, curves nearly overlapping\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 3. Test accuracy by epoch. The curves overlay because precision and compilation change throughput, not learning. The reporting layer excludes short and synthetic runs from this view.</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Nine Bugs, Kept On Purpose</h4>\n          <p>\n            The defect log is the most reusable output of the build, so it ships in the repository rather than being tidied away. Four of the nine came from an API answering a slightly different question than the one being asked: <code>is_bf16_supported</code> counting emulation, torchvision's md5 check rejecting a legitimate mirror, MPS reporting instantaneous memory where a peak was wanted, and <code>sys.argv[0]</code> producing a command that could not reproduce its own run. One was a hang rather than a crash. The default TCP rendezvous is blocked on some hosts and the job waits, which reads exactly like slow training and costs hours before anyone suspects the launcher. Three were wrong numbers on the dashboard, all found by pointing a screen recorder at a page nobody had read closely.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            That last category is why the honesty rule is now enforced rather than promised. A gate extracts every number carrying a measurement unit from the documentation and fails if it does not round, at the precision it was written with, to an artifact value or to a ratio between two artifact values on the same hardware. It found five unsupported claims the first time it ran.\n          </p>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Operational Reading</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              The same cluster, model and communication volume delivered 98% efficiency in fp32 (the 1.96x) and 57% in fp16, and the difference had nothing to do with the distributed layer. Anyone tuning NCCL in response to that drop would have spent a week in the wrong subsystem. Scaling efficiency is a property of the ratio between accelerator speed and host speed, and optimising the accelerator is what breaks it. The measurement that settles it took six short runs and one flag.\n            </p>\n          </div>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>I trained an image recognition model on two graphics cards (GPUs) at once and measured how much faster that was than using one. The model is a vision transformer, a kind of AI model that reads a picture as a grid of small patches. I wrote it out myself and kept it small on purpose, so the model would not be the thing slowing training down. It learned from a standard set of small labelled photos.</p>\n<p>Most published speedups of this kind cannot be checked, so I built this one to be checkable. Before timing anything, I tested that two cards splitting a batch of photos compute the same update to the model as one card handling the whole batch. If they did not, the two setups would be training different models and the speed comparison would mean nothing. I also wrote a second test that is supposed to fail, to prove the first one can catch a mistake.</p>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<p>With standard precision numbers, two cards ran 1.96 times as fast as one, close to the ideal of double. Then I switched to a smaller number format (fp16) that lets each card work about twice as fast. With that one change, the gain from adding the second card fell to 1.15 times.</p>\n<p>There were two suspects: the step where the two cards share their results, and the loading of photos. To separate them, I fed the model fake input that was already sitting in memory, so no time went into loading and preparing photos. With fake input, two cards reached 1.92 times the speed of one, so the sharing step was fine. The machine had only 4 CPU cores, the general purpose processors that load and prepare each photo, and they could not comfortably keep even one fast card supplied. The second card was waiting for data. Adding more helper processes to prepare photos made it slower, because they only competed for the same cores.</p>\n<p>Accuracy barely moved across all of these runs, which is what should happen when only speed settings change. If it had moved, I would have treated it as a bug.</p>\n<h4>How I Kept It Honest</h4>\n<p>Speeds are only compared between runs on the same machine that differ in one setting: the number of cards. I kept a log of the bugs found along the way, including one where the software said the cards supported a number format they have no hardware for, so they quietly imitated it. I also added a check that fails if any number in the write up does not match a recorded run. It found five unsupported claims the first time it ran.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The 1.92x is a ceiling.</strong> The fake input runs reuse a small pool of ready made inputs, so they show the most that fixing the photo loading could give, not what a fix would deliver.</li>\n<li><strong>The fp16 advice does not travel.</strong> On an Apple laptop the same switch made training slower, until I added a compile step that removed the extra number conversions.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Does splitting ViT training across two GPUs give a speedup you can verify?", "d": "Most distributed training speedups cannot be checked. This project makes every claim checkable, and its main finding is a regression in its own scaling."}, {"k": "data", "t": "CIFAR-10 from a mirror", "d": "50,000 train and 10,000 test images, 30 epochs, no pretraining. The canonical host failed with an SSL EOF, so a mirror writes its own format rather than rebuilding the md5 checked pickle layout, and every artifact records its source."}, {"k": "step", "t": "A small vision transformer, written out rather than imported", "d": "1,806,538 parameters; patch size 4 on 32&times;32 inputs gives 64 tokens plus a class token. Small on purpose so the model is not the throughput bottleneck. Each all reduce carries about 7 MB of gradients."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Three hardware groups, 24 measured runs", "lanes": [[{"k": "data", "t": "Apple M5 laptop, one GPU", "d": "PyTorch on the MPS backend, with FileStore rendezvous on macOS."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "gloo over the laptop's 10 CPU cores", "d": "Multi process training on CPU only."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "2x Tesla T4 on Kaggle with NCCL", "d": "The group where the scaling results come from."}]]}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the baseline differ from its run only in rank count?", "d": "Precision, compilation, accumulation, workers, epochs and dataset fraction must match, and speedup is computed only inside one hardware group. The reporting layer enforces this.", "yes": "A rank speedup is reported.", "no": "No speedup is reported. The rule was added after the dashboard divided a real data run by a synthetic data baseline and showed 1.11x where the matched figure is 1.15x."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do two ranks with batch B match one rank with batch 2B?", "d": "DDP averages gradients across ranks. A real gloo process group checks the identity to <code>rtol=1e-4</code>, next to an inverse test that computes the wrong thing on purpose.", "yes": "Throughput numbers compare the same model as their baseline.", "no": "Every throughput comparison would describe a different model and be void."}, {"k": "result", "t": "FSDP loses to DDP, as predicted in advance", "d": "On 2x T4 in fp16: 2,884 img/s against DDP's 4,376, saving 5% of peak memory at the cost of 34% of throughput. At 1,806,538 parameters there is almost nothing to shard."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Mixed precision is a large win on one T4, as predicted", "d": "fp16 gives 2.06x the fp32 throughput at one rank (3,810 against 1,853 img/s), and peak memory falls from 553 MB to 347 MB."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does DDP land near the predicted 1.8x?", "d": "The prediction reasoned that a small model makes the all reduce expensive relative to compute.", "yes": "Communication would be the cost, as reasoned.", "no": "The reasoning was backwards. Small gradient tensors, about 7 MB, overlap with the backward pass almost entirely."}, {"k": "result", "t": "DDP in fp32: 1.96x at 98% efficiency", "d": "3,637 img/s on two T4s against 1,853 on one, with top-1 of 80.01% against 80.70%."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Same DDP config in fp16: 1.15x at 57% efficiency", "d": "4,376 img/s against 3,810 on one rank. Hardware, model and communication volume are identical; the only change is that each GPU computes roughly twice as fast."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Synthetic input mode", "d": "Serves pre normalised tensors from a small in memory pool, removing decode, augmentation and worker processes, so two ranks against one measures compute plus communication and nothing else."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the NCCL all reduce the bottleneck?", "d": "Compare scaling with the input pipeline removed against scaling with it in place.", "yes": "Synthetic input runs would scale poorly too.", "no": "With synthetic input, 7,580 img/s on two ranks against 3,956 on one: 1.92x at 96%. The collective is innocent."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Real data, by dataloader worker count", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "2 workers per rank: 1.18x at 59%", "d": "3,462 img/s on one rank, 4,073 on two."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "8 workers per rank: 1.11x at 56%", "d": "3,599 on one rank, 3,996 on two. Worse, because 16 worker processes on 4 cores buy context switches and nothing else."}]]}, {"k": "result", "t": "Cause isolated: a 4 vCPU host", "d": "Artifacts record <code>cpu_cores: 4</code>. One T4 in fp16 consumes 3,956 img/s of synthetic input, so 4 vCPUs cannot feed even one GPU comfortably. Real data never passes about 4,073 img/s. The second T4 is unfed."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The synthetic 96% is an upper bound", "d": "Synthetic runs index a small in memory pool, so their 96% bounds what fixing the input path could achieve. It is not a promise of what a fix delivers."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the mixed precision win carry over to Apple silicon?", "d": "The same fp16 flag, run on the M5.", "yes": "fp16 would help there as it does on the T4.", "no": "726 img/s against 1,061 in fp32, a 32% slowdown, because eager mode pays for a cast around every operator."}, {"k": "result", "t": "M5 fp16 with torch.compile: 2.23x the fp32 baseline", "d": "2,361 img/s once the casts are fused into the graph. Wall clock for the full 30 epoch run falls from 24 minutes to 11."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Precision selected from compute capability", "d": "On a T4 (sm_75, no hardware bf16) <code>is_bf16_supported()</code> returns True through emulation, which sent every mixed precision run down an emulated path. Each artifact now records the dtype used."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does accuracy hold still across configurations?", "d": "These are throughput changes, so accuracy is the control.", "yes": "T4 top-1 spans 79.41% to 80.70% over seven configurations; the three Apple silicon runs land within 0.16 points of each other.", "no": "A material shift would have produced a bug report instead of a results table."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Defect log of nine bugs, kept in the repository", "d": "Four were APIs answering a slightly different question, one was a hang from a blocked TCP rendezvous that looks like slow training, and three were wrong numbers on the dashboard."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does every documented number trace to an artifact?", "d": "A gate extracts each number with a unit and checks that it rounds to an artifact value or a ratio between two artifact values on the same hardware.", "yes": "The documentation passes.", "no": "The gate fails. It found five unsupported claims the first time it ran."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "Same cluster, model and communication volume gave 98% efficiency in fp32 and 57% in fp16. The drop comes from the ratio of accelerator speed to host speed, not the distributed layer. The synthetic 96% is a ceiling."}]}, {"title": "ZTF Transient Pipeline: Real Time Astronomical Alert Classification and Warehouse", "meta": "Streaming ML &middot; Analytics Engineering &middot; Jul 2026", "badge": "27,378 real ZTF alerts &middot; multimodal CNN &middot; dbt: 8 models, 21 tests", "tags": ["Python", "Apache Kafka", "Avro", "TensorFlow", "Multimodal CNN", "dbt", "DuckDB", "Apache Airflow", "FastAPI", "Astropy"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">27,378 real Avro alerts (ZTF, Jul 20 2026) &nbsp;|&nbsp; 500 scored end to end &nbsp;|&nbsp; dbt: 8 models, 21 tests, 32 checks passing &nbsp;|&nbsp; two plane architecture &nbsp;|&nbsp; portable DuckDB to BigQuery / Snowflake</div>\n\n          <h4>The Problem</h4>\n          <p>In time domain astronomy the telescope produces far more data than any human can look at, and the overwhelming majority of it is noise. A survey astronomer or alert broker operator needs high purity transient candidates surfaced quickly, and needs to trust that the nightly numbers behind them are correct. Those are two jobs: a fast, accurate model at the front, and a tested, governed data layer behind it. I built ZTF Transient Pipeline to do both in one system.</p>\n\n          <h4>The Sequel to ShopFloor</h4>\n          <p>I designed this project to close the three gaps I listed as limitations in ShopFloor AI, and to do it on production shaped infrastructure. Simulated Kafka becomes real Avro alert data flowing over a real Kafka broker. A synthetic benchmark dataset becomes a public survey stream. The single node app gains a real warehouse, a dbt transformation layer, and drift monitoring.</p>\n\n          <h4>The Data</h4>\n          <p>The Zwicky Transient Facility (ZTF) streams over a million optical alerts per night as Avro packets over Kafka. Each packet embeds three small image cutouts (science, template, and difference) plus a table of measured features. The pipeline downloads a night of real archived alerts and replays them through its own Dockerized Kafka broker. The payloads are real ZTF data; only the timing is replayed. The README says so plainly. Replaying the archive puts real data through a real broker without needing broker whitelisting on the critical path.</p>\n\n          <h4>Streaming ML Plane</h4>\n          <p>fastavro deserializes the alerts, and Astropy handles the astronomical coordinate and time conventions. A multimodal classifier in TensorFlow and Keras scores each alert: a convolutional branch reads the three image cutouts, a tabular branch reads the measured features, and the two are fused to predict whether an alert is a real transient or an artifact, then a coarse class. Rather than invent an accuracy target, the model is framed against BTSbot, a published survey broker classifier that scores detections from the same image plus feature inputs. A FastAPI service exposes the scorer, and a streaming consumer writes predictions and alert metadata into the warehouse.</p>\n\n          <h4>Analytics Engineering Plane</h4>\n          <p>This half is the SQL and analytics engineering work. A dbt project transforms the raw landed alerts through three layers: staging models that clean and type the raw stream, intermediate models that assemble per object lightcurves using window functions, and mart models including an incremental daily alert summary and a model monitoring rollup. The project ships with 21 data quality tests, source freshness checks, and generated lineage docs. It defaults to DuckDB so anyone can run it with zero cloud setup, and the same models port to BigQuery and Snowflake with only a profile change.</p>\n\n          <h4>Orchestration and Monitoring</h4>\n          <p>An Apache Airflow DAG runs the full nightly batch end to end: download, deserialize, load, transform, and monitor. A Population Stability Index drift monitor watches the prediction distribution over time, so a shift in what the model is seeing surfaces as a number. Continuous integration through GitHub Actions runs the test suite and a dbt build on every push, and a Codespaces devcontainer lets the whole thing spin up in the browser.</p>\n\n          <h4>Proven on Real Data</h4>\n          <p>I ran the pipeline against a real night of the survey: 27,378 Avro alert packets extracted from July 20, 2026. Of those, 500 alerts were loaded, scored, and rebuilt through the warehouse. A full <code>dbt build</code> returned 8 models and 21 tests with 32 checks passing and zero errors, on real data rather than a seed sample. The repository is public at <a href=\"https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/ztf-transient-pipeline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/TanayPratapSingh/ztf-transient-pipeline</a>.</p>\n\n          <h4>Limitations</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Replay, not live consumption.</strong> Nightly archive replay uses real data, but live streaming from the survey broker needs an IP whitelist, so it is kept as an optional upgrade rather than a dependency.</li>\n            <li><strong>Coarse labels.</strong> The classifier exercises the pipeline; its labels are not a science grade taxonomy. It is a systems project first.</li>\n            <li><strong>Trial scale compute.</strong> Everything runs single node under Docker Compose, not at production survey volume.</li>\n            <li><strong>Validation against broker labels.</strong> There is no spectroscopic confirmation, and the reference labels are themselves model outputs, so the benchmark is relative rather than ground truth.</li>\n          </ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>The Zwicky Transient Facility is a sky survey telescope that sends out an alert whenever something in the sky changes. It sends over a million alerts a night, far more than anyone can look at, and most of them are noise. An astronomer needs real events picked out quickly, and needs to trust the nightly numbers behind them.</p>\n<p>I built one system for both jobs: a model at the front that sorts alerts, and a tested data store behind it that keeps the numbers right. It follows my earlier ShopFloor AI project and replaces its simulated data with a real public survey stream.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<p>I downloaded one real night of archived alerts and replayed them through my own copy of Kafka, the messaging software the survey uses to send them. The alerts are real; only their timing is replayed. Each alert carries three small pictures (the new image, an older reference image, and the difference between the two) plus a table of measurements.</p>\n<p>A model reads both the pictures and the table, decides whether the alert is a real event or a camera artifact, and gives it a rough category. Instead of inventing an accuracy target, I set it against BTSbot, a published classifier that works from the same kind of inputs.</p>\n<p>The results go into a database, where a series of SQL steps built with a tool called dbt cleans the raw alerts, traces each object&rsquo;s brightness over time, and builds daily summaries. Automated tests check the data along the way, a scheduler runs everything nightly, and a drift monitor flags when the model&rsquo;s predictions start to shift.</p>\n<h4>What I Ran</h4>\n<p>I ran it on a real night of the survey, July 20, 2026: 27,378 alerts. A sample of 500 of them was loaded, scored, and rebuilt through the database. The database build on that sample finished with 32 checks passing and zero errors, on real alerts rather than a small made up test file.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It replays an archived night instead of reading the live feed, which needs the survey to approve my network address.</li>\n<li>Only the 500 alert sample, out of 27,378, went all the way through the model and the database.</li>\n<li>The categories are coarse. The model is there to run the pipeline, not to classify objects to a scientific standard.</li>\n<li>No object was confirmed with a spectrum, the usual way astronomers check what something is, and the labels I compare against are themselves model outputs. The comparison is relative.</li>\n<li>Everything runs on one machine, not at the volume of a real survey.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can one system surface real transients and keep the nightly numbers trustworthy?", "d": "Time domain astronomy produces far more alerts than anyone can look at, mostly noise. The system needs a fast model at the front and a tested, governed data layer behind it."}, {"k": "data", "t": "The ZTF alert stream", "d": "Over a million optical alerts per night as Avro packets over Kafka. Each packet embeds science, template, and difference image cutouts plus a table of measured features."}, {"k": "data", "t": "One archived night: 27,378 real alerts", "d": "Avro alert packets extracted from July 20, 2026."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Replay through a Dockerized Kafka broker", "d": "The payloads are real ZTF data; only the timing is replayed. This keeps broker whitelisting off the critical path."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Deserialize with fastavro and Astropy", "d": "fastavro reads the packets, and Astropy handles the astronomical coordinate and time conventions."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Multimodal classifier in TensorFlow and Keras", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Convolutional branch", "d": "Reads the three image cutouts."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Tabular branch", "d": "Reads the measured features."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Fuse: real transient or artifact, then a coarse class", "d": "The two branches are fused to predict whether an alert is a real transient or an artifact, then a coarse class."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Framed against BTSbot", "d": "Rather than invent an accuracy target, the model is set against BTSbot, a published broker classifier that scores detections from the same image plus feature inputs."}, {"k": "step", "t": "FastAPI scorer and streaming consumer", "d": "A FastAPI service exposes the scorer, and a consumer writes predictions and alert metadata into the warehouse."}, {"k": "split", "t": "dbt layers, on DuckDB by default, portable to BigQuery and Snowflake", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Staging", "d": "Cleans and types the raw landed alert stream."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Intermediate", "d": "Assembles per object lightcurves with window functions."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Marts", "d": "An incremental daily alert summary and a model monitoring rollup."}]]}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do the dbt tests and source freshness checks pass?", "d": "The project ships 21 data quality tests, freshness checks, and generated lineage docs. CI runs the suite and a dbt build on every push.", "yes": "On the 500 alert sample, <code>dbt build</code> returned 8 models and 21 tests with 32 checks passing and zero errors.", "no": "dbt reports the failing test or the stale source."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Airflow runs the nightly batch", "d": "One DAG runs download, deserialize, load, transform, and monitor, end to end."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Has the prediction distribution drifted?", "d": "A Population Stability Index monitor watches the model&rsquo;s predictions over time.", "yes": "The shift surfaces as a PSI number.", "no": "The monitor reports a stable distribution."}, {"k": "result", "t": "27,378 alerts extracted, a sample of 500 scored end to end", "d": "The 500 alerts were loaded, scored, and rebuilt through the warehouse on real data rather than a seed sample."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Replay, not live consumption", "d": "Live streaming from the survey broker needs an IP whitelist, so it stays an optional upgrade."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Coarse labels", "d": "The classifier exercises the pipeline; its labels are not a science grade taxonomy. It is a systems project first."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Broker labels as the benchmark", "d": "There is no spectroscopic confirmation, and the reference labels are themselves model outputs, so the benchmark is relative rather than ground truth."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Trial scale compute", "d": "Everything runs single node under Docker Compose, not at production survey volume."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "Real ZTF alerts flowed through Kafka, a multimodal classifier, and a tested dbt warehouse, for a 500 alert sample from one night. It shows the pipeline works on real data, not a science grade classifier."}]}, {"title": "Groundwork: RAG Evaluation, Guardrails, and an LLM Gateway on Real Data", "meta": "LLM Platform Engineering &middot; RAG Evaluation &middot; Aug 2026", "badge": "recall@5 0.921 &middot; ECE 0.045 &middot; injection recall 0.884 unseen &middot; 113 tests, CI green", "tags": ["Python", "RAG", "LLM Gateway", "Guardrails", "Calibration (ECE)", "Prompt Injection", "BM25", "FastAPI", "Docker", "Kubernetes", "Responsible AI", "CI/CD"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">recall@5 0.921 &nbsp;|&nbsp; ECE 0.045 &nbsp;|&nbsp; injection recall 0.884 on an unseen attack corpus &nbsp;|&nbsp; correctly declines 96.2% of unanswerable questions &nbsp;|&nbsp; 11,873 questions evaluated per run &nbsp;|&nbsp; 113 tests, CI green</div>\n\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>Getting a good answer out of an LLM feature once is the easy part. The hard part is knowing whether the answer is good, knowing when the system should refuse, proving the guardrails do not reject a tenth of your legitimate traffic, and being able to say what a change cost you. Groundwork is those four things built around a small retrieval augmented generation system, so they have something concrete to be true about.</p>\n<p><strong>Every number in the project is measured on real, public, human labelled data.</strong> No corpus was written for the demo, no gold answers were authored to be easy, and no attack was invented to be caught. A fetch script downloads five datasets and records a SHA-256 for each in a manifest. It runs with no API key at all, on a deterministic offline reader, so every figure below is reproducible on a laptop for free.</p>\n\n<h4>Four Concerns, One Codebase</h4>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>What is implemented</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>LLM and RAG evaluation</td><td>Retrieval metrics, SQuAD official answer metrics, abstention scored as a classifier, confidence calibration with reliability diagrams, a three way retrieval ablation, and a release gate that exits non zero</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Guardrails</td><td>Input and output rails, each scored on a published labelled attack corpus and again on an attack corpus it has never seen</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Secure deployment</td><td>Hashed API keys with constant time comparison, a tamper evident audit hash chain, PII redaction measured per entity type, a threat model, and a hardened container</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LLM gateway</td><td>One chokepoint for every model call: auth, tier routing, two layer cache, per tenant budgets and rate limits, retries, graceful degradation, full cost accounting</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>The Data</h4>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Dataset</th><th>Role</th><th>Size</th><th>Licence</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>SQuAD v2.0 dev</td><td>Corpus of 1,204 Wikipedia paragraphs, 11,873 questions, gold answer spans, and 5,945 unanswerable questions</td><td>4.2 MB</td><td>CC BY-SA 4.0</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>deepset/prompt-injections</td><td>Injection rail precision and recall</td><td>662</td><td>CC BY 4.0</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>jackhhao/jailbreak-classification</td><td>Jailbreak rail</td><td>1,306</td><td>See card</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Lakera/gandalf_ignore_instructions</td><td>Held out attacks, never trained on</td><td>1,000</td><td>MIT</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>ai4privacy/pii-masking-200k</td><td>PII redaction recall per entity type</td><td>1,000</td><td>See card</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p>The full public write up and code are at <a href=\"https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/Groundwork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/TanayPratapSingh/Groundwork</a>.</p>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>Getting one good answer out of an AI question answering feature is the easy part. Groundwork is about the harder parts: knowing whether an answer is good, knowing when the system should refuse, checking that its safety filters do not block a tenth of normal requests, and knowing what each change cost. I built these around a small system that looks up passages and answers questions from them, so the checks had something concrete to measure.</p>\n<h4>Real Data Only</h4>\n<p>Every number comes from public data labelled by people. I wrote none of the test material: no passages written for the demo, no answers made easy, no attacks invented to be caught. A script downloads five public datasets and records a fingerprint of each file, so anyone can confirm they have the same data. The questions come from SQuAD, a public set of 11,873 questions about Wikipedia paragraphs, about half of which cannot be answered from the text. The attacks come from public collections of prompts written to trick AI systems, including one collection the filters were never trained on.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Evaluation</strong> scores whether the right passage was found, whether the answer matches, whether the system declined the right questions, and whether its confidence matches how often it is right. A release gate can fail the build.</li>\n<li><strong>Safety filters</strong> check what goes in and what comes out. Each is scored on a published set of attacks and again on a set it has never seen.</li>\n<li><strong>Security:</strong> access keys are stored in scrambled form, an audit log shows if anyone tampers with it, and personal information is removed, with the removal measured for each kind.</li>\n<li><strong>A gateway</strong> sits in front of every model call. It checks access, routes the call, caches answers, enforces budgets and rate limits, and records the cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The right passage was in the top five search results for 0.921 of the questions, about nine in ten.</li>\n<li>The system correctly declined 96.2% of the questions that cannot be answered, using a deterministic offline reader: a fixed answer program that gives the same output every time and needs no AI provider account.</li>\n<li>On that same offline reader, its confidence was close to how often it was right, with a calibration error of 0.045, where zero would be perfect.</li>\n<li>The injection filter caught 0.884 of the attacks in the collection it had never seen.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The answers come from the deterministic offline reader, so the declining and confidence scores describe that reader, not a hosted large language model.</li>\n<li>The question answering system itself is small. It exists so the checks have something concrete to measure.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can a small RAG system be measured for quality, refusal, guardrails, and cost?", "d": "Groundwork builds evaluation, guardrails, secure deployment, and an LLM gateway around a small retrieval augmented generation system, so the measurements have something concrete to be true about."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Five public, human labelled datasets, each hashed with SHA-256 in a manifest", "lanes": [[{"k": "data", "t": "SQuAD v2.0 dev", "d": "1,204 Wikipedia paragraphs, 11,873 questions, gold answer spans, and 5,945 unanswerable questions. 4.2 MB, CC BY-SA 4.0."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "deepset/prompt-injections", "d": "662 examples for injection rail precision and recall."}, {"k": "data", "t": "jackhhao/jailbreak-classification", "d": "1,306 examples for the jailbreak rail."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "Lakera/gandalf_ignore_instructions", "d": "1,000 held out attacks, never trained on."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "ai4privacy/pii-masking-200k", "d": "1,000 examples for PII redaction recall per entity type."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Deterministic offline reader", "d": "The system runs with no API key at all, so every figure is reproducible on a laptop for free. No corpus, gold answer, or attack was written for the demo."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Retrieval, with a three way ablation", "d": "Retrieval over the 1,204 SQuAD paragraphs is scored with retrieval metrics, and the evaluation includes a three way retrieval ablation."}, {"k": "result", "t": "recall@5 0.921", "d": "The gold paragraph is among the top five retrieved for 0.921 of the questions, on SQuAD v2.0 dev."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Answer or decline?", "d": "Abstention is scored as a classifier over the 11,873 questions evaluated per run.", "yes": "The answer is scored with the SQuAD official answer metrics.", "no": "The system declines. It correctly declines 96.2% of unanswerable questions, measured on the deterministic offline reader."}, {"k": "result", "t": "ECE 0.045", "d": "Confidence calibration, with reliability diagrams: stated confidence sits close to observed accuracy. Measured on the deterministic offline reader."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do the scores clear the release gate?", "d": "The release gate exits non zero on a failure, so it can fail a build.", "yes": "The build continues. The project's 113 tests pass and CI is green.", "no": "The gate exits non zero and fails the build."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Input and output rails", "d": "Each rail is scored on a published labelled attack corpus, then again on an attack corpus it has never seen."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Injection recall 0.884 on an unseen attack corpus", "d": "Measured on the held out Lakera attacks the rail was never trained on."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Secure deployment", "d": "Hashed API keys with constant time comparison, a tamper evident audit hash chain, PII redaction measured per entity type, a threat model, and a hardened container."}, {"k": "step", "t": "LLM gateway as the single chokepoint", "d": "Every model call passes auth, tier routing, a two layer cache, per tenant budgets and rate limits, retries, and graceful degradation, with full cost accounting."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The reader is offline and deterministic", "d": "Abstention and calibration scores describe the deterministic offline reader, not a hosted language model."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "A small RAG system", "d": "The answering system is small; it exists so the measurements have something concrete to be true about."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "On real, public, human labelled data, retrieval reaches recall@5 0.921 and the injection rail catches 0.884 of unseen attacks. Abstention and calibration come from a deterministic offline reader, not a hosted model."}]}, {"title": "Replicating BEIR: Reproducing a Retrieval Benchmark and Finding a Silent Truncation Bug", "meta": "Information Retrieval &middot; Reproducibility &middot; Sep 2026", "badge": "6 of 6 published cells reproduced &middot; 4 exact &middot; silent truncation bug found and fixed", "tags": ["Python", "Information Retrieval", "BEIR", "BM25", "Dense Retrieval", "sentence-transformers", "DPR", "TAS-B", "pytrec_eval", "Reciprocal Rank Fusion", "Reproducibility"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">6 of 6 published cells reproduced &nbsp;|&nbsp; 4 exact to three decimals &nbsp;|&nbsp; DPR on SciFact recovered from 0.260 to a published 0.318 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 12 minutes on a laptop, no GPU &nbsp;|&nbsp; every number traces to an artifact in runs/</div>\n\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>Reading a paper tells you what the authors claim. Rebuilding it tells you whether the claim survives contact with a different machine, a different implementation and a fresh set of defaults. I rebuilt the central experiment from <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08663\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">BEIR</a> (Thakur et al., NeurIPS 2021 Datasets and Benchmarks) from scratch: the claim that dense retrievers trained on MS MARCO lose to a plain bag of words baseline once you move them to a domain they were not trained on.</p>\n<p>The useful part of a replication is the one cell that disagrees, because chasing it is the only way to learn something the paper did not tell you.</p>\n\n<h4>What Reproduced</h4>\n<p>I used two scientific datasets from the benchmark, chosen because they are the smallest and therefore fit on a laptop: SciFact (5,183 documents, 300 queries) and NFCorpus (3,633 documents, 323 queries). Both counts match the paper exactly. The metric is nDCG@10, scored with pytrec_eval, the same trec_eval bindings the authors report with.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>SciFact published</th><th>SciFact mine</th><th>NFCorpus published</th><th>NFCorpus mine</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>BM25</td><td>0.665</td><td>0.677</td><td>0.325</td><td>0.320</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>TAS-B</td><td>0.643</td><td><strong>0.643</strong></td><td>0.319</td><td><strong>0.319</strong></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DPR</td><td>0.318</td><td><strong>0.318</strong></td><td>0.189</td><td>0.188</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>bge-small (2023, not in the paper)</td><td>n/a</td><td>0.713</td><td>n/a</td><td>0.343</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Hybrid RRF (not in the paper)</td><td>n/a</td><td>0.687</td><td>n/a</td><td>0.341</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The paper holds for the models the paper tested. It does not survive a 2023 embedder: bge-small-en-v1.5, at 33M parameters, beats BM25 on both datasets. The authors were right in 2021 and the gap they identified has since closed. Reciprocal rank fusion over BM25 and TAS-B also beat both of its own inputs on both datasets, and took the best Recall@100 on SciFact at 0.948.</p>\n\n<h4>The Cell That Disagreed</h4>\n<p>DPR on SciFact came in at 0.260 against a published 0.318. Not a rounding difference. TAS-B had already reproduced exactly on both datasets, so the loader, the metrics and the scoring were not at fault, which left something specific to how DPR sees a document.</p>\n<p>The cause is a default. The sentence-transformers port of the DPR context encoder ships with <code>max_seq_length = 100</code>, so every abstract was cut off after roughly its first paragraph. No error, no warning, no sign of it in the output. The tell was that searching with <strong>only the title</strong> scored 0.261 against title plus full text at 0.260. That only makes sense if the body was being discarded.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Context encoder token limit</th><th>nDCG@10</th><th>Recall@100</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>100 (the shipped default)</td><td>0.276</td><td>0.601</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>256</td><td>0.298</td><td>0.687</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>350</td><td>0.315</td><td>0.710</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>512</td><td><strong>0.318</strong></td><td><strong>0.727</strong></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>published</td><td>0.318</td><td>0.727</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The bug was invisible on NFCorpus, which reproduced either way. NFCorpus carries 38.2 judged documents per query against SciFact’s 1.1, so it absorbs ranking damage that SciFact exposes. Run on NFCorpus alone, this replication would have hidden the bug entirely. A benchmark with one dataset cannot tell you when you are wrong.</p>\n\n<h4>What Is Not Identical To The Paper</h4>\n<p>Every reproduction deviates somewhere. Stating the deviations is the difference between a replication and a claim.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Deviation</th><th>Why, and what it costs</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>BM25 is not Anserini</td><td>The paper scores BM25 with Anserini, which is Lucene. That needs a Java runtime the machine did not have, so BM25 is reimplemented in Python with the same k1 of 0.9 and b of 0.4, the same Porter stemming and the same 33 word Lucene English stopword list, but one concatenated field instead of two and exact rather than quantised document lengths. This is the likely source of the only two inexact cells, +0.012 and -0.005.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Plain rather than capped recall</td><td>The paper reports capped Recall@100. The two agree for every SciFact query but not for every NFCorpus query.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>bge-small is an addition</td><td>A 2023 model the paper could not have tested, included to measure how far the field moved, and labelled as not in the paper wherever it appears.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Hybrid RRF is an addition</td><td>Reciprocal rank fusion with k of 60, from Cormack et al. 2009.</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p>Every number here traces to a command that was run and a JSON artifact committed under <code>runs/</code>. Code at <a href=\"https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/beir-replication\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/TanayPratapSingh/beir-replication</a>.</p>", "simple": "<h4>What I Did</h4>\n<p>BEIR is a well known test for search systems, published in 2021. Its central claim is that newer AI search models, trained on one large dataset called MS MARCO, lose to a plain keyword counting method called BM25 once they are moved to a subject they were not trained on. I rebuilt that experiment from scratch, on a different machine with different code, to see whether the claim holds.</p>\n<p>I used the two smallest datasets in the benchmark, both scientific, so everything runs in 12 minutes on a laptop with no graphics card. The score is nDCG@10, which rates how well the top ten results are ordered; higher is better.</p>\n<h4>What Reproduced</h4>\n<p>The paper publishes six scores for the three systems I rebuilt on these two datasets. All six came back, and four matched to three decimal places. BM25 came out slightly off, likely because my Python rewrite differs from the paper&rsquo;s version in small ways. The paper&rsquo;s version needs Java, which the machine did not have.</p>\n<p>The claim holds for the models the paper tested. It does not hold for a newer one. bge-small, a small search model from 2023 that is not in the paper, beat BM25 on both datasets, so the gap the authors found in 2021 has since closed. I also merged the ranked lists from BM25 and one of the paper&rsquo;s AI models, and the merged list beat both of its inputs on both datasets.</p>\n<h4>The Score That Did Not Match</h4>\n<p>One model, DPR, scored 0.260 on SciFact against a published 0.318. Another model had already matched the paper exactly on both datasets, so the data loading and the scoring were fine. The problem had to be in how DPR reads a document.</p>\n<p>The clue was that searching with only the titles scored no worse than titles plus the full text. That only makes sense if the text was being thrown away, and it was. The library I used ships DPR with a default reading limit of 100 tokens (pieces of words), so every abstract was cut off after roughly its first paragraph, with no error and no warning. Raising the limit to 512 tokens brought DPR to 0.318, the published 0.318.</p>\n<p>The bug did not show up on the other dataset, NFCorpus. It has 38.2 documents graded for relevance per question against 1.1 for SciFact, so it absorbs ranking mistakes that SciFact exposes. Had I only run NFCorpus, the bug would have stayed hidden.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>I ran two datasets from the benchmark, the two smallest, both scientific.</li>\n<li>My BM25 is a Python rewrite with the paper&rsquo;s settings, not the paper&rsquo;s own tool.</li>\n<li>A second score, the share of relevant documents found in the top 100, is computed without a cap the paper applies. The two versions agree on every SciFact question but not on every NFCorpus question.</li>\n<li>bge-small and the merged lists are my additions and are labelled as not in the paper wherever they appear.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Do dense retrievers trained on MS MARCO lose to BM25 out of domain?", "d": "The central claim of BEIR (Thakur et al., NeurIPS 2021). I rebuilt the experiment from scratch on a different machine, implementation and set of defaults."}, {"k": "data", "t": "SciFact and NFCorpus, the two smallest BEIR datasets", "d": "SciFact has 5,183 documents and 300 queries; NFCorpus has 3,633 documents and 323 queries. Both are scientific and fit on a laptop."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do the corpus and query counts match the paper?", "yes": "Both match exactly, so the evaluation starts from the same data.", "no": "The data would differ from the paper's before any model ran."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Systems retrieved over both datasets", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "BM25 (published)", "d": "Bag of words baseline, reimplemented in Python with k1 of 0.9, b of 0.4, Porter stemming and the 33 word Lucene English stopword list."}, {"k": "step", "t": "TAS-B (published)", "d": "Dense retriever from the paper."}, {"k": "step", "t": "DPR (published)", "d": "Dense retriever from the paper, run through the sentence-transformers port of its context encoder."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "bge-small-en-v1.5 (addition)", "d": "A 2023 embedder at 33M parameters, which the paper could not have tested. Labelled not in the paper."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Hybrid RRF (addition)", "d": "Reciprocal rank fusion of BM25 and TAS-B with k of 60, from Cormack et al. 2009. Labelled not in the paper."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Score nDCG@10 with pytrec_eval", "d": "nDCG@10 rates the ordering of the top ten results. pytrec_eval wraps the same trec_eval the authors report with. Recall@100 is scored too."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does TAS-B match the published nDCG@10?", "yes": "Exactly on both: 0.643 against a published 0.643 on SciFact, 0.319 against 0.319 on NFCorpus. The loader, metrics and scoring are sound.", "no": "The pipeline itself would be suspect."}, {"k": "result", "t": "BM25 close but not exact", "d": "SciFact 0.677 against a published 0.665; NFCorpus 0.320 against a published 0.325."}, {"k": "result", "t": "DPR on NFCorpus: 0.188 against a published 0.189", "d": "Within a thousandth of the paper."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does DPR on SciFact match the published 0.318?", "yes": "No investigation would be needed.", "no": "It scored 0.260 against a published 0.318. Not a rounding difference, and since TAS-B was exact, the cause is specific to how DPR sees a document."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does adding the body text beat searching on titles alone?", "d": "A diagnostic run on DPR over SciFact.", "yes": "The body would be contributing to retrieval.", "no": "Title only scored 0.261 against title plus full text at 0.260, which only makes sense if the body was discarded."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Cause: the shipped default <code>max_seq_length = 100</code>", "d": "The sentence-transformers DPR context encoder truncated every abstract after roughly its first paragraph, with no error, no warning and no sign in the output."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Token limit sweep recovers the published DPR score", "d": "nDCG@10 on SciFact: 0.276 at 100, 0.298 at 256, 0.315 at 350, 0.318 at 512 against a published 0.318. Recall@100 at 512 is 0.727 against a published 0.727."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Did NFCorpus expose the truncation bug?", "yes": "Either dataset alone would have caught it.", "no": "It reproduced either way. NFCorpus has 38.2 judged documents per query against SciFact's 1.1, so it absorbs ranking damage SciFact exposes. Run alone, it would have hidden the bug."}, {"k": "result", "t": "6 of 6 published cells reproduced, 4 exact to three decimals", "d": "The paper holds for the models it tested. The full run takes 12 minutes on a laptop with no GPU, and every number traces to an artifact in <code>runs/</code>."}, {"k": "result", "t": "bge-small beats BM25 on both datasets", "d": "0.713 against my BM25's 0.677 on SciFact, 0.343 against 0.320 on NFCorpus. No published cell exists for it. The gap the authors found in 2021 has since closed."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Hybrid RRF beats both of its inputs on both datasets", "d": "0.687 on SciFact against BM25 0.677 and TAS-B 0.643; 0.341 on NFCorpus against 0.320 and 0.319. It also took the best Recall@100 on SciFact, 0.948. No published cell exists for it."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "BM25 is a Python reimplementation, not Anserini", "d": "Anserini needs a Java runtime the machine did not have. One concatenated field instead of two and exact rather than quantised lengths are the likely source of the BM25 gaps, +0.012 and -0.005."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Plain rather than capped Recall@100", "d": "The paper reports capped recall. The two agree on every SciFact query but not on every NFCorpus query."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Two small scientific datasets only", "d": "SciFact and NFCorpus were chosen because they are the smallest. The reproduction covers these two datasets."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "The BEIR claim reproduces for BM25, TAS-B and DPR on these two datasets, once a silent truncation default is fixed. A 2023 embedder reverses it. The bge-small and RRF rows are additions with no published baseline."}]}, {"title": "JMA Wireless: Standardizing Five Factory KPIs and Locating the Real Constraint", "meta": "Lean Six Sigma &middot; Analytics Engineering &middot; Jan to May 2026", "badge": "project lead, 4 person team &middot; 31 GB JSON to under 5 MB &middot; ~$355K projected annualized opportunity", "tags": ["DMAIC", "Power BI", "DAX", "Python", "Pandas", "openpyxl", "ETL", "Minitab", "SIPOC", "Pugh Matrix", "FMEA", "Control Plan"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">5 KPIs standardized across 2 lines &nbsp;|&nbsp; 31 GB of nested PLC JSON reduced to under 5 MB &nbsp;|&nbsp; automated line at 97.3% uptime but 46.3% schedule attainment &nbsp;|&nbsp; ~$355K projected annualized opportunity &nbsp;|&nbsp; 15 week DMAIC engagement</div>\n\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>JMA Wireless builds cell tower transmitters and active 5G infrastructure in Liverpool, New York. Its Jumper Department produces the radio frequency jumper cables that connect transmitters to the antennas on towers and small cells. The parts are functionally simple and precision sensitive: every connector must be terminated to tight torque and electrical specification, and every cable must pass continuity, return loss, and visual inspection before it ships.</p>\n<p>The department runs two parallel lines with completely different economics. Jumper 1.0 is manual, staffed by eight operators across two ten hour shifts. Jumper 2.0 is a fully automated cell with PLC level data capture. Before this project there was no way to compare them, because terms like Uptime and Schedule Attainment meant different things to different functions. Leadership could not draw a clean line from what happened on the floor on Tuesday to the financial plan.</p>\n\n<h4>What I Owned</h4>\n<p>I was Project Lead on a four person Whitman School team running a full DMAIC engagement from January to May 2026, sponsored by JMA’s SVP of Global Operations with a named project champion and weekly on site access. I wrote the charter, SIPOC, and CTQC tree, ran the KPI definition interviews, built the Python pipeline that made the automated line’s data usable, and wrote the Control Plan that governs the dashboard after handover.</p>\n\n<h4>The Method</h4>\n<p>\n  DMAIC as it was run, including the loops that fired. Two of them mattered. A root cause that did not survive validation went back for re analysis, and a target the data showed was unreachable went back to be revalidated rather than left red on a dashboard forever.\n</p>\n\n<figure class=\"project-figure\">\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/jma_02.jpg\" alt=\"DMAIC project flow from problem identification through KPI definition, baseline, root cause analysis, solution selection, dashboard build, control plan and monitoring\">\n  <figcaption>Figure 1. The DMAIC path run on this engagement, including the re analysis and target validation loops</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h4>Standardizing the KPIs</h4>\n<p>\n  The most important Define deliverable was a written, sponsor approved definition document: five KPIs, each with one agreed formula and one agreed target, confirmed by the sponsor before anything was built.\n</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>KPI</th><th>Formula</th><th>Target</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Parts per Labor Hour</td><td>Total good parts / total labor hours</td><td>&ge; 50</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Uptime</td><td>Runtime / (runtime + downtime)</td><td>&ge; 85%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>First Time Throughput</td><td>Good parts / total parts tested</td><td>&ge; 95%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Schedule Attainment</td><td>Actual parts / weekly target</td><td>&ge; 100%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Culture and Engagement</td><td>Pulse score (60%) + weekly active dashboard users (40%)</td><td>Index &ge; 75</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p>\n  The Culture and Engagement metric is the unusual one. Both JMA and Whitman treat culture as a real lean variable, but standard manufacturing data never captures it, so I specified a five question two minute pulse survey plus a passive count of distinct named users who open the dashboard at least twice a week.\n</p>\n\n<h4>The Data Engineering Problem</h4>\n<p>\n  The automated cell publishes one JSON record per test event, and each record nests an array of tests, each nesting an array of measurements, each nesting an array of channel readings. Two raw datasets of 809 and 1,000 records expanded to more than 31 GB when loaded straight into Power BI. That is unusable twice over: Power BI expands the nesting on every visual that touches the data, and nobody with less than 64 GB of RAM can open the file at all.\n</p>\n<p>\n  I built a Python pipeline with pandas and openpyxl that walks the nesting with explicit <code>json_normalize</code> record paths and writes one flat row per test event. It casts timestamps to datetime, channel readings to float, and pass and fail to boolean, so Power Query never has to coerce anything. It also computes cycle time in seconds as a direct input to the Uptime and Parts per Labor Hour measures. The output is under 5 MB and loads instantly.\n</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Extract</td><td>Load raw test report JSON</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Parse</td><td>Walk the nesting with explicit <code>json_normalize</code> record paths</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Type cast</td><td>datetime64, float64, boolean, so Power BI needs no coercion</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Calculate</td><td>Derive cycle time in seconds from start and end timestamps</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Load</td><td>Write a Power BI ready workbook via openpyxl</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Baseline: What the Standardized Numbers Showed</h4>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>KPI</th><th>Jumper 1.0 (manual)</th><th>Jumper 2.0 (automated)</th><th>Target</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>First Time Throughput</td><td>87.9%</td><td>92.5%</td><td>95%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Uptime</td><td>31.3%</td><td><strong>97.3%</strong></td><td>85%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Schedule Attainment</td><td>139.7%</td><td><strong>46.3%</strong></td><td>100%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Parts per Labor Hour</td><td>34.91</td><td>7.94</td><td>50</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p>\n  Parts per Labor Hour is the one number on that table that should not be read straight across. Both lines use the same scheduled labor hours denominator so the comparison is consistent, but the automated cell only needs a supervising technician for part of a shift. Measured on engaged technician minutes, the automated figure is roughly an order of magnitude higher, so the dashboard exposes both that figure and a cell specific cycles per engaged hour measure rather than letting 7.94 stand as a verdict.\n</p>\n\n<h4>The Finding That Mattered</h4>\n<p>\n  The automated cell held <strong>97.3% uptime</strong> and still delivered only <strong>46.3% of its weekly target</strong>. Those two numbers cannot both be a machine problem. A cell that runs almost all of the time and still misses plan by more than half is input limited rather than equipment limited. The binding constraint was what entered the cell.\n</p>\n<p>\n  The manual line was quietly absorbing the shortfall at 139.7% of its own target. That looks like heroics and reads well on a weekly report, but it depresses labor productivity, strains the operator pool, and masks the real demand signal going back to planning. The pattern only became visible because standardizing the definitions made the two numbers comparable.\n</p>\n<p>\n  A fishbone on that gap put the leading causes in Method, Material, and Measurement: kitting batches sized for the manual line, connector subassemblies arriving batched rather than continuously, and a weekly target set at commissioning that had never been revisited against demonstrated capability.\n</p>\n\n<h4>Where Quality and Time Go</h4>\n<p>\n  Two Paretos carried the Analyze phase. On quality, torque and return loss together account for roughly three quarters of all first time failures, which points at tooling and torque training on one side and connector seating on the other, not at a diffuse quality problem.\n</p>\n\n<figure class=\"project-figure\">\n  <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/jma_01.jpg\" alt=\"Pareto chart of first time throughput failure modes on Jumper 1.0: torque out of spec 41 percent, return loss 33 percent, visual reject 14 percent, continuity fail 8 percent, other 4 percent\">\n  <figcaption>Figure 2. FTT failure modes on Jumper 1.0. Torque and return loss are 74% of all first time failures</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p>\n  On time, the manual line loses more than a third of its scheduled hours waiting for material and another quarter to changeover. That is the same upstream flow problem that starves the automated cell, in a different shape.\n</p>\n<table>\n  <thead><tr><th>Jumper 1.0 downtime category</th><th>Share</th><th>Typical event</th><th>Per week</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Material wait (cable, connectors)</td><td><strong>38%</strong></td><td>18 min</td><td>~24</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Setup and changeover</td><td>27%</td><td>32 min</td><td>~9</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Tool calibration and maintenance</td><td>19%</td><td>22 min</td><td>~9</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Operator absent or break overrun</td><td>12%</td><td>11 min</td><td>~11</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Other</td><td>4%</td><td>mixed</td><td>mixed</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h4>Recommendations, and the One I Piloted</h4>\n<p>\n  Three recommendations came out of Improve, selected against six candidates with a Pugh matrix and sequenced on a benefit and effort grid: a fifteen minute daily standup held at a wall mounted screen showing the dashboard, a two bin continuous kitting feed so the automated cell is never starved of inputs, and a reset of the cell’s weekly target from the number set at commissioning to demonstrated capability, with a documented ramp as kitting improves.\n</p>\n<p>\n  The third one is worth defending because it looks like moving the goalposts. A target the cell cannot currently hit makes the dashboard permanently red, and a permanently red dashboard is one people stop reading. Resetting it to roughly 1,150 cables per week and ramping by about 200 per week as the kitting fix lands means the team can see progress it is making.\n</p>\n<p>\n  I piloted the standup for two weeks on the manual line first shift and measured it rather than asserting it worked. Median time from a KPI going red to a named owner taking the first action fell from about <strong>4 hours to about 25 minutes</strong>, because the standup surfaced the red status instead of a supervisor finding it on a walk. Operator self reported familiarity with the five KPIs rose from <strong>2.4 to 4.1</strong> on a five point scale.\n</p>\n\n<h4>What the Dollar Figure Rests On</h4>\n<p>\n  The headline number is a projection, not money in the bank. Closing the automated line’s schedule attainment gap to plan recovers roughly <strong>27,000 cables per year</strong> with no added labor. At an assumed <strong>$12 contribution margin per cable</strong> that is about $324,000 a year. Retiring the manual weekly reporting cycle frees about <strong>48 analyst hours per week</strong>, roughly $31,200 a year at an assumed <strong>$12.50 fully loaded hourly rate</strong>. Together that is about $355,000 of recurring annual opportunity.\n</p>\n<p>\n  Both of those rates are conservative assumptions the team adopted pending confirmation from JMA Finance, and the report states them in an assumptions table so any figure can be revised without re deriving the analysis. The cable recovery is a projection from demonstrated capability, not an observed post implementation result.\n</p>\n\n<h4>What I Would Do Differently</h4>\n<p>\n  The Culture and Engagement metric was scoped late and is the one KPI with no baseline value; the instrument is built and wired into the dashboard but the first administration slipped past the engagement. I also did not write unit tests for the Python pipeline. It produces correct output today, but the only thing standing between a schema change upstream and a silently wrong dashboard is an analyst noticing, and a small set of assertions would have removed that dependency before handover.\n</p>\n\n<h4>Sustainment</h4>\n<p>\n  The dashboard ships with a Control Plan: a named refresh owner and cadence, an FMEA on the dashboard itself covering the ways a stale or broken feed could mislead a supervisor, and a tiered escalation framework that triggers on red KPI status so a red light has a defined next action instead of being ambient.\n</p>", "simple": "<h4>What We Worked On</h4>\n<p>JMA Wireless makes cell tower and 5G equipment in Liverpool, New York. One department makes the cables that connect transmitters to antennas, on two lines: one worked by hand, and one fully automated. I led a four person Whitman School team through a 15 week improvement project there, from January to May 2026, using DMAIC, a standard step by step method for fixing a process (define, measure, analyze, improve, control).</p>\n<p>The two lines could not be compared, because words like uptime meant different things to different departments. So the first thing we produced was a written definition of five measures, each with one formula and one target, signed off by the project's sponsor before anything was built. Two of them come up again below: uptime, the share of time a line is running, and schedule attainment, how much of its weekly target it made.</p>\n<h4>Making the Data Usable</h4>\n<p>The automated line records every test in a deeply nested data format. Loaded straight into Power BI, the reporting tool, two sample files grew past 31 GB, too big to open on any computer with less than 64 GB of memory. I wrote a Python program that flattens the records into one row per test, which brought the data under 5 MB.</p>\n<h4>What We Found</h4>\n<p>The automated line was running 97.3% of the time but making only 46.3% of its weekly target. A line that runs almost all the time and still misses its plan by more than half is short of input. The leading causes we found were that parts were prepared in batches sized for the manual line, and that the weekly target had been set when the cell was installed and never checked against what it could do. Meanwhile the manual line was quietly covering the gap by producing well over its own target. That looks good on a weekly report, but it strains the operators and hides the true demand from planning.</p>\n<h4>What We Recommended</h4>\n<p>We recommended three changes: a short daily standup meeting in front of a screen showing the measures, a steady feed of parts to the automated line, and a reset of its weekly target to what it can produce today, rising as the parts feed improves. I ran the standup as a two week pilot on the manual line's first shift. The typical time from a measure turning red to a named person taking action fell from about 4 hours to about 25 minutes.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The money is a projection.</strong> Our estimate of about $355,000 a year rests on two assumed rates, a margin per cable and an hourly labor cost, that were still waiting on confirmation from JMA's finance team. The extra cables are projected from what the line has shown it can do, not counted after a change.</li>\n<li><strong>The pilot covered one shift.</strong> The standup result comes from two weeks on the manual line's first shift.</li>\n<li><strong>One measure has no starting value.</strong> The culture and engagement survey is built into the dashboard, but its first round slipped past the end of the project.</li>\n<li><strong>The data program has no automated tests.</strong> If the source data changes shape upstream, only an analyst noticing stands between that change and a dashboard showing wrong numbers.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Why does JMA's automated jumper cable line miss plan?", "d": "The Jumper Department runs two lines, and terms like Uptime and Schedule Attainment meant different things to different functions, so the lines could not be compared or traced to the financial plan."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Two parallel lines with different economics", "lanes": [[{"k": "data", "t": "Jumper 1.0, manual", "d": "Eight operators across two ten hour shifts."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "Jumper 2.0, fully automated cell", "d": "PLC level data capture: one JSON record per test event."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "15 week DMAIC engagement, January to May 2026", "d": "A four person Whitman School team with Tanay as Project Lead, sponsored by JMA's SVP of Global Operations, with a named champion and weekly on site access. Define produced the charter, SIPOC and CTQC tree."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the sponsor approve one formula and one target per KPI?", "d": "KPI definition interviews feed a written definition document.", "yes": "The approved document becomes the basis for everything built after it.", "no": "Nothing is built until the sponsor confirms the definitions."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Five KPIs standardized", "d": "Parts per Labor Hour &ge; 50, Uptime &ge; 85%, First Time Throughput &ge; 95%, Schedule Attainment &ge; 100%, and a Culture and Engagement index &ge; 75 from a pulse survey (60%) plus weekly active dashboard users (40%)."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Nested PLC test JSON", "d": "Each record nests tests, measurements and channel readings. Two datasets of 809 and 1,000 records expand past 31 GB in Power BI, and nobody with less than 64 GB of RAM can open the file."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Python ETL with pandas and openpyxl", "d": "Explicit <code>json_normalize</code> record paths write one flat row per test event. Timestamps, readings and pass or fail are typed so Power Query coerces nothing, and cycle time in seconds is derived."}, {"k": "result", "t": "31 GB of nested JSON reduced to under 5 MB", "d": "The Power BI ready workbook loads instantly."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Baseline on the standardized definitions", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "Jumper 1.0, manual", "d": "First Time Throughput 87.9%, Uptime 31.3%, Schedule Attainment 139.7%, Parts per Labor Hour 34.91."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Jumper 2.0, automated", "d": "First Time Throughput 92.5%, Uptime 97.3%, Schedule Attainment 46.3%, Parts per Labor Hour 7.94."}]]}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Parts per Labor Hour does not read straight across", "d": "Both lines use scheduled labor hours, but the cell needs a technician for only part of a shift. On engaged minutes it is roughly an order of magnitude higher, so the dashboard also shows cycles per engaged hour."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Can 97.3% uptime and 46.3% attainment both be a machine problem?", "d": "The automated cell runs almost all the time and still misses plan by more than half.", "yes": "The fix would be on the equipment.", "no": "The cell is input limited rather than equipment limited. The binding constraint is what enters the cell."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Manual line absorbing the shortfall at 139.7% of target", "d": "Reads well on a weekly report but depresses labor productivity, strains the operator pool, and masks the demand signal going back to planning. Visible only once the definitions were standardized."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Fishbone on the attainment gap", "d": "Leading causes in Method, Material and Measurement: kitting batches sized for the manual line, connector subassemblies arriving batched rather than continuously, and a weekly target set at commissioning and never revisited."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Quality Pareto: torque and return loss are 74% of failures", "d": "Together they are roughly three quarters of first time failures on Jumper 1.0, pointing at tooling and torque training on one side and connector seating on the other, not at a diffuse quality problem."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Time Pareto: material wait is 38% of Jumper 1.0 downtime", "d": "Typical event 18 min, about 24 a week; setup and changeover add 27%. The same upstream flow problem that starves the automated cell."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the root cause survive validation?", "d": "One of two DMAIC loops that fired on this engagement.", "yes": "It moves forward to Improve.", "no": "It goes back for re analysis, as one root cause did here."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Pugh matrix over six candidates", "d": "Sequenced on a benefit and effort grid into three recommendations: a fifteen minute daily standup at a wall mounted dashboard screen, two bin continuous kitting, and a reset of the cell's weekly target."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Can the cell reach the target set at commissioning?", "d": "The second loop: a target the data showed was unreachable goes back to be revalidated.", "yes": "The target stays.", "no": "Reset to roughly 1,150 cables per week, ramping about 200 per week as kitting improves, so the dashboard is not permanently red."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Standup pilot: red KPI to first action fell from 4 hours to 25 minutes", "d": "Median times, about 4 hours to about 25 minutes, over two weeks on the manual line's first shift. Operator self reported KPI familiarity rose from 2.4 to 4.1 on a five point scale."}, {"k": "result", "t": "About $355,000 projected recurring annual opportunity", "d": "About 27,000 cables a year recovered with no added labor at an assumed $12 margin (about $324,000), plus 48 analyst hours a week freed at an assumed $12.50 rate (about $31,200)."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The dollar figure is a projection", "d": "Both rates are conservative assumptions pending JMA Finance confirmation, kept in an assumptions table. Cable recovery is projected from demonstrated capability, not an observed post implementation result."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Culture metric has no baseline; the pipeline has no unit tests", "d": "The survey's first administration slipped past the engagement. Without assertions, only an analyst noticing stands between an upstream schema change and a silently wrong dashboard."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Control Plan for sustainment", "d": "A named refresh owner and cadence, an FMEA on the dashboard covering stale or broken feeds, and tiered escalation that triggers on red KPI status, so a red light has a defined next action."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "Standardized definitions made 97.3% uptime against 46.3% attainment visible and placed the constraint upstream of the automated cell. The pilot covers one shift over two weeks, and the $355,000 is a projection on assumed rates."}]}, {"title": "ShopFloor AI: Predictive Maintenance and RAG for the Factory Floor", "meta": "MLOps &middot; Predictive Maintenance &middot; Jul 2026", "badge": "0.85 F1 &middot; 0.96 AUC &middot; 7-service deployed stack", "tags": ["Python", "Scikit learn", "Gradient Boosting", "MLflow", "Apache Kafka", "Apache Airflow", "Docker Compose", "FastAPI", "Streamlit", "LangChain", "ChromaDB", "RAG"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">Gradient Boosting F1 = 0.85 &nbsp;|&nbsp; AUC-ROC = 0.96 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 3 models tracked in MLflow &nbsp;|&nbsp; 16 engineered features &nbsp;|&nbsp; 7 service Docker Compose stack &nbsp;|&nbsp; AI4I 2020 (UCI, CC BY 4.0)</div>\n\n          <h4>Overview</h4>\n          <p>ShopFloor AI is an end to end manufacturing platform. It predicts machine failures from sensor data with classical ML, streams real time production KPIs through Apache Kafka, and answers natural language questions over maintenance documentation with LLM powered retrieval augmented generation. The three parts run as seven containerized services rather than a notebook. The data is the AI4I 2020 Predictive Maintenance Dataset (UCI ML Repository, CC BY 4.0).</p>\n\n          <h4>Tech Stack</h4>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Technology</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Streaming</td><td>Apache Kafka</td><td>Real time sensor ingestion and anomaly alerting</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>ML tracking</td><td>MLflow</td><td>Experiment logging, model comparison, registry</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Orchestration</td><td>Apache Airflow</td><td>Scheduled retraining with data quality gates</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>RAG</td><td>LangChain + ChromaDB + OpenAI API</td><td>Natural language Q&amp;A over SOPs</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>API</td><td>FastAPI</td><td>REST endpoints for predictions and queries</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Dashboard</td><td>Streamlit + Plotly</td><td>KPI monitoring, failure predictor, chat, drift</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Infrastructure</td><td>Docker Compose</td><td>7 containerized microservices</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>ML</td><td>scikit-learn</td><td>Gradient Boosting / Random Forest classifiers</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <h4>How It Works</h4>\n          <p>A Streamlit dashboard calls a FastAPI backend, which fronts three subsystems: an MLflow model registry for predictions, a ChromaDB vector store queried through LangChain and the OpenAI API for document Q&amp;A, and a Kafka broker carrying the live sensor stream. A producer replays sensor data onto the topic and a consumer computes rolling KPIs and raises anomaly alerts. An Airflow DAG retrains the model nightly behind data quality gates.</p>\n          <pre>Streamlit dashboard\n     |  HTTP\nFastAPI backend  -->  /predict  ·  /ask  ·  /health\n     |-- MLflow model registry\n     |-- ChromaDB vector DB  -->  LangChain + OpenAI  (RAG)\n     +-- Kafka broker  -->  producer (sensor stream) + consumer (rolling KPIs, alerts)\n\nAirflow (daily, 2 AM):  data-quality gate --> train --> evaluate --> promote --> RAG refresh</pre>\n\n          <h4>Dataset</h4>\n          <p>AI4I 2020 Predictive Maintenance Dataset (UCI, CC BY 4.0): 10,000 records at a 3.39% failure rate, across five independent failure modes (tool wear, heat dissipation, power, overstrain, random). Raw signals include air and process temperature, rotational speed, torque, tool wear, and a product quality variant (L 50% / M 30% / H 20%).</p>\n\n          <h4>Feature Engineering</h4>\n          <p>16 features are derived from the 5 raw sensor readings so the physics of failure is explicit rather than latent. Representative examples:</p>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Derivation</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><code>temp_diff</code></td><td>Process temp &minus; Air temp</td><td>Heat dissipation indicator</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>power_W</code></td><td>Torque &times; (2&pi; &times; RPM / 60)</td><td>Power failure detection</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>wear_torque_product</code></td><td>Tool wear &times; Torque</td><td>Overstrain indicator</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><code>tool_wear_ratio</code></td><td>Tool wear / 240</td><td>Normalized wear proximity to failure</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <h4>Results</h4>\n          <table>\n            <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>F1</th><th>AUC-ROC</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>Gradient Boosting</strong></td><td><strong>0.8548</strong></td><td><strong>0.9591</strong></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Random Forest</td><td>0.8387</td><td>0.9823</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Logistic Regression</td><td>0.3054</td><td>0.9455</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <p>Gradient Boosting was selected and registered in MLflow as the production model. A newly trained model must clear a 0.80 F1 threshold in the nightly pipeline before it can be promoted, so a bad batch never reaches production.</p>\n\n          <h4>Setup</h4>\n          <p>The whole system runs end to end with Docker Compose behind a Makefile:</p>\n          <pre>git clone https://github.com/TanayPratapSingh/shopfloor-ai\ncd shopfloor-ai\ncp .env.example .env      # add your OPENAI_API_KEY\nmake setup                # install deps, download AI4I data, build images\nmake train                # train 3 models, log to MLflow, register the best\nmake up                   # start all 7 services\nmake ingest               # load 6 SOPs into ChromaDB for RAG</pre>\n          <p>Services come up on these ports: dashboard (:8501), API docs (:8001/docs), MLflow (:5001), Airflow (:8080). A 15 test suite (<code>make test</code>) and GitHub Actions CI cover data loading, feature engineering, streaming, and SOP structure.</p>\n\n          <h4>Limitations and Future Work</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Synthetic dataset.</strong> AI4I is 10,000 synthetic records; a production deployment would integrate real MES/SCADA feeds and far larger volumes.</li>\n            <li><strong>Replayed streaming.</strong> The Kafka pipeline replays historical records rather than consuming a live feed; the architecture supports real connectors.</li>\n            <li><strong>Bundled SOPs.</strong> RAG answers depend on the 6 shipped documents; organization specific docs would improve retrieval relevance.</li>\n            <li><strong>Planned.</strong> SHAP explanations in the prediction API, multi model A/B testing in the DAG, WebSocket live dashboard updates, and AWS ECS deployment with S3 backed MLflow storage.</li>\n          </ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>ShopFloor AI is software for a factory floor. It does three jobs: it predicts which machines are about to fail, it tracks production numbers as they stream in, and it answers plain English questions about the maintenance manuals. The three parts run as seven connected services, and the whole system starts with a handful of commands.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Failure prediction.</strong> The training data is a public dataset of 10,000 synthetic (computer generated) machine records, of which 3.39% end in a failure. From five raw readings, such as temperatures, speed, torque and tool wear, I derived 16 measurements that spell out how machines fail, for example the gap between process and air temperature, which signals poor cooling, or the power the machine draws. I trained three models and kept the best one.</li>\n<li><strong>Nightly retraining with a gate.</strong> Every night the system checks the incoming data and retrains the model. A new model only replaces the current one if it scores at least 0.80 on F1, a score that balances catching real failures against raising false alarms. A bad batch of data cannot push a worse model into use.</li>\n<li><strong>Live numbers.</strong> Machine readings are fed through a streaming service. Another service keeps rolling production figures and raises an alert when a reading looks abnormal.</li>\n<li><strong>Questions about the manuals.</strong> An AI assistant first finds the relevant passages in the maintenance procedure documents, then writes its answer from them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A dashboard shows the production figures, a failure predictor and a chat with the assistant. Fifteen automated tests, also run on GitHub, cover data loading, the derived measurements, streaming and the structure of the documents.</p>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<p>Of the three models, Gradient Boosting had the best F1, 0.85, and an AUC of 0.96 (AUC measures how well a model ranks machines that will fail above machines that will not), both on the synthetic dataset. Random Forest ranked machines slightly better by AUC but had a lower F1. The simplest of the three, logistic regression, ranked machines nearly as well by AUC but had a far lower F1. Gradient Boosting is the one the system uses.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The data is synthetic.</strong> The 10,000 records are not readings from a real factory. A real deployment would need feeds from real factory systems, at far larger volumes.</li>\n<li><strong>The stream is a replay.</strong> The live numbers come from old records played back in order, not from machines running now.</li>\n<li><strong>The assistant knows only six documents.</strong> It answers from the procedures that ship with it. A company&rsquo;s own documents would help it find more relevant passages.</li>\n<li><strong>Some features are planned, not built.</strong> Explanations for each prediction, side by side model testing, live dashboard updates and cloud deployment are still to come.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can one stack predict failures, stream KPIs and answer SOP questions?", "d": "ShopFloor AI combines classical ML failure prediction, real time KPI streaming and retrieval augmented Q&amp;A over maintenance documents, run as seven containerized services rather than a notebook."}, {"k": "data", "t": "AI4I 2020 Predictive Maintenance Dataset (UCI, CC BY 4.0)", "d": "10,000 synthetic records at a 3.39% failure rate across five failure modes: tool wear, heat dissipation, power, overstrain and random. Product quality variants are L 50%, M 30%, H 20%."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Engineer 16 features from the 5 raw sensor readings", "d": "The physics of failure is made explicit: <code>temp_diff</code> for heat dissipation, <code>power_W</code> for power failure, <code>wear_torque_product</code> for overstrain, <code>tool_wear_ratio</code> for wear proximity to failure."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Three scikit-learn models tracked in MLflow", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "Gradient Boosting: F1 0.8548, AUC-ROC 0.9591", "d": "Highest F1 of the three, on the synthetic AI4I data."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Random Forest: F1 0.8387, AUC-ROC 0.9823", "d": "Highest AUC-ROC of the three, lower F1, on the synthetic AI4I data."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Logistic Regression: F1 0.3054, AUC-ROC 0.9455", "d": "AUC-ROC close to the other two but by far the lowest F1, on the synthetic AI4I data."}]]}, {"k": "result", "t": "Gradient Boosting registered as the production model", "d": "Headline: F1 0.85 and AUC-ROC 0.96, on 10,000 synthetic records. The MLflow registry keeps trained models separate from the deployed one."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Airflow DAG retrains nightly at 2 AM", "d": "Data quality gate, then train, evaluate, promote, and refresh the RAG index, in that order."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the newly trained model clear 0.80 F1?", "yes": "It can be promoted to production.", "no": "It is not promoted, so a bad batch never reaches production."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Services running side by side", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Kafka producer", "d": "Replays historical sensor records onto a topic as the live stream."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Kafka consumer", "d": "Computes rolling KPIs from the stream and raises anomaly alerts."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "SOP ingest into ChromaDB", "d": "Loads 6 SOP documents into a vector store for retrieval."}, {"k": "step", "t": "RAG answers through LangChain and the OpenAI API", "d": "Finds the relevant SOP passages and answers natural language questions from them."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "FastAPI backend", "d": "REST endpoints <code>/predict</code>, <code>/ask</code> and <code>/health</code> in front of the registry, the vector store and the broker."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Streamlit and Plotly dashboard", "d": "KPI monitoring, a failure predictor, chat and drift, all calling the backend over HTTP."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Docker Compose stack of 7 services behind a Makefile", "d": "<code>make setup</code>, <code>make train</code>, <code>make up</code> and <code>make ingest</code> bring the system up end to end. Dashboard on :8501, API docs on :8001/docs, MLflow on :5001, Airflow on :8080."}, {"k": "step", "t": "A 15 test suite plus GitHub Actions CI", "d": "The tests cover data loading, feature engineering, streaming and SOP structure."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Synthetic dataset", "d": "AI4I is 10,000 synthetic records, so the F1 and AUC-ROC describe this dataset. Production would need real MES/SCADA feeds and far larger volumes."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Replayed streaming", "d": "The Kafka pipeline replays historical records rather than consuming a live feed. The architecture supports real connectors."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Bundled SOPs", "d": "RAG answers depend on the 6 shipped documents. Organization specific documents would improve retrieval relevance."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Planned, not built", "d": "SHAP explanations in the prediction API, multi model A/B testing in the DAG, WebSocket live dashboard updates, and AWS ECS deployment with S3 backed MLflow storage."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "The stack runs as seven services with a gated nightly retrain. The 0.85 F1 and 0.96 AUC-ROC come from synthetic data, the stream is a replay, and the assistant knows six documents."}]}, {"title": "handoff: Supervisor Orchestration with Escalations That Have Deadlines", "meta": "Agent Orchestration &middot; Human in the Loop &middot; Sep 2026", "badge": "212 tests &middot; zero dependencies &middot; unanswered page &rarr; safer substitute", "tags": ["Python", "Agent Orchestration", "Supervisor", "Human in the Loop", "Escalation Policy", "State Machine", "Audit Trail", "Zero Dependencies", "unittest", "CI/CD"], "body": "<div class='metric'>212 tests &middot; 11 behavioural scenarios &middot; zero dependencies &middot; unanswered page &rarr; safer substitute &middot; CI green on Python 3.11 to 3.13</div>\n\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>Most human in the loop is a modal dialog that blocks forever. The agent reaches something risky, calls confirm(), and waits. It does not say who is being asked, it does not say how long they have, and it has no answer at all for the case that happens at 3am, which is that nobody replies. An agent parked on an unanswered prompt is worse than one that never asked, because the incident is still burning and now nothing is watching it. handoff treats escalation as a routing problem with tiers, skills, load, deadlines and a written down behaviour for silence, and builds the rest of the system around that.</p>\n\n<h4>The Sequel to comms-agent</h4>\n<p>comms-agent put a human in the loop gate inside an agent graph: flag the low confidence items, let a reviewer correct them, then report. That answers what happens when the reviewer shows up. handoff covers what happens when they do not, which is the case that breaks these systems in production.</p>\n\n<h4>Escalation as Policy, Not a Callback</h4>\n<p>The policy is a table keyed by risk, held as data in config/policy.yaml: which tiers may approve, in what order, how many seconds the first tier gets, how many signatures are required, and what happens on expiry. Because it is data, somebody who does not read Python can review it in a pull request. Four classes of mistake are caught at load time rather than during an incident: a rule that requires approval without stating an expiry behaviour, a quorum larger than its own ladder, a ladder tier that no responder stands on, and a declared fallback that is missing, self referential, or not safer.</p>\n\n<h4>When Nobody Answers</h4>\n<p>An irreversible failover_database is proposed. The page goes to the engineer on call who holds the infrastructure skill, with 180 seconds to answer. Nobody answers. The system neither fails over nor sits still. It runs enable_read_only instead, the substitute the failover tool itself declares. Writes are rejected and checkout degrades, but no transaction in flight is lost and a human can reverse it on arrival. The journal records that it happened because nobody answered, which is a different fact from a human deciding it was a good idea.</p>\n\n<h4>Disagreement Is a Second Trigger</h4>\n<p>Risk is not the only reason to involve a person. When the log analyst sees a high error rate and the metrics analyst sees healthy latency, both confidently, the hypothesis is marked contested rather than averaged into a clean answer the system does not have. Any action that addresses a contested hypothesis has its effective risk raised one level, so a low risk service restart that would normally run unattended stops and asks. Uncertainty about the diagnosis and danger in the fix are different problems with the same remedy, and one mechanism means nobody has to remember to wire up a second check.</p>\n\n<h4>What It Refuses To Do</h4>\n<p>The expiry behaviours are deny, safe_fallback, escalate and hold. There is deliberately no allow. Approving an action because nobody looked at it in time writes down the absence of a decision as though it were one. A ladder whose terminal state can be \"ran it anyway\" is a delay mechanism wearing the costume of a control. A test asserts that the enum member does not exist, so adding it means deleting a test whose docstring explains why it should not.</p>\n<p>The supervisor is the only component permitted to execute. Workers are handed a registry filtered to read only tools, so they cannot reach a mutating one by name. There are no dependencies, so the whole suite runs on a bare interpreter in under a tenth of a second, and during the build it caught two design flaws, one of them a safety path that could never run.</p>\n\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The cluster is simulated.</strong> It is a dataclass. Nothing in the project has talked to Kubernetes, a database, or a real pager.</li>\n<li><strong>Rules, not models.</strong> The five workers are deterministic with hand written thresholds, and no LLM is called anywhere. Whether the escalation layer holds up when the workers are nondeterministic is the open question this project does not answer.</li>\n<li><strong>No scheduler.</strong> The caller drives the deadline tick, which is what makes every deadline testable at its precise boundary. It also means a deployment needs something that calls it on a timer, and that part is not written here.</li>\n<li><strong>No notification channel.</strong> The journal exposes a subscribe hook where a pager would attach. Nothing attaches to it.</li>\n<li><strong>Static roster.</strong> Seven people in a YAML file, with no rotation schedule, timezones, or handover.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>When an automated system reaches a risky step, it usually stops and asks a person to approve, then waits forever. It does not say who is being asked or how long they have, and it has no plan for the case that happens at 3am, when nobody replies. A system stuck waiting is worse than one that never asked, because the incident is still going and nothing is watching it.</p>\n<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>handoff is a supervisor program. It runs a small team of automated workers on a simulated incident, and it decides when to call in a person and what to do if that person never answers. It follows on from comms-agent, where a person checked the uncertain calls; handoff covers what happens when the person does not show up.</p>\n<p>Every request for approval follows a written rule, kept in a plain settings file that someone who does not read code can review: who may approve, in what order, how long the first person has, how many approvals are needed, and what happens when time runs out. The program checks the rules when it starts and rejects broken ones, such as a rule with no plan for silence.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<p><strong>When nobody answers.</strong> A switch to a backup database is proposed, a step that cannot be undone. The program pages the on call engineer with the right skill and gives them 180 seconds. Nobody answers. Instead of making the switch or doing nothing, the program puts the database into read only mode, the safer option the switch itself names. New writes are rejected and checkout works less well, but no transaction in progress is lost, and a person can undo it on arrival. The log records that this happened because nobody answered.</p>\n<p><strong>When the workers disagree.</strong> If the worker reading the logs sees many errors while the worker reading the metrics sees fast response times, both confidently, the diagnosis is marked as disputed instead of averaged. Any fix for a disputed diagnosis counts as one level riskier, so a low risk restart that would normally run on its own stops and asks.</p>\n<p><strong>What it will not do.</strong> When time runs out, a rule can refuse the action, run a safer substitute, pass it to the next person up, or freeze it. There is no option to approve it anyway, since that would record a decision nobody made. A test checks that the option does not exist.</p>\n<h4>How I Tested It</h4>\n<p>There are 212 tests, including 11 scenarios that check how the system behaves. The program uses nothing beyond standard Python, so the full suite runs in under a tenth of a second. While I built it, the tests caught two design flaws, one of them a safety path that could never run.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The servers are simulated.</strong> Nothing here has talked to a real cluster, database or pager.</li>\n<li><strong>The workers follow fixed rules.</strong> They use hand written thresholds and no AI model. Whether the escalation layer holds up with unpredictable workers is the open question.</li>\n<li><strong>No timer and no alerts.</strong> Something outside has to drive the deadlines on a timer, and no paging service is attached.</li>\n<li><strong>A fixed list of seven people.</strong> There are no rotation schedules, time zones or handovers.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "What should an agent do when the person it escalates to never answers?", "d": "Most human in the loop is a <code>confirm()</code> that blocks forever. handoff, the sequel to comms-agent, treats escalation as routing with tiers, skills, load, deadlines and a written down behaviour for silence."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Escalation policy in <code>config/policy.yaml</code>", "d": "A table keyed by risk: which tiers may approve, in what order, seconds for the first tier, signatures required, and the behaviour on expiry. Reviewable in a pull request by someone who does not read Python."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Static roster of seven responders", "d": "Seven people in a YAML file, each with tiers and skills. No rotation schedule, timezones or handover."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Simulated cluster", "d": "The incident runs against a dataclass. No Kubernetes, database or real pager is involved."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the policy pass validation at load time?", "d": "Four mistakes are checked: approval with no expiry behaviour, a quorum larger than its ladder, a tier no responder stands on, and a fallback that is missing, self referential or not safer.", "yes": "The policy loads.", "no": "It is rejected at load time rather than discovered during an incident."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Five deterministic workers diagnose; only the supervisor executes", "d": "Workers use hand written thresholds and get a registry filtered to read only tools, so they cannot reach a mutating tool by name. No LLM is called anywhere."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do two workers confidently disagree?", "d": "Example: the log analyst sees a high error rate while the metrics analyst sees healthy latency.", "yes": "The hypothesis is marked contested, and any action addressing it has its effective risk raised one level.", "no": "The hypothesis stands and risk is unchanged."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the action's effective risk require approval?", "yes": "The page goes up the policy's ladder.", "no": "It runs unattended. A low risk service restart does, unless its hypothesis is contested."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Route the page by tier, skill and load", "d": "For an irreversible <code>failover_database</code>, the page goes to the on call engineer holding the infrastructure skill, with 180 seconds to answer."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does a responder answer before the deadline?", "d": "The caller drives the deadline tick, so every deadline is testable at its exact boundary.", "yes": "The required signatures decide the action.", "no": "The rule's expiry behaviour takes over."}, {"k": "split", "t": "The four expiry behaviours", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "<code>deny</code>", "d": "The action does not run."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "<code>safe_fallback</code>", "d": "Run the safer substitute the tool itself declares."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "<code>escalate</code>", "d": "Pass the page further up the ladder."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "<code>hold</code>", "d": "Freeze the action where it is."}]]}, {"k": "result", "t": "Unanswered failover runs <code>enable_read_only</code> instead", "d": "The system neither fails over nor sits still. Writes are rejected and checkout degrades, but no transaction in flight is lost and a human can reverse it on arrival."}, {"k": "step", "t": "The journal records the cause", "d": "It logs that the fallback ran because nobody answered, a different fact from a human approving it. A subscribe hook is where a pager would attach."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does an <code>allow</code> expiry behaviour exist?", "d": "Approving because nobody looked in time would write down the absence of a decision as though it were one.", "yes": "A test fails. Adding it means deleting a test whose docstring explains why it should not exist.", "no": "Silence can never approve the original action."}, {"k": "result", "t": "212 tests, 11 behavioural scenarios, zero dependencies", "d": "CI is green on Python 3.11 to 3.13, and the whole suite runs on a bare interpreter in under a tenth of a second."}, {"k": "result", "t": "The suite caught two design flaws during the build", "d": "One was a safety path that could never run."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The cluster is simulated", "d": "Nothing in the project has talked to Kubernetes, a database or a real pager."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Rules, not models", "d": "The five workers are deterministic. Whether the escalation layer holds up when workers are nondeterministic is the open question this project does not answer."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "No scheduler", "d": "A deployment needs something that calls the deadline tick on a timer, and that part is not written."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "No notification channel", "d": "The journal exposes a subscribe hook for a pager. Nothing attaches to it."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "The tests show that silence leads to a declared, safer outcome and that disagreement raises risk. They do not show the layer working against real infrastructure, real pagers or nondeterministic workers."}]}, {"title": "comms-agent: Agent Orchestration for Communications Monitoring", "meta": "Agent Orchestration &middot; LLM Systems &middot; Aug 2026", "badge": "stateful graph &middot; tool calling &middot; 9 of 24 flagged, 7 corrected via HITL gate", "tags": ["Python", "Agent Orchestration", "State Graph", "Tool Calling", "LLM", "Human in the Loop", "Reporting"], "body": "<div class='metric'>24 mention run &middot; 9 flagged low confidence &middot; 7 corrected by human before reporting &middot; plan &rarr; classify &rarr; HITL gate &rarr; report</div>\n\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>comms-agent is the automation layer on top of a monitoring pipeline. media-pulse is the measurement system; comms-agent runs it and produces the brief. It combines agent orchestration with planning and tool calling, human review of the model's uncertain output, and an explicit confidence gate with an audit trail.</p>\n\n<h4>The Agent</h4>\n<p>The flow runs plan, ingest, classify, a conditional review gate, human review, aggregate, and report. The gate is a conditional edge. If any mention came back below the confidence threshold, the run routes to human review; otherwise it skips straight to aggregation. So the human in the loop quality gate lives in the agent's control flow. On the sample run the agent flags 9 of 24 mentions and the reviewer, simulated in the demo, corrects 7 of them before anything is reported, so the brief is built from the corrected labels.</p>\n\n<h4>The Orchestrator</h4>\n<p>Underneath is a small state graph engine with the pieces production agent frameworks rely on: named nodes over a shared state, fixed and conditional edges, per node retries, a max step guard against infinite loops, and a complete execution trace. It uses no agent framework on purpose, and porting the same graph to a library like LangGraph is a mechanical change. Every tool call is recorded, so a run produces an auditable trace of exactly what the agent did.</p>\n\n<h4>The LLM</h4>\n<p>The agent depends only on a small LLM interface. The default behind it is a deterministic mock model, which keeps runs and tests reproducible offline. Swapping in a hosted model is a one method change with no edits elsewhere, because the pipeline depends on the interface, not the provider.</p>\n\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Synthetic sample.</strong> It runs on the same fictional brand corpus as media-pulse.</li>\n<li><strong>Mock model.</strong> The default LLM templates the report wording; a hosted model improves the phrasing, not the pipeline.</li>\n<li><strong>Simulated reviewer.</strong> In the demo the human review step is backed by an oracle, where production would use a real review UI.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>comms-agent is a program that reads mentions of a brand, sorts each one, and writes a short report from them. It sits on top of media-pulse, my measurement system for the same mentions, and turns its output into a brief someone can read.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<p>The program walks through fixed steps in order: plan the run, collect the mentions, label each one, then write the report. After labelling, it looks at how sure it was about every label. If any label falls below a confidence cutoff, the run pauses and sends those mentions to a person to check. If every label clears the cutoff, it skips the person and goes straight to the report.</p>\n<p>Every step, and every tool the program calls, is written to a log, so afterwards you can see exactly what it did and in what order. If a step fails it is retried, and a hard limit on the number of steps stops the program from going round in circles.</p>\n<h4>What Happened on the Sample Run</h4>\n<p>Out of 24 mentions, the program was unsure about 9 and sent them for review. The reviewer corrected 7 of those 9 before the report was written, so the report used the corrected labels rather than the program's guesses.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The mentions are made up.</strong> They describe a fictional brand, the same sample media-pulse uses.</li>\n<li><strong>The AI is a stand in.</strong> The demo uses a fake model that gives the same answer every time, so tests repeat exactly. A real model would write better sentences but would not change the steps.</li>\n<li><strong>The reviewer is simulated.</strong> A real version would need a screen where a person checks the flagged mentions.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can an agent write a monitoring brief and still hand its uncertain calls to a person?", "d": "comms-agent runs the media-pulse measurement system and produces the brief."}, {"k": "data", "t": "24 brand mentions", "d": "A synthetic sample about a fictional brand, the same corpus media-pulse uses."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Plan", "d": "The run is a state graph: named nodes over a shared state, joined by fixed and conditional edges."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Ingest", "d": "A tool call loads the mentions. Every tool call is recorded in the execution trace."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Classify", "d": "Each mention gets a label and a confidence through a small LLM interface. The default model behind it is a deterministic mock, so runs and tests repeat offline."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is any label below the confidence threshold?", "d": "The gate is a conditional edge, so human review is part of the agent's control flow.", "yes": "Route the run to human review.", "no": "Skip review and go straight to aggregation."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Human review", "d": "A reviewer checks the flagged mentions and corrects wrong labels. In the demo an oracle plays the reviewer."}, {"k": "result", "t": "9 of 24 flagged, 7 corrected", "d": "The simulated reviewer fixed 7 of the 9 low confidence labels before anything reached the report, on the synthetic sample."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Aggregate and report", "d": "The brief is built from the corrected labels, not the model's first guesses."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Running under every step", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Per node retries", "d": "A failing node is retried before the run gives up."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Max step guard", "d": "Stops the graph if it starts to loop."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Execution trace", "d": "Every node visit and tool call is logged, so the run can be audited."}]]}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Synthetic sample", "d": "The 24 mentions are fictional, so the counts describe the gate's routing, not accuracy on real coverage."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Mock model", "d": "The default model templates the report wording. A hosted model is a one method swap that changes phrasing, not the pipeline."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Simulated reviewer", "d": "An oracle stands in for the person. Production would need a real review UI."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "The run shows that low confidence labels reach a person and that the corrections flow into the brief. It does not measure how often a real model would be wrong on real mentions."}]}, {"title": "Project Mark: Designing Deterministic Analytics Traps to Stress Test Frontier Models", "meta": "AI Evaluation &middot; Analytics Red Teaming &middot; Jul 2026", "badge": "4 verified statistical traps &middot; frontier-model evaluation", "tags": ["Python", "Pandas", "NumPy", "SciPy", "Statistical Inference", "Causal Reasoning", "Experiment Design", "LLM Evaluation", "Data Cleaning", "Reproducibility"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">5 datasets profiled &nbsp;|&nbsp; 4 trap families built and verified &nbsp;|&nbsp; deterministic golden solutions reproduced to the digit &nbsp;|&nbsp; CC licensed multi file corpora &nbsp;|&nbsp; frontier models solved the clean traps</div>\n\n          <h4>What The Project Was</h4>\n          <p>\n            The brief was unusually specific. Every submission had to be a product analytics question of the kind a technology company faces, analyzing how users behave inside a digital product to drive a product, growth, or monetization decision. Each had to resolve to a single deterministic recommendation that ten domain experts would independently agree on, be reconstructible entirely from the shipped files, and be hard enough that a panel of frontier models would fail it. A valid stump had a concrete bar. Across eight model responses, at least two of the first four and one of the second four had to land on a meaningfully different answer. Difficulty had to come from methodology and reasoning, never from vague wording or a trick definition.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>The Constraint: Fairness Plus Difficulty At The Same Time</h4>\n          <p>\n            A good task has to be fair and hard at once. Fair means the correcting fact lives somewhere in the corpus, every file opens with standard tooling, and all defensible cleanings converge on the same answer. Hard means a strong analyst, or a strong model, still gets it wrong on the first pass. The input files also had to be real and properly licensed (public domain or Creative Commons only, verified per dataset) and never model generated, with six or more files across a variety of formats and several of them load bearing. Satisfying every one of those at once, on real data, is most of the work.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Traps I Built And Verified</h4>\n          <p>\n            Every trap below was implemented against real data and checked with code so the intended answer reproduced exactly before anything shipped. Each one pairs a headline number that is real, easy to compute, and misleading with a correcting analysis that a rushed solver skips.\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Composition shift (Simpson's paradox).</strong> A blended retention or conversion metric that declines quarter over quarter while every underlying segment is flat or improving, because the mix of segments moved. The naive read (the product is getting worse) is refuted by a segmentation the solver has to discover and join across files on a mismatched key.</li>\n            <li><strong>Outlier artifact in an A/B revenue test.</strong> On a real experiment dataset, one arm's revenue looked competitive, but 123 users (0.06 percent of the group) each paid a near identical anomalous amount and accounted for 89.4 percent of that arm's revenue, while the other arm had none. The correct call requires recognizing the cluster as non genuine, handling the zero inflated heavy tailed distribution properly (decompose conversion times value, use outlier resistant tests), and flipping the decision the raw averages imply.</li>\n            <li><strong>Confounded and circular metrics.</strong> A composite health score presented as independent corroboration that embeds the very metric under investigation, so citing it double counts one signal. The same family covers correlation dressed up as causation on observational data.</li>\n            <li><strong>Mismatched population merges.</strong> A distractor file with similar looking columns that covers a different population entirely (different platform, window, or cohort), tempting a solver to merge it in and report a figure that does not apply to the group in question.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>The Final Task: An Engagement Feature Decision On Real Learning Platform Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            The most fully developed task used the Open University Learning Analytics Dataset (real virtual learning environment click logs, registrations, and outcomes for tens of thousands of students, released under CC BY). The business question was which one engagement initiative to fund to raise course pass rates: a richer per session experience, or a nudge to return on more days. A shipped internal pre read recommended the per session option, backed by a real and reproducible correlation (total clicks versus passing at r = 0.56, with pass rate climbing from 0.3 percent to 91.9 percent across activity quintiles).\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            That correlation is the trap. Total activity confounds two distinct levers: how many days a student returns, and how much they do per session. Decompose clicks into active days times clicks per active day, regress both against passing, and the entire association lives in return frequency (standardized effect around 0.24, and about 0.34 with all students included). Per session intensity collapses to essentially zero once days present is controlled. The result held within every module and whether or not withdrawn students were included, so the deterministic answer is to fund return frequency, not intensity. I built two corroborating decoy artifacts pointing at the wrong lever and verified that every figure reproduced from the raw files. The task cleared the program's automated construction and determinism checks.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Why This Is A Happy Failure</h4>\n          <p>\n            It did not stump the models. In repeated live rollouts, strong frontier models independently rebuilt the analysis from the raw files, ran the frequency versus intensity decomposition, ignored the decoy pre reads, and reached the correct answer, often quoting the exact adjusted numbers. Hardening the framing and adding more authoritative decoys did not change the outcome. The move that cracks the task, decompose a composite metric and test the parts, is a reflexive step for a capable analyst, so a frontier model does it whether or not the prompt signposts it.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            Stumping a frontier model on a clean, fair analytics problem requires a trap that sits outside a strong reasoner's default repertoire, which is a far higher bar than \"a smart person would need to be careful here.\" I watched capable models reject a plausible but confounded headline artifact and reconstruct the correct causal story from first principles. That is a calibrated data point about where these systems are now strong, and it changed how I think about designing evaluations for them.\n          </p>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>I built data analysis puzzles meant to trip up the strongest AI models, for a program with strict rules. Each puzzle had to be a real business question about how people use a digital product, with one right answer that ten experts would agree on, answerable from the files provided, and hard enough that top AI models would get it wrong. Across eight model attempts, at least two of the first four and one of the second four had to land on a clearly different answer. The difficulty had to come from the analysis, not from vague wording.</p>\n<p>The files had to be real, openly licensed data, never written by a model.</p>\n<h4>The Traps</h4>\n<p>Each trap pairs a headline number that is real, easy to compute and misleading with a deeper check that a rushed analyst skips. I built four kinds and checked each in code, so the intended answer reproduced exactly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A shift in the mix.</strong> An overall rate falls while every group inside it holds steady or improves, because the mix of groups changed.</li>\n<li><strong>A strange cluster in a test of two versions.</strong> In a real experiment, 123 users, 0.06 percent of one group, each paid almost the same unusual amount and made up 89.4 percent of that group&rsquo;s revenue. Spotting them flips the decision the raw averages suggest.</li>\n<li><strong>A score that counts one thing twice.</strong> A health score offered as separate evidence that already contains the number under question. The same family covers treating a correlation as a cause.</li>\n<li><strong>A file about the wrong people.</strong> A file with similar looking columns that covers a different group, tempting the solver to merge it in.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The Final Puzzle</h4>\n<p>The most developed one used real click logs from the Open University. The question was which one initiative to fund to raise pass rates: richer study sessions, or nudges to come back on more days. A memo in the files recommended richer sessions, citing a real pattern: the pass rate climbs from 0.3 percent in the least active fifth of students to 91.9 percent in the most active.</p>\n<p>That pattern is the trap. Total clicks mixes two things: how many days a student comes back, and how much they do each time. Separate them and the whole link sits with coming back more often, while effort per session drops to about zero. That held in every course module, so the right answer is to fund return visits. I added two decoy documents pointing the wrong way.</p>\n<h4>What Happened</h4>\n<p>The models were not fooled. In repeated live runs, strong AI models rebuilt the analysis from the raw files, separated the clicks the same way, ignored the decoys and reached the right answer. Harder framing and more convincing decoys did not change that. Breaking a combined number into its parts is a reflex for a capable analyst, so the models did it whether or not the question hinted at it.</p>\n<p>The puzzle passed the program&rsquo;s automated checks but did not stump the models. To stump a top model on a clean, fair problem, a trap has to sit outside what a strong reasoner does by default, a far higher bar than a problem where a smart person would need to be careful.</p>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can a fair, deterministic analytics task on real data stump frontier models?", "d": "The goal was a product analytics question that a panel of frontier models would fail, with difficulty from methodology and reasoning, never from vague wording or a trick definition."}, {"k": "data", "t": "The program brief", "d": "A user behaviour question driving a product, growth or monetization decision, with one deterministic recommendation that ten domain experts would independently agree on, reconstructible entirely from the shipped files."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Real, licensed, multi file corpora", "d": "Public domain or Creative Commons only, verified per dataset, never model generated, six or more files across formats with several load bearing. 5 datasets were profiled."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the task fair?", "d": "Fair means the correcting fact is in the corpus, every file opens with standard tooling, and all defensible cleanings converge on one answer.", "yes": "It still has to be hard: a strong analyst or model gets it wrong on the first pass.", "no": "It fails the brief, however hard it is."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Four trap families, each with a real but misleading headline", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Composition shift (Simpson's paradox)", "d": "A blended metric declines while every segment is flat or improving, because the mix moved. The refuting segmentation needs a join across files on a mismatched key."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Outlier artifact in an A/B revenue test", "d": "On a real experiment, 123 users (0.06 percent of the group) paid a near identical anomalous amount and made 89.4 percent of that arm's revenue. Handling it flips the decision the raw averages imply."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Confounded and circular metrics", "d": "A composite health score embeds the metric under investigation, so citing it double counts one signal. The family also covers correlation read as causation on observational data."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Mismatched population merges", "d": "A distractor file with similar looking columns covers a different platform, window or cohort, tempting a merge that reports a figure for the wrong group."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Verify each trap in code before shipping", "d": "Deterministic golden solutions were reproduced to the digit from real data, so the intended answer was exact before anything shipped."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Open University Learning Analytics Dataset (CC BY)", "d": "Real virtual learning environment click logs, registrations and outcomes for tens of thousands of students."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Pose one funding decision", "d": "Which single engagement initiative raises course pass rates: a richer per session experience, or a nudge to return on more days?"}, {"k": "result", "t": "The headline: total clicks versus passing at r = 0.56", "d": "Real and reproducible. Pass rate climbs from 0.3 percent to 91.9 percent across activity quintiles, and a shipped internal pre read uses it to recommend the per session option."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Decompose clicks into active days times clicks per active day", "d": "Total activity confounds two levers: how many days a student returns and how much they do per session. Both are regressed against passing."}, {"k": "result", "t": "The whole association lives in return frequency", "d": "Standardized effect around 0.24, and about 0.34 with all students included. Per session intensity collapses to essentially zero once days present is controlled."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does it hold within every module, with and without withdrawn students?", "yes": "It held both ways, so the deterministic answer is to fund return frequency, not intensity.", "no": "The task would have no single deterministic answer."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Add two decoy artifacts pointing at the wrong lever", "d": "Both corroborate the per session option. Every figure in the task was verified to reproduce from the raw files."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the task clear the program's automated checks?", "d": "Construction and determinism checks.", "yes": "It cleared both.", "no": "The task would fail the program's construction requirements."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is it a valid stump across eight model responses?", "d": "The bar: at least two of the first four and one of the second four land on a meaningfully different answer.", "yes": "The task would count as a stump.", "no": "It did not stump the models."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Frontier models solved it in repeated live rollouts", "d": "They rebuilt the analysis from the raw files, ran the frequency versus intensity decomposition, ignored the decoy pre reads and reached the correct answer, often quoting the exact adjusted numbers."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Did harder framing or more authoritative decoys change the outcome?", "yes": "The task would have become a stump.", "no": "The models still solved it."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The cracking move is reflexive for a capable analyst", "d": "Decomposing a composite metric and testing the parts is a default step, so a frontier model does it whether or not the prompt signposts it."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "A negative result: frontier models solved the clean traps. Stumping them fairly needs a trap outside a strong reasoner's default repertoire, a far higher bar than one where a smart person would need to be careful."}]}, {"title": "media-pulse: Communications Measurement with Human-in-the-Loop QA", "meta": "Applied NLP &middot; Human in the Loop &middot; Aug 2026", "badge": "topic acc 0.88 &rarr; 0.96 with review &middot; sentiment 0.50 &rarr; 0.85 &middot; ECE 0.156", "tags": ["Python", "Scikit learn", "VADER", "TF-IDF", "Logistic Regression", "Streamlit", "Calibration (ECE)", "Human in the Loop", "Responsible AI"], "body": "<div class='metric'>120 sample mentions &middot; topic accuracy 0.88 &rarr; 0.96 after review &middot; sentiment 0.50 &rarr; 0.85 &middot; topic ECE 0.156 &middot; held out test n = 48</div>\n\n<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>media-pulse runs the full communications measurement loop in one system, from ingestion through reporting. It combines AI classification with human quality assurance, media and sentiment analytics, internal tooling for non technical teams, and responsible AI practice.</p>\n\n<h4>What It Does</h4>\n<p>Ingestion is pluggable: a bundled sample source ships with the project and an RSS adapter shows how a live feed drops into the same schema without changing anything downstream. Each mention is scored for sentiment (VADER) and topic (a TF-IDF and logistic regression model), with a confidence attached to every prediction. It also gets message pull through detection for pre approved key messages and a relevance score.</p>\n\n<h4>Human in the Loop</h4>\n<p>Any prediction whose confidence falls below a threshold goes to a review queue instead of being trusted. A reviewer confirms or corrects it, the corrected label overwrites the model output, and every action is written to an immutable audit log. On the sample corpus this lifts held out topic accuracy from 0.88 to 0.96 and sentiment accuracy from 0.50 to 0.85, and the dashboard shows the uplift directly. Large citizen science classification pipelines use the same pattern: the model does the bulk of the work and humans adjudicate the uncertain tail.</p>\n\n<h4>Responsible AI</h4>\n<p>Alongside the business metrics (coverage over time, sentiment mix, share of voice weighted by outlet reach, message pull through), the system runs a quality review of the classifier itself: accuracy on a held out test set, expected calibration error to check that the confidence scores are honest, and an accuracy breakdown by outlet type that shows where the model is weakest.</p>\n\n<h4>Runs Anywhere</h4>\n<p>A four tab Streamlit dashboard (overview, coverage, review queue, model QA) sits on top. The project falls back to bundled numpy and lexicon models when scikit-learn or VADER are not installed, so it runs offline and switches to the production models automatically once the requirements are in place.</p>\n\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Synthetic corpus.</strong> The data is a deterministic sample about a fictional brand, built to exercise the pipeline, not a live media feed.</li>\n<li><strong>Fallback models are weaker.</strong> The lexicon sentiment model used offline is less accurate than a hosted model, which is part of why the human review uplift is large.</li>\n<li><strong>Single node.</strong> It runs on one machine, not at production stream volume.</li>\n</ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>Communications teams track what the news says about their brand: how much coverage there is, what it is about, whether it is positive or negative, and whether their key messages got through. media-pulse does that whole job in one system, from collecting the mentions to a dashboard that people outside the technical team can use.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<p>Each mention gets a mood label (how positive or negative it is, called sentiment) and a topic label, each from a small model, and each with a score for how confident the model is. The system also checks whether the mention repeats one of the brand&rsquo;s approved key messages, and how relevant it is.</p>\n<p>Any label the model is unsure about goes to a review queue instead of being trusted. A person confirms or corrects it, the correction replaces the model&rsquo;s label, and every action is written to a log that cannot be changed. The model does most of the work, and people settle the uncertain cases.</p>\n<p>Mentions come in through a swappable source, so a live news feed can plug into the same system without changes further along. A four tab dashboard shows the overview, coverage, the review queue, and how well the model is doing, including where it is weakest by type of outlet.</p>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<p>The test used 120 made up sample mentions about a fictional brand. On the 48 of them held back for testing, human review raised topic accuracy from 88% to 96%, and sentiment accuracy from 50% to 85%, on that made up sample.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The mentions are made up.</strong> They are a fixed sample about a fictional brand, built to exercise the pipeline, not a live news feed.</li>\n<li><strong>The offline models are weaker.</strong> Without the full software installed, the system uses simpler built in models, including a word list for sentiment that is less accurate than a hosted model. That is part of why the gain from human review is so large.</li>\n<li><strong>One machine.</strong> It runs on a single computer, not at the volume of a real news stream.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can one system run the whole communications measurement loop, with humans catching uncertain predictions?", "d": "media-pulse goes from ingestion through reporting: AI classification with human quality assurance, media and sentiment analytics, and a dashboard for non technical teams."}, {"k": "data", "t": "120 sample mentions, synthetic", "d": "A deterministic sample about a fictional brand, bundled with the project, built to exercise the pipeline rather than a live media feed."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Pluggable ingestion", "d": "The bundled sample source and an RSS adapter write the same schema, so a live feed drops in without changing anything downstream."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Every mention is scored, with a confidence on each prediction", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Sentiment: VADER", "d": "A sentiment label and its confidence."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Topic: TF-IDF and logistic regression", "d": "A topic label and its confidence."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Message pull through and relevance", "d": "Detects pre approved key messages and assigns a relevance score."}]]}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the prediction&rsquo;s confidence below the threshold?", "d": "Every prediction is compared against a confidence threshold.", "yes": "It goes to the review queue instead of being trusted.", "no": "The model&rsquo;s label stands."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Human review", "d": "A reviewer confirms or corrects the label, the corrected label overwrites the model output, and every action is written to an immutable audit log."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Accuracy before and after review, held out test n = 48, synthetic sample", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "Topic accuracy 0.88 &rarr; 0.96", "d": "On the held out 48 of the 120 synthetic mentions."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Sentiment accuracy 0.50 &rarr; 0.85", "d": "On the same synthetic test set. The offline lexicon model is weaker than a hosted model, which is part of why this uplift is large."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Model quality review", "d": "Accuracy on the held out test set, expected calibration error to check that the confidence scores are honest, and an accuracy breakdown by outlet type that shows where the model is weakest."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Topic ECE 0.156", "d": "Expected calibration error of the topic model on the held out synthetic test set (n = 48)."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Business metrics", "d": "Coverage over time, sentiment mix, share of voice weighted by outlet reach, and message pull through."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Four tab Streamlit dashboard", "d": "Overview, coverage, review queue, and model QA, with the review uplift shown directly."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Are scikit-learn and VADER installed?", "d": "The project chooses its models at run time.", "yes": "It switches to the production models automatically.", "no": "It falls back to bundled numpy and lexicon models, so it still runs offline."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Synthetic corpus", "d": "The 120 mentions are a deterministic sample about a fictional brand, so the accuracy figures describe the pipeline on that sample, not performance on real coverage."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Fallback models are weaker", "d": "The lexicon sentiment model used offline is less accurate than a hosted model, which is part of why the human review uplift is large."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Single node", "d": "It runs on one machine, not at production stream volume."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "Routing low confidence predictions to a reviewer raised topic accuracy from 0.88 to 0.96 and sentiment from 0.50 to 0.85 on a 48 mention synthetic test set. It shows the loop works, not how accurate it is on real media."}]}, {"title": "Multi Horizon Geomagnetic Kp Index Prediction", "meta": "Deep Learning &middot; 2026", "badge": "45% better than baseline &middot; 96K+ hourly obs", "tags": ["LSTM", "TensorFlow", "Keras", "Time Series", "NASA OMNI2", "Multi Output", "Regularization"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">1h RMSE = 0.738 (45% better than baseline) &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1h MAE = 0.476 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1h R&sup2; = 0.690 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 96,000+ hourly observations</div>\n\n          <h4>The Problem</h4>\n          <p>\n            Geomagnetic storms come from solar wind disturbances interacting with Earth's magnetosphere, and they have already done real damage. The 1989 Quebec blackout cut power to six million people for nine hours. The 2003 Halloween storms damaged satellites and disrupted aviation. The economic exposure of severe storms is in the billions of dollars. Operators need lead time, ideally six to twelve hours, to safe satellites, derate transformers, and reroute aviation away from polar GPS dead zones. The Kp index (a 0 to 9 quasi logarithmic scale measured every three hours) is the standard operational trigger. A better Kp forecast at multiple horizons gives operators more lead time to act.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Stakeholders and Why Multi Horizon Matters</h4>\n          <p>\n            Different operators need different lead times. Satellite mission controllers need 6 to 12 hour notice to plan orbital maneuvers and power down sensitive instruments. National grid operators need a few hours to derate transformers ahead of geomagnetically induced currents. GPS dependent industries (aviation, surveying, autonomous vehicles, precision agriculture) act on shorter horizons. So the model predicts all four horizons in one forward pass instead of training four separate models. Four separate forecasts might silently disagree with each other; one forward pass gives a single, internally consistent forecast across the full operational range.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            The data comes from NASA's OMNI2 high resolution database, which holds hourly measurements of solar wind and interplanetary magnetic field parameters. It covers eleven years (January 2013 to December 2024), 96,552 hourly records in total. The window spans the declining phase of solar cycle 24, the 2019 to 2020 solar minimum, and the rising phase of solar cycle 25, so the model sees both quiet and active regimes.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            From the original 55 parameters I selected 15 features, based on physics literature and correlation analysis against Kp. Feature importance fell into clear physical categories:\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/aurora_-000.jpg\" alt=\"Pearson correlation of 15 selected features with Kp, grouped by category\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 1. Pearson correlation of selected features with Kp, grouped by IMF, solar wind, derived, and geomagnetic indices</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <p>\n            The strongest predictor was ap_Index (r = 0.825), followed by auroral electrojet indices (AE r = 0.732, AL r = -0.663, AU r = 0.690). IMF_Bz_GSM correlated more weakly than expected (r = -0.238), because the dataset includes all periods rather than just storm windows.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Architecture</h4>\n          <p>\n            The model is a two layer LSTM:\n          </p>\n          <table>\n            <thead>\n              <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th></tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Input sequence length</td><td>24 hours (24 timesteps &times; 15 features)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Output</td><td>4 predictions (Kp_1h, Kp_3h, Kp_6h, Kp_12h)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Loss function</td><td>Mean Squared Error</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Optimizer</td><td>Adam, initial learning rate 0.001</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Batch size</td><td>64 sequences</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Epochs</td><td>Maximum 50, early stopping at epoch 39</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Normalization</td><td>StandardScaler on all features</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <p>\n            The split is chronological (50% train / 16.6% validation / 16.6% test), with 8,761 hour temporal gaps between sets to prevent leakage, because in a time series the sequential boundary matters. Training converged cleanly, with the best validation loss at epoch 39. After that, validation loss plateaued while training loss kept improving (the textbook overfit signature, which early stopping caught).\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/aurora_-001.jpg\" alt=\"Training and validation loss curves over 49 epochs\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 2. Training and validation loss over epochs, with best epoch 39 marked</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Results by Horizon</h4>\n\n          <table>\n            <thead>\n              <tr><th>Horizon</th><th>RMSE</th><th>MAE</th><th>R&sup2;</th><th>Baseline RMSE</th><th>Improvement</th></tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>1 hour</strong></td><td><strong>0.738</strong></td><td><strong>0.476</strong></td><td><strong>0.690</strong></td><td>1.34</td><td><strong>+44.9%</strong></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>3 hours</td><td>0.910</td><td>0.655</td><td>0.528</td><td>1.34</td><td>+32.1%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>6 hours</td><td>1.073</td><td>0.793</td><td>0.343</td><td>1.34</td><td>+19.9%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>12 hours</td><td>1.207</td><td>0.906</td><td>0.170</td><td>1.34</td><td>+9.9%</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/aurora_-002.jpg\" alt=\"LSTM RMSE vs mean baseline RMSE for all four horizons\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 3. RMSE of the LSTM vs the mean baseline across 1, 3, 6, and 12 hour horizons</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <p>\n            Performance degrades smoothly with horizon, as it should for any forecasting system, since the signal to noise ratio falls with lead time. The 1 hour horizon is the most useful for short term tactical actions (GPS protection, immediate satellite ops). The 6 and 12 hour horizons carry less confidence but still support strategic planning.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Behavior During Real Storm Events</h4>\n          <p>\n            The most useful diagnostic is whether the model can flag a storm coming, even imperfectly. Below is a 200 hour window from the test set covering a real storm event (red shaded zone, where actual Kp crosses the threshold of 5). All four horizons capture the storm onset, but with different lag and magnitude. The 1 hour forecast tracks actual Kp closely; the 12 hour forecast smooths the peak but still indicates heightened activity.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/aurora_-003.jpg\" alt=\"Multi-horizon LSTM predictions vs actual Kp over a 200 hour test window\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 4. Multi horizon LSTM predictions vs actual Kp over a 200 hour test window, storm period highlighted in red</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Avoiding the Data Leakage Trap</h4>\n          <p>\n            Geomagnetic indices like ap, AE, and AL are strongly correlated with Kp because they all derive from magnetometer measurements responding to the same underlying phenomena. That is a real leakage risk if the model accidentally uses concurrent index values to predict Kp. I verified that the indices are 3 hour averaged values (not instantaneous), that the 24 hour lookback window uses strictly historical values to predict future Kp, and that the 1 year temporal gaps between train, validation, and test sets prevent any contamination across the splits. Operational forecasting literature confirms that including these indices is standard practice.\n          </p>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Operational Reading</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              The 1 hour forecast clears a useful operational bar: 45% better RMSE than naive mean prediction, and R&sup2; of 0.690 (the model explains roughly two thirds of variance in storm intensity). At longer horizons the model degrades but stays informative. One model gives the immediate alarm and a softer signal further out, instead of four separate models that might silently disagree.\n            </p>\n          </div>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>Geomagnetic storms happen when disturbances in the solar wind, the stream of particles flowing from the Sun, hit Earth&rsquo;s magnetic field. They have done real damage: the 1989 Quebec blackout cut power to six million people for nine hours. Satellite controllers, power grid operators, and industries that depend on GPS all need warning, but on different timescales.</p>\n<p>The standard warning signal is the Kp index, a 0 to 9 scale of how disturbed Earth&rsquo;s magnetic field is. I built a model that predicts Kp 1, 3, 6, and 12 hours ahead in one go, so the four forecasts come from the same model and stay consistent with each other.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<p>The data is eleven years of hourly NASA measurements of the solar wind and of the magnetic field in interplanetary space, 96,552 hours in all. I picked 15 of the 55 measurements, based on the physics and on how closely each one tracks Kp. The model is a neural network built for sequences (an LSTM). It reads the previous 24 hours and outputs all four forecasts.</p>\n<p>I trained it on the earliest years and tested it on later years it had never seen, with a gap of a year between each part so nothing leaked across. Some inputs are other magnetic activity measures that move closely with Kp, so I checked that the model only sees their past values, never the values at the moment it is predicting.</p>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One hour ahead,</strong> the model&rsquo;s error is 45% lower than always guessing the average Kp. It accounts for roughly two thirds of the ups and downs in Kp.</li>\n<li><strong>Further ahead it gets worse.</strong> The gain over guessing the average shrinks steadily, to 9.9% at 12 hours.</li>\n<li><strong>On a real storm in the test years,</strong> all four forecasts caught the start of the storm. The 1 hour forecast tracked Kp closely. The 12 hour forecast smoothed over the peak but still showed heightened activity.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The 45% gain is measured against a naive forecast that always guesses the average.</li>\n<li>At 6 and 12 hours the model explains much less of the movement in Kp, so those forecasts are a softer signal, suited to planning rather than an immediate alarm.</li>\n<li>The storm check covers one real storm, in a 200 hour window.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can one model forecast the Kp index 1, 3, 6, and 12 hours ahead?", "d": "Operators need different lead times: satellite controllers 6 to 12 hours, grid operators a few hours, GPS dependent industries less. Kp, a 0 to 9 quasi logarithmic scale measured every three hours, is the standard operational trigger."}, {"k": "data", "t": "NASA OMNI2 hourly solar wind and IMF data", "d": "January 2013 to December 2024, 96,552 hourly records. The window spans the decline of solar cycle 24, the 2019 to 2020 minimum, and the rise of cycle 25, so the model sees quiet and active regimes."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Select 15 of 55 parameters", "d": "Features are chosen from physics literature and correlation analysis against Kp, and fall into IMF, solar wind, derived, and geomagnetic index groups."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Geomagnetic indices correlate most with Kp", "d": "ap_Index r = 0.825, then AE r = 0.732, AL r = -0.663, AU r = 0.690. IMF_Bz_GSM is weaker than expected (r = -0.238) because the dataset includes all periods, not only storm windows."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do the correlated indices leak future Kp into the inputs?", "d": "ap, AE, and AL derive from the same magnetometer measurements as Kp.", "yes": "The model would be reading Kp off concurrent index values.", "no": "Verified: the indices are 3 hour averages, the 24 hour lookback uses strictly historical values, and 1 year gaps separate the splits. Forecasting literature treats these inputs as standard practice."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Chronological split with gaps", "d": "50% train, 16.6% validation, 16.6% test, in time order, with 8,761 hour gaps between sets to prevent leakage."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Two layer LSTM, four outputs in one forward pass", "d": "Input is 24 timesteps &times; 15 features, output Kp_1h, Kp_3h, Kp_6h, Kp_12h. Mean squared error loss, Adam at learning rate 0.001, batch 64, StandardScaler on all features."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is validation loss still improving?", "d": "Early stopping watches validation loss, with a maximum of 50 epochs.", "yes": "Training continues.", "no": "After epoch 39 validation loss plateaued while training loss kept improving, so early stopping kept the epoch 39 model."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Test results by horizon, against a mean baseline RMSE of 1.34", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "1 hour: RMSE 0.738, +44.9%", "d": "MAE 0.476, R&sup2; 0.690. The model explains roughly two thirds of the variance."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "3 hours: RMSE 0.910, +32.1%", "d": "MAE 0.655, R&sup2; 0.528."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "6 hours: RMSE 1.073, +19.9%", "d": "MAE 0.793, R&sup2; 0.343."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "12 hours: RMSE 1.207, +9.9%", "d": "MAE 0.906, R&sup2; 0.170."}]]}, {"k": "result", "t": "Accuracy degrades smoothly with horizon", "d": "The signal to noise ratio falls with lead time. The 1 hour forecast suits tactical actions such as GPS protection; the 6 and 12 hour forecasts carry less confidence but support strategic planning."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do the forecasts flag a real storm in the test set?", "d": "A 200 hour test window around a real storm, where actual Kp crosses the threshold of 5.", "yes": "All four horizons capture the onset, with different lag and magnitude. The 1 hour forecast tracks Kp closely; the 12 hour one smooths the peak but still indicates heightened activity.", "no": "The model would be no use as an alarm."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "The baseline is naive", "d": "The 45% gain is against always predicting the mean Kp."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Longer horizons are a softer signal", "d": "R&sup2; falls to 0.343 at 6 hours and 0.170 at 12 hours, so those forecasts support planning more than an immediate alarm."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "One storm window", "d": "Storm behavior is shown on a single 200 hour test window."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "The 1 hour forecast is 45% better than the mean baseline and explains roughly two thirds of the variance; longer horizons degrade but stay informative. The comparison is against a naive baseline, and storm behavior rests on one window."}]}, {"title": "The Perfect Lap: F1 Pit Stop Strategy Optimization", "meta": "Machine Learning &middot; Jul 2025 to Dec 2025", "badge": "2.78-lap MAE &middot; 250K+ records &middot; Streamlit deployed", "tags": ["Random Forest", "Python", "Scikit learn", "Streamlit", "SHAP", "Pandas", "CatBoost", "Extra Trees"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">Test MAE = 2.78 laps &nbsp;|&nbsp; Test R&sup2; = 0.591 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 250,000+ records &nbsp;|&nbsp; 45 engineered features &nbsp;|&nbsp; Streamlit deployed</div>\n\n          <h4>The Problem</h4>\n          <p>\n            Few calls in motorsport carry more weight than pit stop timing. A miscall on lap selection can cost a team between two and five constructor championship positions over a season, which means millions of dollars in lost prize money and sponsorship value. Race engineers make these calls in the moment, with limited time, from experience and real time telemetry. The premise of this project was that if a model can predict the optimal lap accurately enough, it becomes a live sanity check that the human strategist either validates or knowingly overrides, instead of a guess they make alone.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Stakeholders and Value</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Race engineers</strong> get a predicted first pit lap on race day, a baseline they can use to justify or override their intuition.</li>\n            <li><strong>Strategy directors</strong> get patterns across decades of races for pre season planning, such as circuit and race length outweighing driver identity.</li>\n            <li><strong>Broadcast analysts and fantasy F1 platforms</strong> get a predicted pit lap, and the features that drove it, to explain to viewers.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Data and Cleaning</h4>\n          <p>\n            The source is the Formula 1 World Championship dataset on Kaggle (1950 to 2020), with 250,000+ records across thirteen interconnected tables (races, results, pit stops, drivers, constructors, lap times, qualifying, status codes, circuits, and more). I added a custom <code>track_features</code> table with tire degradation level and pit lane time loss per circuit, because these physical factors drive strategy and the raw schedule data does not capture them well. The data was filtered to post 2000 races to focus on the modern tire era, since pre 2000 strategy was driven by fuel weight rather than tire compounds. The final modeling dataset is 14,000+ driver race rows with 45 engineered features.\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            Cleaning removed invalid pit lap records (below lap 1 or above lap 40 are data entry errors), stripped drivers with more than two pit stops per race (these signal damage or weather chaos rather than normal strategy), and dropped early DNFs so the training set reflects strategic behavior rather than mechanical failures.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Recovering Missing Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            Pit records before 2012 were sparse. Dropping every row with a missing pit lap would have shrunk the dataset from 10,000 rows to 3,000. I trained a dedicated Random Forest imputer that recovered roughly 70% of missing first pit laps using six predictors that are reliably present even in older races: driver ID, constructor ID, race year, starting grid position, total race laps, and circuit ID. Imputation MAE was about six laps on a held out validation set, which is tolerable given pit windows typically span ten to fifteen laps.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Models Compared</h4>\n          <p>\n            I compared four tree based regressors plus a decision tree baseline, with identical splits and cross validation. I left out neural networks and SVMs on purpose, because they are computationally heavier and consistently underperform tree ensembles on tabular data with this feature mix.\n          </p>\n\n          <table>\n            <thead>\n              <tr><th>Model</th><th>Train MAE</th><th>Test MAE</th><th>Train R&sup2;</th><th>Test R&sup2;</th><th>Status</th></tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>Random Forest</strong></td><td>1.007</td><td><strong>2.78</strong></td><td>0.943</td><td><strong>0.591</strong></td><td>Selected</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Extra Trees</td><td>~0</td><td>2.91</td><td>1.000</td><td>0.551</td><td>Overfits</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>CatBoost</td><td>2.540</td><td>3.02</td><td>0.702</td><td>0.523</td><td>2nd best</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Gradient Boosting</td><td>3.234</td><td>3.15</td><td>0.564</td><td>0.487</td><td>Weakest</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <p>\n            Random Forest reached a Test MAE of 2.78 laps and Test R&sup2; of 0.591, so on average its predicted pit lap is roughly three laps from what teams did. Extra Trees showed near perfect training scores but worse generalization, the classic memorization signature. Gradient Boosting struggled with the noise introduced by safety cars and weather changes, since it prefers smoother data patterns. CatBoost would normally have a categorical handling advantage, but our pipeline pre encoded categorical variables for fair comparison, which removed that edge.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/f1_01.png\" alt=\"Feature importance comparison across four models\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 1. Feature importance across Random Forest, Extra Trees, Gradient Boosting, and CatBoost</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>What the Model Learned</h4>\n          <p>\n            SHAP analysis on the Random Forest model shows that race structure features dominate the prediction. The top five contributors are total laps, planned pit stop count, year (proxying for regulation changes), round, and starting grid position. Driver and constructor identities mattered considerably less than F1 commentary tradition suggests.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/f1_03.png\" alt=\"SHAP beeswarm plot of feature impact on model output\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 2. SHAP beeswarm showing direction and magnitude of each feature's impact on predicted pit lap</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Practical Implication</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              Pit timing is more a property of the physical system (circuit, race length, planned stops) than of the person behind the wheel. For engineers, that means adapting strategy to circuit and race length first, team history second, driver style third.\n            </p>\n          </div>\n\n          <h4>Deployment</h4>\n          <p>\n            The final Random Forest model was serialized and packaged into a Streamlit web application (<code>team_omega_rf_app.py</code>). Race engineers input live race context (circuit, grid position, planned stops, weather) and the app returns a predicted first pit stop lap in real time, alongside the SHAP feature importances that drove the prediction. Serialized model artifacts (<code>f1_random_forest_model.pkl</code>, <code>model_features.pkl</code>) live in the project repository.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Limitations</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>No live race signals.</strong> Safety car deployments, sudden weather changes, and real time tire telemetry are absent. These drive real pit decisions, so there is an information gap between the static model and live race conditions.</li>\n            <li><strong>First stop only.</strong> The model predicts only the first pit lap, not full multi stop strategies. Later stops depend on the first, and the current approach does not capture that dependency.</li>\n            <li><strong>No competitor model.</strong> Real strategy reacts to opponents (undercuts, overcuts). The model encodes neither opponent positions nor gap sizes.</li>\n            <li><strong>Regulation drift.</strong> F1 rules change often. Patterns from early 2000s races may not apply directly to current regulations, which slightly dilutes accuracy when predicting contemporary strategy.</li>\n          </ul>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>In Formula 1, few calls matter more than when a car comes into the pits, and race engineers make that call in the moment, from experience and live data. I built a model that predicts the lap of a driver&rsquo;s first pit stop, meant as a second opinion the strategist can accept or knowingly overrule. It runs as a small web app: enter the circuit, grid position, planned stops and weather, and it returns a predicted lap and the factors behind it.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<p>The data is a public Kaggle dataset with more than 250,000 records of Formula 1 results across thirteen linked tables. I added a table of my own with how hard each circuit is on tires and how much time a pit stop costs there. I kept only races from 2000 on, the modern tire era, since earlier strategy was driven by fuel weight. I also removed records that would mislead the model: pit laps that were data entry errors, drivers with more than two stops (usually damage or weather chaos), and cars that retired early.</p>\n<p>Older races often had no pit record. Throwing those rows away would have discarded most of the data, so I trained a separate model to fill in the missing first pit laps from details every race has, such as the year, the circuit and the starting position. It filled in roughly 70% of the gaps, and on data held back for checking its guesses were about six laps off on average, tolerable when a pit window usually spans ten to fifteen laps.</p>\n<p>I then compared four models built from many simple decision rules, known as tree based models, on identical splits of the data, and kept the best one, a Random Forest.</p>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<p>On test data, the Random Forest&rsquo;s predicted first stop was on average 2.78 laps from the lap the team pitted on. Its R&sup2;, a measure of how much of the lap to lap variation it explains, was 0.591. One rival, Extra Trees, scored almost perfectly on its training data but worse on new data, the classic sign of memorizing instead of learning.</p>\n<p>The biggest factors were the structure of the race: total laps, the number of planned stops, the year (a stand in for rule changes), the round of the season, and the starting position. Who was driving, and for which team, mattered much less than F1 commentary tends to suggest. For engineers, that points to planning around the circuit and race length first, team history second and driver style third.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No live race events.</strong> Safety cars, sudden weather changes and live tire readings are missing, and they drive real pit decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>First stop only.</strong> It does not predict later stops, which depend on the first.</li>\n<li><strong>No rivals.</strong> Real strategy reacts to other cars, and the model knows nothing about their positions or the gaps between them.</li>\n<li><strong>Rules change.</strong> Patterns from early 2000s races may not apply directly to today&rsquo;s rules.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can a model predict a driver's first pit stop lap from race data?", "d": "The premise: an accurate enough prediction becomes a live sanity check that the strategist validates or knowingly overrides, instead of a call made alone from experience and real time telemetry."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Formula 1 World Championship dataset on Kaggle, 1950 to 2020", "d": "250,000+ records across thirteen interconnected tables: races, results, pit stops, drivers, constructors, lap times, qualifying, status codes, circuits and more."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Custom <code>track_features</code> table", "d": "Tire degradation level and pit lane time loss per circuit, added because these physical factors drive strategy and the raw schedule data does not capture them well."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Filter to post 2000 races", "d": "Keeps the modern tire era, since pre 2000 strategy was driven by fuel weight rather than tire compounds."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the record reflect normal strategic behaviour?", "d": "Pit laps below lap 1 or above lap 40 are data entry errors; more than two stops signals damage or weather chaos; early DNFs are mechanical failures.", "yes": "The record stays in the training set.", "no": "It is removed."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the first pit lap missing?", "d": "Pit records before 2012 were sparse. Dropping every row with a missing pit lap would shrink the dataset from 10,000 rows to 3,000.", "yes": "A dedicated imputer fills it in.", "no": "The recorded lap is used."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Random Forest imputer on six reliably present predictors", "d": "Driver ID, constructor ID, race year, starting grid position, total race laps and circuit ID, all present even in older races."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Roughly 70% of missing first pit laps recovered", "d": "Imputation MAE was about six laps on a held out validation set, tolerable given pit windows typically span ten to fifteen laps."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Modeling dataset: 14,000+ driver race rows, 45 engineered features", "d": "Neural networks and SVMs were left out on purpose, as computationally heavier and consistently weaker than tree ensembles on tabular data with this feature mix."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Four tree based regressors, identical splits and cross validation", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "Random Forest: Test MAE 2.78, Test R&sup2; 0.591", "d": "Train MAE 1.007 and Train R&sup2; 0.943. Selected."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Extra Trees: Test MAE 2.91, Test R&sup2; 0.551", "d": "Train MAE ~0 and Train R&sup2; 1.000. Overfits."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "CatBoost: Test MAE 3.02, Test R&sup2; 0.523", "d": "Train MAE 2.540 and Train R&sup2; 0.702. Second best."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Gradient Boosting: Test MAE 3.15, Test R&sup2; 0.487", "d": "Train MAE 3.234 and Train R&sup2; 0.564. Weakest; it struggled with noise from safety cars and weather changes."}]]}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does a near perfect training score carry over to the test set?", "d": "Extra Trees scored a Train R&sup2; of 1.000.", "yes": "It would be the strongest model.", "no": "Its test scores were worse than Random Forest's, the classic memorization signature."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Random Forest selected: Test MAE 2.78 laps", "d": "On average its predicted first pit lap is roughly three laps from what teams did, with a Test R&sup2; of 0.591."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "CatBoost lost its categorical edge", "d": "The pipeline pre encoded categorical variables for a fair comparison, which removed the advantage CatBoost would normally have."}, {"k": "step", "t": "SHAP analysis on the Random Forest", "d": "Measures the direction and size of each feature's contribution to the predicted pit lap."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Race structure features dominate", "d": "Top five: total laps, planned pit stop count, year (proxying for regulation changes), round, and starting grid position. Driver and constructor identities mattered considerably less than F1 commentary tradition suggests."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Streamlit app, <code>team_omega_rf_app.py</code>", "d": "Engineers enter circuit, grid position, planned stops and weather; the serialized model returns a predicted first pit lap in real time with the SHAP importances behind it."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "No live race signals", "d": "Safety car deployments, sudden weather changes and real time tire telemetry are absent, though they drive real pit decisions."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "First stop only", "d": "Later stops depend on the first, and the model does not capture that dependency or full multi stop strategies."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "No competitor model", "d": "Real strategy reacts to undercuts and overcuts. The model encodes neither opponent positions nor gap sizes."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Regulation drift", "d": "Patterns from early 2000s races may not apply directly to current regulations, which slightly dilutes accuracy on contemporary strategy."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "Pit timing here is more a property of circuit, race length and planned stops than of the driver, so strategy adapts to circuit and race length first. The 2.78 lap MAE covers first stops only, without live race signals or opponents."}]}, {"title": "Managing July Energy Demand Under a Warming Climate", "meta": "Predictive Modeling &middot; Jan 2025 to May 2025", "badge": "+25.4% peak load at +5&deg;C &middot; 9.2% savings modeled", "tags": ["Random Forest", "XGBoost", "Linear Regression", "Python", "Climate Scenarios", "Residential Energy"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">Random Forest R&sup2; = 0.48 &nbsp;|&nbsp; RMSE = 0.500 kWh &nbsp;|&nbsp; +25.4% projected peak hour load at +5&deg;C &nbsp;|&nbsp; 9.2% savings from 1&deg;C setpoint shift</div>\n\n          <h4>The Problem</h4>\n          <p>\n            When July temperatures rise, residential air conditioning load swings hard upward, peak hour demand spikes, and grid operators face a choice: build more generation (slow, expensive, capital intensive) or shift demand through pricing and behavioral signals (fast, cheap, but only effective if you know what works). This project gives eSC the second tool. We forecast residential consumption under a sustained warming scenario, then quantify which interventions recover the most demand without infrastructure investment.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Stakeholders</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Utility capacity planners</strong> get a modeled peak hour load under a sustained warming scenario instead of extrapolating last summer.</li>\n            <li><strong>Demand response program managers</strong> get a savings estimate for the simplest intervention available (a 1&deg;C cooling setpoint shift), which they can use to justify smart thermostat rollouts.</li>\n            <li><strong>Regulators and ratepayer advocates</strong> get evidence on how much behavioral and efficiency programs can substitute for new generation capacity.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            Three datasets were joined on common keys: a household metadata file (square footage, occupants, wall insulation type, cooling setpoint), an hourly energy consumption file linked by <code>bldg_id</code>, and a weather file linked by <code>in.county</code>. A metadata file documented what each column means. The combined data was filtered to July to focus on peak cooling season, then merged into one record per house per hour. A reusable merge function lets the same pipeline run on other months and seasons.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>What Drives Residential Consumption</h4>\n          <p>\n            EDA established four primary drivers of July energy usage:\n          </p>\n\n          <div class=\"figure-row\">\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/energy_12.png\" alt=\"Energy usage vs dry bulb temperature\">\n              <figcaption>Figure 1. Energy usage increases with outdoor dry bulb temperature</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/energy_09.png\" alt=\"Energy usage vs square footage\">\n              <figcaption>Figure 2. Energy usage scales near linearly with household square footage</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n          </div>\n\n          <div class=\"figure-row\">\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/energy_11.png\" alt=\"Energy usage by number of occupants\">\n              <figcaption>Figure 3. Energy usage by number of occupants</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/energy_10.png\" alt=\"Energy usage by wall insulation type\">\n              <figcaption>Figure 4. Energy usage by wall insulation type</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n          </div>\n\n          <p>\n            Outdoor temperature is the dominant driver, and consumption scales near linearly with square footage. Occupant count matters but plateaus quickly. Usage varies only modestly across wall insulation types in the housing stock, which suggests envelope upgrades have lower marginal return than HVAC and setpoint interventions on this dataset.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Models Compared</h4>\n          <p>\n            Three models were trained on five features (dry bulb temperature, square footage, occupants, cooling setpoint, hour of day):\n          </p>\n\n          <table>\n            <thead>\n              <tr><th>Model</th><th>RMSE (kWh)</th><th>R&sup2;</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Linear Regression</td><td>0.583</td><td>0.29</td><td>Cannot capture non linear temperature response</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>XGBoost</td><td>0.503</td><td>0.47</td><td>Strong on non linear patterns, close second</td></tr>\n              <tr class=\"winner\"><td><strong>Random Forest</strong></td><td><strong>0.500</strong></td><td><strong>0.48</strong></td><td>Selected for forecasting</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n\n          <h4>The +5&deg;C Stress Test</h4>\n          <p>\n            Sustained warming scenarios were applied directly to the temperature feature and pushed through the trained model:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Average July hour, baseline</strong>: 1.09 kWh predicted.</li>\n            <li><strong>Average July hour, +5&deg;C</strong>: 1.25 kWh predicted (a 14.7% increase).</li>\n            <li><strong>Peak hour (9 PM) baseline</strong>: 81,600 kWh aggregate across the sample.</li>\n            <li><strong>Peak hour (9 PM) at +5&deg;C</strong>: 102,355 kWh aggregate, a 25.4% surge.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Policy Lever: 1&deg;C Setpoint Adjustment</h4>\n            <p>\n              Layering a 1&deg;C cooling setpoint increase during peak hours (16:00 to 21:00) on top of the +5&deg;C warming scenario produces:\n            </p>\n            <ul>\n              <li>Average usage at +5&deg;C: 1.30 kWh per hour</li>\n              <li>Average usage with +1&deg;C setpoint adjustment: 1.18 kWh per hour</li>\n              <li><strong>Average savings: 0.12 kWh per hour, or 9.2% of peak hour demand</strong></li>\n            </ul>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              A single degree of thermostat tolerance, applied across a customer base, cuts modeled peak hour demand in the warming scenario by nearly one tenth, with no infrastructure investment.\n            </p>\n          </div>\n\n          <h4>Recommendations to eSC</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Time of use pricing during peak hours.</strong> Shift discretionary demand off peak through price signals rather than capacity expansion.</li>\n            <li><strong>Smart thermostat rollout.</strong> Thermostats can apply the 1&deg;C setpoint adjustment automatically across the customer base instead of relying on individual behavior change.</li>\n            <li><strong>Modern HVAC incentives.</strong> Older units carry disproportionate cooling load; replacement programs targeted at the worst performers compound the setpoint benefit.</li>\n            <li><strong>Passive cooling design for new builds.</strong> A long term lever that compounds over the multi decade life of the housing stock.</li>\n          </ul>", "simple": "<h4>The Problem</h4>\n<p>When July gets hotter, home air conditioning use climbs and the busiest hour of the day gets busier. Grid operators can build more power generation, which is slow and expensive, or shift demand through pricing and behavior, which is fast and cheap but only works if you know what works. Our project gave eSC evidence for the second option: a forecast of home energy use under sustained warming, and which changes win back the most demand without new infrastructure.</p>\n<h4>What We Did</h4>\n<p>We joined three datasets: details about each house (floor area, number of people, wall insulation, thermostat setting), hourly energy use for each house, and local weather. We kept July, the peak cooling month, and built one record per house per hour.</p>\n<p>Outdoor temperature was the biggest driver of energy use, and use rose almost in step with a home&rsquo;s floor area. The number of people mattered but leveled off quickly, and wall insulation made only a modest difference.</p>\n<p>We trained three models to predict a house&rsquo;s hourly use from five inputs: temperature, floor area, number of people, thermostat setting and hour of day. A Random Forest did best, narrowly ahead of XGBoost and well ahead of a straight line fit. Its R&sup2;, a measure of how much of the hour to hour variation in use it explains, was 0.48.</p>\n<h4>What We Found</h4>\n<p>We raised the temperature fed into the model by 5&deg;C and ran it again. The model predicted that the average July hour would use 14.7% more energy, and that total use across the sample of houses at the busiest hour, 9 PM, would rise by 25.4%.</p>\n<p>On top of that hotter July, we tested one simple change: setting thermostats 1&deg;C warmer during the peak hours of 16:00 to 21:00. In the model, that cut average use in those hours by 9.2%, with no new infrastructure.</p>\n<p>We recommended pricing that is higher at peak hours, smart thermostats that make the 1&deg;C change automatically instead of relying on each household, incentives to replace older cooling units, and passive cooling design in new homes.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The hotter July is a scenario, not an observation.</strong> We changed only the temperature fed into the model, so the 25.4% rise is a prediction from a model with an R&sup2; of 0.48, not a measurement.</li>\n<li><strong>The 9.2% saving is modeled too.</strong> It assumes the 1&deg;C change applies across the whole customer base.</li>\n<li><strong>The scope is narrow.</strong> The model uses five inputs and covers July only.</li>\n<li><strong>The insulation finding is local.</strong> Insulation&rsquo;s small effect holds for the houses in this dataset.</li>\n</ul>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "How much would a sustained +5&deg;C July raise residential demand?", "d": "And how much would a 1&deg;C cooling setpoint shift recover, without infrastructure investment? The aim was to give eSC evidence for shifting demand instead of building generation."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Three datasets joined on common keys", "lanes": [[{"k": "data", "t": "Household metadata", "d": "Square footage, occupants, wall insulation type and cooling setpoint, with a metadata file documenting each column."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "Hourly energy consumption", "d": "Linked to the households by <code>bldg_id</code>."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "Weather", "d": "Linked by <code>in.county</code>."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Filter to July and merge to one record per house per hour", "d": "July is peak cooling season. A reusable merge function lets the same pipeline run on other months and seasons."}, {"k": "result", "t": "EDA: four primary drivers of July usage", "d": "Outdoor dry bulb temperature dominates, usage scales near linearly with square footage, occupant count plateaus quickly, and wall insulation type varies usage only modestly in this housing stock."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Three models on five features", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "Linear Regression: RMSE 0.583 kWh, R&sup2; 0.29", "d": "Cannot capture the non linear temperature response."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "XGBoost: RMSE 0.503 kWh, R&sup2; 0.47", "d": "Strong on non linear patterns, a close second."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Random Forest: RMSE 0.500 kWh, R&sup2; 0.48", "d": "Best on both measures."}]]}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does a tree model beat Linear Regression on RMSE and R&sup2;?", "d": "Features: dry bulb temperature, square footage, occupants, cooling setpoint and hour of day.", "yes": "Both do. Random Forest, narrowly ahead of XGBoost, is selected for forecasting.", "no": "A linear model would be enough to forecast demand."}, {"k": "step", "t": "+5&deg;C stress test", "d": "Sustained warming is applied directly to the temperature feature and pushed through the trained Random Forest."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Average July hour: 1.09 to 1.25 kWh, a 14.7% increase", "d": "Modeled by the Random Forest under the +5&deg;C scenario, not measured."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Peak hour (9 PM): 81,600 to 102,355 kWh, a 25.4% surge", "d": "Aggregate across the sample, modeled under the +5&deg;C scenario."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Layer a 1&deg;C setpoint increase on the +5&deg;C scenario", "d": "Applied during peak hours, 16:00 to 21:00, across the customer base."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Modeled savings: 0.12 kWh per hour, 9.2% of peak hour demand", "d": "Average peak hour usage falls from 1.30 to 1.18 kWh per hour in the warming scenario, with no infrastructure investment."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Recommendations to eSC", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Time of use pricing", "d": "Shift discretionary demand off peak through price signals rather than capacity expansion."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Smart thermostat rollout", "d": "Apply the 1&deg;C setpoint adjustment automatically instead of relying on individual behavior change."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Modern HVAC incentives", "d": "Older units carry disproportionate cooling load; replacing the worst performers compounds the setpoint benefit."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Passive cooling for new builds", "d": "A long term lever over the multi decade life of the housing stock."}]]}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Scenario results are model outputs", "d": "Warming is applied only through the temperature feature of a model with R&sup2; 0.48 and RMSE 0.500 kWh, so the 14.7%, 25.4% and 9.2% figures are modeled, not observed."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Five features, July only", "d": "The model sees dry bulb temperature, square footage, occupants, cooling setpoint and hour of day, on July data."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Insulation conclusion holds on this dataset", "d": "Modest variation across wall insulation types suggests envelope upgrades have lower marginal return than HVAC and setpoint interventions on this dataset."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "Under the model, a +5&deg;C July lifts the 9 PM peak by 25.4%, and a 1&deg;C peak hour setpoint shift recovers 9.2%. Both are scenario outputs from a model with R&sup2; 0.48."}]}, {"title": "Challenging \"Wealth Equals Health\": Education as the Hidden Lever", "meta": "Quantitative Data Storytelling &middot; Jan 2025 to May 2025", "badge": "180+ countries &middot; ANOVA F = 426.5 (p &lt; 2e-16)", "tags": ["R", "ggplot2", "Multiple Regression", "ANOVA", "Bayesian Inference", "Gapminder"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">180+ countries &nbsp;|&nbsp; Period: 2000 to 2020 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Combined BF = 1.65 &times; 10&#179;&#x2078;&#x2078; &nbsp;|&nbsp; ANOVA F = 426.5 (p &lt; 2e-16)</div>\n\n          <h4>The Problem</h4>\n          <p>\n            \"Richer countries are healthier\" is one of the most common framings in development work, and it has a real consequence for where money goes. Development funding tends to flow toward GDP growth as the master strategy, with health and education treated as downstream beneficiaries. The explicit goal here was evidence a community leader can use to argue for education investment in rural communities. The final report is written for an intentionally non technical audience: adults, teenagers, and children who can act on the conclusion.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Research Questions</h4>\n          <ol>\n            <li>To what extent does income influence life expectancy across countries?</li>\n            <li>Can education serve as a more effective tool than income in reducing child mortality?</li>\n          </ol>\n\n          <h4>Data</h4>\n          <p>\n            The data is the Gapminder global dataset, covering 180+ countries between 2000 and 2020, with these variables:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>GDP per capita</strong> as the measure of national income, log transformed to address right skew from extreme high income outliers.</li>\n            <li><strong>Life expectancy</strong> as the primary health outcome, ranging from roughly 40 to 85 years.</li>\n            <li><strong>Child mortality</strong> as the most policy responsive health metric, ranging from fewer than 5 deaths per 1,000 births in high income countries to over 150 in low income countries.</li>\n            <li><strong>Mean years of schooling</strong> for adults aged 25 to 34, the most reliable cross country proxy for education access, ranging from 2 to 15 years.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Exploratory Visualizations</h4>\n\n          <div class=\"figure-row\">\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_02.png\" alt=\"GDP vs Life Expectancy scatter plot with regression line\">\n              <figcaption>Figure 1. Log GDP vs life expectancy, all countries 2000 to 2020</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n            <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n              <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_04.png\" alt=\"GDP vs Child Mortality scatter plot with regression line\">\n              <figcaption>Figure 2. Log GDP vs child mortality, all countries 2000 to 2020</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n          </div>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_03.png\" alt=\"Education vs Child Mortality scatter plot showing strong negative relationship\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 3. Mean years of schooling vs child mortality. The negative relationship is stronger and tighter than for GDP</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Question 1: Does GDP Predict Life Expectancy?</h4>\n          <p>\n            Linear regression of life expectancy on log GDP per capita gave a coefficient of <strong>17.6</strong> (p &lt; 2e-16), a strong positive relationship. Bayesian regression returned a Bayes Factor of <strong>5.66 &times; 10&#178;&#185;&#x2078;</strong> in favor of GDP as a predictor, which is overwhelming evidence. The answer to the first question is yes, GDP matters.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Question 2: Does Education Beat GDP for Child Mortality?</h4>\n          <p>\n            Multiple regression of child mortality on both mean years of schooling and log GDP returned two significant negative coefficients:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Mean years of schooling</strong>: estimate = -0.76, p &lt; 2e-16</li>\n            <li><strong>Log GDP per capita</strong>: estimate = -74.14, p &lt; 2e-16</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p>\n            Bayesian model comparison returned a combined Bayes Factor of <strong>1.65 &times; 10&#178;&#x2078;&#x2078;</strong> in favor of the model that includes both predictors. That is overwhelming evidence that each predictor carries independent information about child mortality.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Confirmatory Tests</h4>\n          <p>\n            Welch's t test compared child mortality between high education and low education country groups:\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_09.png\" alt=\"Boxplot of child mortality by education group\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 4. Child mortality by education group (Welch's t test). Mean difference &#8776; 57 deaths per 1,000 births, p &lt; 2.2e-16</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <p>\n            One way ANOVA on life expectancy by GDP group (Low, Medium, High) returned F = 426.5, p &lt; 2e-16:\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/wealth_10.png\" alt=\"Boxplot of life expectancy by GDP group\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 5. Life expectancy by GDP group (ANOVA, F = 426.5, p &lt; 2e-16)</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>The Practical Conclusion</h4>\n            <p>\n              Income matters, but it is not the lever development funders should pull first. Education is just as strong a predictor of child mortality as GDP is, and unlike GDP, a rural community can act on it directly through school access, teacher training, and maternal literacy programs.\n            </p>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              Each additional year of schooling is associated with a measurable drop in child deaths, regardless of country wealth. For a community leader, the evidence based course is to keep children (especially girls) in school through secondary education, combine schooling with practical instruction on hygiene, nutrition, vaccination, and early childhood care, and advocate for school infrastructure investment.\n            </p>\n          </div>", "simple": "<h4>The Question</h4>\n<p>A common idea in development work is that richer countries are healthier, so funding tends to go toward growing the economy, with health and education expected to follow. I tested that idea on data from more than 180 countries between 2000 and 2020, with two questions. Does a country&rsquo;s income predict how long its people live? And can education do more than income to reduce how many young children die?</p>\n<p>The goal was evidence a community leader could use to argue for education investment in rural communities, so the final report was written for a general audience, including teenagers and children.</p>\n<h4>What I Used</h4>\n<p>The data is the public Gapminder dataset. For each country it has income per person, life expectancy, child deaths per 1,000 births, and the average years of schooling of young adults, which stands in for access to education.</p>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<p><strong>Income does predict life expectancy.</strong> Countries with more income per person live longer. A Bayes factor, which weighs how much better the data fit when income is included as a predictor, came out overwhelmingly in favor of income. Grouping countries into low, medium and high income showed clear differences in life expectancy between the groups.</p>\n<p><strong>Schooling adds something income does not.</strong> When I put schooling and income into the same model of child deaths, both were linked to fewer deaths, and the evidence overwhelmingly favored the model that includes both. That means each one carries information about child deaths that the other does not. Countries in the high education group had on average about 57 fewer child deaths per 1,000 births than countries in the low education group.</p>\n<p>The report&rsquo;s practical conclusion is that income matters, but it is not the lever funders should pull first. Education predicts child deaths as strongly as income does, and a rural community can act on it directly through school access, teacher training and literacy programs for mothers. For a community leader, that means keeping children, especially girls, in school through secondary education, pairing school with practical lessons on hygiene, nutrition, vaccination and early childhood care, and pushing for investment in school buildings and facilities.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<p>The analysis compares countries, and its findings are associations, in the report&rsquo;s own word: where young adults have more schooling, fewer children die, whatever the country&rsquo;s wealth. Education is measured by a single figure, the average years of schooling for adults aged 25 to 34, which the report calls the most reliable cross country stand in for education access.</p>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Does income predict health, and can education beat income on child mortality?", "d": "Development funding tends to treat GDP growth as the master strategy. The goal was evidence a community leader can use to argue for education investment in rural communities."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Gapminder global dataset, 180+ countries, 2000 to 2020", "d": "Country level series for income, health and education."}, {"k": "split", "t": "The four variables", "lanes": [[{"k": "data", "t": "GDP per capita", "d": "The income measure, log transformed to address right skew from extreme high income outliers."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "Life expectancy", "d": "The primary health outcome, from roughly 40 to 85 years."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "Child mortality", "d": "The most policy responsive health metric, from fewer than 5 deaths per 1,000 births in high income countries to over 150 in low income ones."}], [{"k": "data", "t": "Mean years of schooling, adults aged 25 to 34", "d": "The most reliable cross country proxy for education access, from 2 to 15 years."}]]}, {"k": "result", "t": "Exploratory plots: schooling tracks child mortality more tightly than GDP", "d": "Scatter plots of log GDP against life expectancy and child mortality, and of schooling against child mortality. The schooling relationship is negative, stronger and tighter than the GDP one."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Question 1: regress life expectancy on log GDP per capita", "d": "Linear regression across all countries, 2000 to 2020."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Coefficient 17.6 (p &lt; 2e-16)", "d": "A strong positive relationship between log GDP per capita and life expectancy."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does Bayesian regression favor GDP as a predictor of life expectancy?", "yes": "Bayes Factor 5.66 &times; 10&#178;&#185;&#x2078; in favor of GDP, overwhelming evidence. The answer to question 1 is yes, GDP matters.", "no": "GDP would not be supported as a predictor."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does life expectancy differ across Low, Medium and High GDP groups?", "d": "One way ANOVA on life expectancy by GDP group.", "yes": "F = 426.5, p &lt; 2e-16.", "no": "GDP group would not separate life expectancy."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Question 2: regress child mortality on schooling and log GDP together", "d": "Multiple regression with both predictors in one model."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Both coefficients negative and significant", "d": "Mean years of schooling: estimate &minus;0.76, p &lt; 2e-16. Log GDP per capita: estimate &minus;74.14, p &lt; 2e-16."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does Bayesian model comparison favor the model with both predictors?", "yes": "Combined Bayes Factor 1.65 &times; 10&#178;&#x2078;&#x2078;, overwhelming evidence that each predictor carries independent information about child mortality.", "no": "One predictor would be enough on its own."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does child mortality differ between high and low education countries?", "d": "Welch's t test on the two education groups.", "yes": "Mean difference of about 57 deaths per 1,000 births, p &lt; 2.2e-16.", "no": "Education group would not separate child mortality."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Practical conclusion: income matters, but is not the first lever", "d": "Education is as strong a predictor of child mortality as GDP, and a rural community can act on it directly through school access, teacher training and maternal literacy programs."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Recommendations for a community leader", "d": "Keep children, especially girls, in school through secondary education; pair schooling with instruction on hygiene, nutrition, vaccination and early childhood care; advocate for school infrastructure investment."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Findings are cross country associations", "d": "The report states each extra year of schooling is associated with a measurable drop in child deaths, regardless of country wealth. The tests compare countries in the Gapminder data."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Education is measured by one proxy", "d": "Mean years of schooling for adults aged 25 to 34 stands in for education access across countries."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "Income predicts life expectancy, and schooling carries information about child mortality that income does not. The evidence is associations across 180+ countries, measured with a single education proxy, written up for a non technical audience."}]}, {"title": "Uber Data Engineering Pipeline", "meta": "Data Engineering &middot; Jul 2024 to Dec 2024", "badge": "End-to-end GCP pipeline &middot; star schema &middot; BigQuery", "tags": ["GCP", "BigQuery", "Power BI", "SQL", "Python", "ETL", "Star Schema"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">End to end cloud pipeline &nbsp;|&nbsp; Star schema modeling &nbsp;|&nbsp; Cloud Storage + Compute Engine + BigQuery &nbsp;|&nbsp; Interactive BI dashboard</div>\n\n          <h4>Objective</h4>\n          <p>\n            I wanted the full lifecycle in one project, end to end on a real cloud stack: ingestion, storage, transformation, modeling, querying, and visualization. The deliverable was the entire path from a raw data file to a stakeholder ready dashboard.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Architecture</h4>\n          <p>\n            I modeled the raw NYC taxi trip data as a star schema in Lucid Charts, with fact and dimension tables and explicit primary and foreign key relationships. The infrastructure was deployed on Google Cloud Platform in three layers:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Storage layer.</strong> Cloud Storage as the data lake for raw and intermediate Parquet files.</li>\n            <li><strong>Compute layer.</strong> Compute Engine VMs running Python ETL jobs that read from Cloud Storage, transform with Pandas and NumPy, and write back as cleaned Parquet.</li>\n            <li><strong>Analytics layer.</strong> BigQuery for SQL analytics at scale, with the cleaned data loaded as partitioned tables for efficient time range queries.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Analysis and Presentation</h4>\n          <p>\n            Exploratory analysis in Jupyter surfaced patterns in trip distance distribution, fare structure, revenue concentration, and service usage across boroughs and times of day. The findings then fed an interactive Power BI dashboard with geographical heat maps, time series views, and slicers for stakeholder driven exploration.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Lesson</h4>\n          <p>\n            Moving from local CSV files to distributed cloud storage and compute changed how I think about data work more than any individual tool did. When data lives in object storage and compute is elastic, scaling up by an order of magnitude becomes a configuration change rather than a bottleneck.\n          </p>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>I wanted the whole path that data takes, in one project, on a real cloud setup: from a raw file of New York City taxi trips to a dashboard that someone can explore without writing code. Along the way the data has to be brought in, stored, cleaned, organised, queried, and shown.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<p>First I designed how the data would be organised, as a star schema. That puts the core trip records in one central table and the descriptive details in smaller tables around it, linked by ID keys, so questions about the trips can be answered by joining them up.</p>\n<p>The system runs on Google Cloud in three layers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Storage.</strong> The raw files, and the partly processed ones, sit in Cloud Storage, Google&rsquo;s file store.</li>\n<li><strong>Processing.</strong> Python programs on rented cloud machines read those files, clean them, and write the cleaned versions back.</li>\n<li><strong>Analysis.</strong> The cleaned data is loaded into BigQuery, Google&rsquo;s database for large SQL queries, split up by time so that queries over a date range run efficiently.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<p>Exploring the data turned up patterns in how far trips go, how fares are structured, where the revenue concentrates, and how use of the service varies across the city&rsquo;s boroughs and across the day. Those findings went into an interactive Power BI dashboard with heat maps of the city, trends over time, and filters so a viewer can explore on their own.</p>\n<p>The move from files on my own computer to cloud storage and computing changed how I think about data work more than any single tool did. When the data lives in cloud storage and the computing power can grow on demand, handling ten times more data becomes a settings change instead of a bottleneck.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<p>This project reports no measured speeds, costs, or data sizes. The point about handling ten times more data is a lesson from building the system, not a benchmark I ran.</p>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can one project cover the full data lifecycle on a real cloud stack?", "d": "The deliverable is the whole path from a raw data file to a stakeholder ready dashboard: ingestion, storage, transformation, modeling, querying, and visualization."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Raw NYC taxi trip data", "d": "The pipeline starts from a raw data file of New York City taxi trips."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Star schema designed in Lucid Charts", "d": "Fact and dimension tables with explicit primary and foreign key relationships, so trips sit in one central table linked to the details around them."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Deployed on Google Cloud Platform in three layers", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Storage: Cloud Storage as the data lake", "d": "Holds the raw and intermediate Parquet files."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Compute: Python ETL on Compute Engine VMs", "d": "Jobs read Parquet from Cloud Storage."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Transform with Pandas and NumPy", "d": "The cleaned data is written back to Cloud Storage as Parquet."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Analytics: load into BigQuery", "d": "The cleaned data becomes partitioned tables for efficient time range queries."}, {"k": "step", "t": "SQL analytics at scale", "d": "Queries run in BigQuery over the partitioned tables."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Exploratory analysis in Jupyter", "d": "The cleaned data is explored for patterns before anything goes on a dashboard."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Patterns in distance, fares, revenue, and usage", "d": "Trip distance distribution, fare structure, revenue concentration, and service usage across boroughs and times of day."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Interactive Power BI dashboard", "d": "Geographical heat maps, time series views, and slicers for stakeholder driven exploration."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Lesson: storage and compute in the cloud change the scaling problem", "d": "With data in object storage and elastic compute, scaling up by an order of magnitude becomes a configuration change rather than a bottleneck."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "No measured performance figures", "d": "The project reports no timings, costs, or data volumes, so the scaling lesson comes from building the system, not from a benchmark."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "This is a complete cloud pipeline from raw taxi data to a dashboard, built on a star schema and three GCP layers. It shows the architecture, not measured throughput or cost."}]}, {"title": "Travel Itinerary Management System", "meta": "Database Engineering &middot; Jan 2025 to May 2025", "badge": "12-entity normalized schema &middot; full itinerary in one call", "tags": ["SQL", "Data Modeling", "Stored Procedures", "Triggers", "User Defined Functions", "ERD Design", "3NF"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">12 normalized entities &nbsp;|&nbsp; 3 stored procedures &nbsp;|&nbsp; 2 triggers &nbsp;|&nbsp; 1 user defined function &nbsp;|&nbsp; Single procedure call generates full itinerary</div>\n\n          <h4>The Problem</h4>\n          <p>\n            An itinerary database carries rules that have to hold on every access path: an activity cannot have negative cost, a hotel must belong to a real destination, an itinerary cost should always equal the sum of its components. Put those rules in application code and each new feature or platform reintroduces them, badly. This project puts them in the database itself, so any client (web, mobile, batch import, ad hoc SQL) gets the same guarantees automatically.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Stakeholders</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Travelers</strong> creating a trip get one call that returns a complete itinerary, with the duration and cost checks already run.</li>\n            <li><strong>Application developers</strong> stop writing defensive code in three languages to enforce rules the database already enforces.</li>\n            <li><strong>Data analysts</strong> querying the database directly get consistent data, because the triggers and foreign keys also apply to rows written through direct SQL.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Entity Relationship Design</h4>\n          <p>\n            The schema has twelve entities in third normal form with full referential integrity. The core entity is <code>itinerary</code>, which links to destinations, activities, accommodations, transportation, seasonal information, and tags through explicit foreign keys.\n          </p>\n\n          <figure class=\"project-figure\">\n            <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"images/travel_03.png\" alt=\"Logical ERD showing all 12 tables with primary keys, foreign keys, and relationships\">\n            <figcaption>Figure 1. Logical ERD with all 12 tables, primary keys, foreign keys, and cardinality relationships</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n\n          <h4>Stored Procedures</h4>\n          <p>\n            Three procedures cover the operations a travel app performs:\n          </p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><code>sp_add_full_itinerary_single</code>: Inserts a complete itinerary (activity, transport, accommodation, season info, tag) in a single atomic call. It replaces five separate inserts plus consistency checks at the application layer.</li>\n            <li><code>sp_get_full_itinerary</code>: Returns every linked detail of an itinerary by ID, with the joins done inside the database.</li>\n            <li><code>sp_get_itineraries_for_destination</code>: Lists all itineraries available under a selected destination, the most common browse query.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Triggers for Data Integrity</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><code>trg_check_activity_duration</code>: Rejects any activity insert or update where duration is zero or negative. That catches UI bugs and data import errors that would otherwise silently corrupt the cost calculations downstream.</li>\n            <li><code>trg_check_activity_cost</code>: Rejects negative cost entries, which usually indicate a typo or unit conversion error rather than a real refund.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>Function for Reusable Logic</h4>\n          <ul>\n            <li><code>ufn_estimate_total_activity_time</code>: Returns the total time a traveler will spend on all activities in a given itinerary. Views, filters, and ranking interfaces call it, so itineraries can be sorted or filtered by total activity duration without each of them redoing the math.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h4>User Story Worked Through End to End</h4>\n          <p>\n            <em>\"As a traveler, I want to create a trip by selecting options, so I can generate a full itinerary.\"</em>\n          </p>\n          <p>\n            The client passes destination, activity, transport, accommodation, season, and tag to <code>sp_add_full_itinerary_single</code>. The procedure inserts each child record, links them through the itinerary table, fires the duration and cost triggers, and returns the new <code>itinerary_id</code>. The client makes one round trip, and the database validates the whole insert and applies it atomically.\n          </p>\n\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <h4>Design Philosophy</h4>\n            <p style=\"margin-bottom: 0;\">\n              With the business rules at the data layer, the application code gets simpler and integrity holds on every read and write path. When the database itself rejects a zero duration or a negative cost, no client has to write defensive code in its own language to catch it.\n            </p>\n          </div>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>A database for planning trips. A trip plan carries rules that must always hold: an activity cannot have a negative cost, a hotel must belong to a real destination, and a trip&rsquo;s total cost should equal the sum of its parts. If those rules live in each app that uses the data, every new feature or platform has to rebuild them, and does it badly. I put the rules inside the database itself, so a website, a phone app, a bulk upload, or someone typing queries by hand all get the same checks.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<p>The data is split into 12 linked tables, arranged so each fact is stored in one place (a standard design called third normal form). At the centre is the itinerary, which links to destinations, activities, places to stay, transport, seasonal information, and tags.</p>\n<p>A few pieces of logic run inside the database:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Three stored procedures</strong>, saved routines an app can call by name, handle the common jobs: create a whole itinerary in one step, fetch every detail of one itinerary, and list the itineraries for a destination.</li>\n<li><strong>Two triggers</strong>, checks that run automatically whenever data is written, reject any activity that lasts zero time or less, and any negative cost. A negative cost usually means a typo or a unit conversion mistake, not a real refund.</li>\n<li><strong>One function</strong> adds up how long a traveller will spend on all the activities in an itinerary, so screens can sort or filter by it without each one redoing the math.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>A Trip, Start to Finish</h4>\n<p>A traveller picks a destination, an activity, transport, a place to stay, a season, and a tag. The app sends all of it to the database in one call. The database saves each piece, links it to the new itinerary, runs the duration and cost checks, and sends back the new itinerary&rsquo;s ID. The database checks the whole request and saves it as one unit, so a trip is never left half saved.</p>\n<p>That one call replaces five separate saves plus the checks the app would otherwise have to run itself. Because the checks live in the database, they also apply to anyone writing data directly, outside the app.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<p>This project covers the design and one worked example. It reports no measured speeds and no test results.</p>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can an itinerary database enforce its business rules on every access path?", "d": "Rules such as no negative cost, a hotel tied to a real destination, and an itinerary cost equal to the sum of its parts go in the database, so web, mobile, batch import, and ad hoc SQL clients get the same guarantees."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Twelve entity schema in third normal form", "d": "Full referential integrity. The core <code>itinerary</code> entity links to destinations, activities, accommodations, transportation, seasonal information, and tags through explicit foreign keys, drawn as a logical ERD."}, {"k": "data", "t": "A traveler&rsquo;s choices", "d": "User story: create a trip by selecting options. The client passes destination, activity, transport, accommodation, season, and tag."}, {"k": "step", "t": "<code>sp_add_full_itinerary_single</code>", "d": "One atomic call inserts each child record and links them through the itinerary table."}, {"k": "check", "t": "<code>trg_check_activity_duration</code>: is the duration zero or negative?", "d": "Runs on every activity insert or update.", "yes": "The write is rejected, catching UI bugs and import errors that would otherwise silently corrupt cost calculations downstream.", "no": "The activity is accepted."}, {"k": "check", "t": "<code>trg_check_activity_cost</code>: is the cost negative?", "d": "Runs on every activity cost entry.", "yes": "The entry is rejected. A negative cost usually indicates a typo or unit conversion error rather than a real refund.", "no": "The cost is accepted."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Does the whole insert validate?", "d": "The database validates the whole insert and applies it atomically.", "yes": "The procedure returns the new <code>itinerary_id</code> after one client round trip.", "no": "The insert is not applied, so no partial itinerary is left behind."}, {"k": "result", "t": "One call replaces five inserts", "d": "The single procedure replaces five separate inserts plus consistency checks at the application layer."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Reading the data back", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "<code>sp_get_full_itinerary</code>", "d": "Returns every linked detail of an itinerary by ID, with the joins done inside the database."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "<code>sp_get_itineraries_for_destination</code>", "d": "Lists all itineraries under a selected destination, the most common browse query."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "<code>ufn_estimate_total_activity_time</code>", "d": "Returns total activity time for an itinerary, so views, filters, and ranking interfaces sort by it without redoing the math."}]]}, {"k": "split", "t": "Who gets the guarantees", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Travelers", "d": "One call returns a complete itinerary, with the duration and cost checks already run."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Application developers", "d": "They stop writing defensive code in three languages to enforce rules the database already enforces."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "Data analysts", "d": "Triggers and foreign keys also apply to rows written through direct SQL, so the data stays consistent."}]]}, {"k": "result", "t": "Integrity on every read and write path", "d": "With the business rules at the data layer, application code gets simpler, and no client has to catch a zero duration or negative cost in its own language."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Design and one worked example", "d": "The project reports no measured performance and no test results, so it shows the design, not measured behavior."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "The project shows business rules enforced inside the database through 12 normalized entities, 3 stored procedures, 2 triggers, and 1 function, walked through one user story end to end."}]}, {"title": "Digit Recognition Neural Network from Scratch", "meta": "Deep Learning &middot; Jan 2024 to May 2024", "badge": "94% MNIST accuracy &middot; built from scratch, no frameworks", "tags": ["Python", "NumPy", "Neural Networks", "Backpropagation", "From Scratch"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">94% test accuracy on MNIST &nbsp;|&nbsp; No ML frameworks &nbsp;|&nbsp; Manual forward and backward passes</div>\n\n          <h4>Why Build from Scratch</h4>\n          <p>\n            Frameworks hide the parts of a neural network that are most useful to understand: the matrix shapes flowing forward, the gradient math flowing backward, and the way optimization changes the weights. Building all three by hand once gives you a working mental model of what the framework is doing underneath. When a production model later silently fails, you reason about gradient flow rather than swapping layers and hoping.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Implementation</h4>\n          <p>\n            Forward propagation is explicit weighted sums and activation functions: sigmoid in the hidden layers in the first iteration, then ReLU once vanishing gradients became visible, and softmax at the output for the ten class classification. Backpropagation computes gradients with the chain rule, layer by layer, using an explicit derivative formula for each activation function. Training runs in a mini batch gradient descent loop with tunable learning rate, batch size, and network depth.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Tuning</h4>\n          <p>\n            Hyperparameter tuning was iterative and manual. With the learning rate too high the loss bounced, and with it too low, training stalled. A network that was too shallow could not capture digit structure, and in one that was too deep without proper initialization the gradients vanished. Working through these failures by hand is how you learn what each knob controls.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Result</h4>\n          <p>\n            Final accuracy was 94% on the held out MNIST test set. That is not state of the art; the goal was understanding rather than benchmark chasing.\n          </p>", "simple": "<h4>What I Built</h4>\n<p>A neural network that looks at an image of a handwritten digit and says which of the ten digits it is. I wrote it without any AI library, so every step a library normally does behind the scenes is written out by hand. The images come from MNIST, a standard collection of handwritten digits.</p>\n<h4>Why Build It by Hand</h4>\n<p>Libraries hide the parts of a neural network that are most useful to understand: how numbers flow forward through the network to make a guess, how the error flows backward to work out what to change, and how each change adjusts the network. Building all three once gives a working picture of what a library is doing underneath, which helps when a real model later fails without an obvious error.</p>\n<h4>How It Works</h4>\n<p>A neural network is a stack of layers made of simple units. Each unit multiplies its inputs by weights, adds them up, and passes the total through a simple function. The last layer turns its outputs into a score for each of the ten digits. To learn, the network compares its guess with the right answer and works backward, layer by layer, to find how much each weight contributed to the error. This step is called backpropagation. Then it nudges every weight a little in the direction that reduces the error, a small batch of images at a time.</p>\n<p>My first version used one kind of function in the hidden layers, called sigmoid. Once the corrections passed backward started shrinking to almost nothing before they reached the early layers, a problem called vanishing gradients, I switched to another, called ReLU.</p>\n<h4>Tuning by Hand</h4>\n<p>I tuned the settings by hand, one experiment at a time. When the learning rate, the size of each nudge, was too high, the error bounced around; when it was too low, training stalled. A network with too few layers could not capture the shapes of the digits, and one with too many layers and badly chosen starting weights stopped learning, because the corrections faded out on the way back.</p>\n<h4>What I Found</h4>\n<p>The finished network got 94% of the digits right on a held out test set, images it never saw during training. That is not state of the art; the goal was to understand how the pieces work rather than to chase a benchmark.</p>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Can a digit classifier be built with no ML framework at all?", "d": "Frameworks hide the matrix shapes flowing forward, the gradient math flowing backward, and the way optimization changes the weights. Building all three by hand once gives a working mental model of what a framework does underneath."}, {"k": "data", "t": "MNIST handwritten digits", "d": "A ten class classification task. Accuracy is measured on the held out test set."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Forward propagation", "d": "Explicit weighted sums and activation functions, computed layer by layer."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Do sigmoid hidden layers train well enough?", "d": "The first iteration used sigmoid in the hidden layers.", "yes": "Sigmoid would have stayed.", "no": "Vanishing gradients became visible, so the hidden layers switched to ReLU."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Softmax output layer", "d": "Turns the last layer's outputs into a score for each of the ten classes."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Backpropagation by the chain rule", "d": "Gradients are computed layer by layer, using an explicit derivative formula for each activation function."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Mini batch gradient descent", "d": "The training loop updates the weights one mini batch at a time, with tunable learning rate, batch size and network depth."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Manual hyperparameter tuning", "d": "Iterative and by hand, working through each failure to see what each knob controls."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Failures met while tuning", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "Learning rate too high: the loss bounced", "d": "With the learning rate set too high, the training loss bounced."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Learning rate too low: training stalled", "d": "With the learning rate set too low, training stalled."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Network too shallow: missed digit structure", "d": "Too few layers to capture the shapes of the digits."}, {"k": "result", "t": "Too deep without proper initialization: gradients vanished", "d": "The backward signal faded before reaching the early layers."}]]}, {"k": "result", "t": "94% accuracy on the held out MNIST test set", "d": "Final accuracy of the hand built network."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Not state of the art", "d": "The goal was understanding rather than benchmark chasing, and 94% is not a state of the art result on MNIST."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "A network with forward and backward passes written by hand reaches 94% on MNIST. The result shows the from scratch implementation learns; it is not a competitive benchmark."}]}, {"title": "Real Time Big Data Inflow: Communication and Computation Delay Analysis", "meta": "Research &middot; Jan 2024 to Dec 2024", "badge": "40 to 60% latency reduction &middot; 6-dimension comparison", "tags": ["IoT", "Edge Computing", "Fog Computing", "Cloud Computing", "Stream Architecture"], "body": "<div class=\"metric\">40 to 60% latency reduction potential &nbsp;|&nbsp; 12+ primary sources reviewed &nbsp;|&nbsp; Comparative analysis across six dimensions</div>\n\n          <h4>Argument</h4>\n          <p>\n            The implicit default in most enterprise architectures is to send everything to the cloud. For real time systems, that default is wrong. The hard design work is deciding which computations belong near the sensor (where latency is cheap but durability is fragile and storage is limited) and which belong in the data center (where storage is durable and compute is elastic but round trips cost real time).\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Approach</h4>\n          <p>\n            The paper reviews twelve primary sources on stream processing, edge deployment patterns, and latency optimization in distributed systems. Each source is summarized for its proposed architecture and headline findings. From the literature, I propose a tiered hybrid architecture that pushes time critical computation toward the edge and keeps cloud tiers for durability, batch analytics, and global state.\n          </p>\n\n          <h4>Comparative Analysis</h4>\n          <p>\n            The paper compares cloud only and tiered architectures across six dimensions: scalability, reliability, bandwidth usage, privacy and security, cost effectiveness, and end to end latency. The tiered architecture wins on latency (40 to 60% reduction for appropriately partitioned workloads), bandwidth (less raw data crosses the WAN), and privacy (sensitive data can be processed locally). It pays a small price in operational complexity and consistency guarantees.\n          </p>", "simple": "<h4>What I Wrote</h4>\n<p>A research paper on where to process data that streams in nonstop, such as readings from sensors. The usual default in large company systems is to send everything to the cloud, meaning distant data centers. For systems that have to respond in real time, the paper argues that default is wrong, because every round trip to a data center adds delay.</p>\n<h4>The Trade Off</h4>\n<p>Processing data close to where it is produced, near the sensor, keeps delay low, but storage there is limited and data kept there is easily lost. A cloud data center stores data safely and can add computing power on demand, but every round trip to it costs time. The hard design work is deciding which calculations belong in each place.</p>\n<h4>How I Approached It</h4>\n<p>I reviewed twelve primary studies on processing streaming data, running it close to its source (often called the edge), and cutting delay in systems spread across many machines. For each one I summarized the design it proposed and its main findings. From that reading I proposed a layered design: time critical work is pushed toward the edge, and the cloud keeps the jobs that need safe long term storage, large batch analysis, or one shared view of the whole system.</p>\n<h4>What the Comparison Found</h4>\n<p>The paper compares sending everything to the cloud with the layered design on six measures: how well each can grow, reliability, network use, privacy and security, cost, and total delay. The layered design comes out ahead on three. Delay falls by 40 to 60% for workloads that are split sensibly between the layers. Less raw data has to cross the long distance network. Sensitive data can be processed where it is collected instead of being sent away. In exchange, the layered design is somewhat harder to run, and it offers weaker guarantees that every part of the system sees the same data at the same moment.</p>\n<h4>What It Does Not Show</h4>\n<p>This is a review and a proposal, so the 40 to 60% is a potential reduction, not a measurement, and it holds only for workloads that suit being split this way. Reliability, growth and cost are among the six measures, but no winner is reported for them.</p>", "flow": [{"k": "start", "t": "Which real time computations belong at the edge, and which in the cloud?", "d": "Most enterprise architectures default to sending everything to the cloud. The paper argues that for real time systems this default is wrong."}, {"k": "data", "t": "Twelve primary sources", "d": "Published work on stream processing, edge deployment patterns, and latency optimization in distributed systems."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Summarize each source", "d": "Each source is summarized for its proposed architecture and headline findings."}, {"k": "split", "t": "The placement trade off", "lanes": [[{"k": "step", "t": "Near the sensor (edge)", "d": "Latency is cheap, but durability is fragile and storage is limited."}], [{"k": "step", "t": "In the data center (cloud)", "d": "Storage is durable and compute is elastic, but round trips cost real time."}]]}, {"k": "step", "t": "Proposed tiered hybrid architecture", "d": "Time critical computation is pushed toward the edge, and cloud tiers keep durability, batch analytics, and global state."}, {"k": "step", "t": "Compare cloud only against tiered on six dimensions", "d": "Scalability, reliability, bandwidth usage, privacy and security, cost effectiveness, and end to end latency."}, {"k": "split", "t": "Where the tiered architecture wins", "lanes": [[{"k": "result", "t": "Latency: 40 to 60% reduction", "d": "For appropriately partitioned workloads, according to the paper's comparison."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Bandwidth: less raw data crosses the WAN", "d": "Less raw data has to cross the wide area network."}], [{"k": "result", "t": "Privacy: sensitive data can be processed locally", "d": "It can be processed where it is collected."}]]}, {"k": "result", "t": "The price: operational complexity and consistency guarantees", "d": "The tiered architecture pays a small cost in how hard it is to operate and in the consistency it can guarantee."}, {"k": "check", "t": "Is the workload appropriately partitioned between edge and cloud?", "d": "The latency figure is conditional on how the work is split.", "yes": "The comparison puts the latency reduction at 40 to 60%.", "no": "The 40 to 60% figure is stated only for appropriately partitioned workloads, so it does not carry over."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "A review and a proposal, not a measured system", "d": "The 40 to 60% is stated as a potential reduction for appropriately partitioned workloads, not a measured result."}, {"k": "limit", "t": "Three dimensions with no reported outcome", "d": "Scalability, reliability and cost effectiveness are among the six dimensions, but no winner is reported for them."}, {"k": "end", "t": "How to read it", "d": "The paper argues from twelve sources that time critical work belongs near the edge, with a 40 to 60% latency cut for suitable workloads. It is an argument from published work, not a measurement."}]}];
